    "originalPrint": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nCell injury and cellular adaptations: Intracellular hyaline accumulation (Mallory bodies in alcoholic liver disease; Councilman bodies in viral hepatitis). Extracellular hyaline accumulation: Amyloidosis (demonstrates apple-green birefringence under polarized light with Congo Red staining). Irreversible cell death patterns: 1) Coagulative necrosis (architecture preserved, protein denaturation; classic for myocardial infarction and ischemia in all solid organs except brain), 2) Liquefactive necrosis (enzymatic digestion; brain infarcts and bacterial abscesses), 3) Caseous necrosis ('cheese-like', combination of coagulative/liquefactive; hallmark of Tuberculosis), 4) Fat necrosis (saponification; acute pancreatitis), 5) Fibrinoid necrosis (vasculitis), 6) Gangrenous necrosis (dry vs wet).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThis page covers essential clinical and scientific concepts of General Pathology: Hyaline Degeneration, Amyloidosis & Necrosis Patterns. Understanding these foundational principles allows rapid recognition of Board examination question stems and clinical diagnostic dilemmas.",
