    "originalPrint": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nFilamentous bacteria & caries microbiology: 1) Actinomyces: Gram-positive, anaerobic to microaerophilic, branching filamentous rods. Actinomyces israelii causes Cervicofacial Actinomycosis ('lumpy jaw': chronic granulomatous infection following dental extraction or trauma; multiple draining sinus tracts discharging yellow 'sulfur granules' composed of bacterial colonies). Actinomyces viscosus is heavily implicated in Root Surface Caries in elderly patients with gingival recession. 2) Lactobacillus: Gram-positive, facultatively anaerobic rod producing abundant lactic acid; secondary colonizer responsible for the deep progression of dental caries into dentin (acidophilic and aciduric).",
