    "summary": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nFilamentous bacteria & caries microbiology: 1) Actinomyces: Gram-positive, anaerobic to microaerophilic, branching filamentous rods. Actinomyces israelii causes Cervicofacial Actinomycosis ('lumpy jaw': chronic granulomatous infection following dental extraction or trauma; multiple draining sinus tracts discharging yellow 'sulfur granules' composed of bacterial colonies). Actinomyces viscosus is heavily implicated in Root Surface Caries in elderly patients with gingival recession. 2) Lactobacillus: Gram-positive, facultatively anaerobic rod producing abundant lactic acid; secondary colonizer responsible for the deep progression of dental caries into dentin (acidophilic and aciduric).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThis page covers essential clinical and scientific concepts of Oral Microbiology: Actinomyces Species, Actinomycosis & Lactobacillus. Understanding these foundational principles allows rapid recognition of Board examination question stems and clinical diagnostic dilemmas.\n\nPRC Board Examination Pearl:\nFocus on high-yield hallmarks, diagnostic criteria, and standard clinical dosages/classifications associated with Oral Microbiology: Actinomyces Species, Actinomycosis & Lactobacillus.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
