    "summary": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nObligate intracellular bacteria: 1) Rickettsia: Gram-negative obligate intracellular bacteria transmitted by arthropod vectors; target vascular endothelial cells causing vasculitis, fever, rash. Rickettsia prowazekii causes Epidemic Typhus (vector: human body louse, Pediculus humanus); Rickettsia typhi causes Endemic/Murine Typhus (vector: rat flea, Xenopsylla cheopis); Rickettsia rickettsii causes Rocky Mountain Spotted Fever (vector: Dermacentor tick). 2) Chlamydia trachomatis: Most common bacterial sexually transmitted infection; causes non-gonococcal urethritis, pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), and ocular trachoma (leading cause of preventable blindness worldwide). Biphasic lifecycle: infectious extracellular Elementary Body (EB) vs metabolically active intracellular Reticulate Body (RB).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThis page covers essential clinical and scientific concepts of Atypical Bacteria: Rickettsial Infections, Vectors & Chlamydia Trachomatis. Understanding these foundational principles allows rapid recognition of Board examination question stems and clinical diagnostic dilemmas.\n\nPRC Board Examination Pearl:\nFocus on high-yield hallmarks, diagnostic criteria, and standard clinical dosages/classifications associated with Atypical Bacteria: Rickettsial Infections, Vectors & Chlamydia Trachomatis.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
