    "originalPrint": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nEnteroviruses and clinical oral lesions: Picornaviridae (small naked positive-sense ssRNA viruses, fecal-oral transmission): 1) Poliovirus: destroys anterior motor horn cells in spinal cord, causing flaccid asymmetric paralysis. 2) Coxsackievirus Group A: A16 causes Hand-Foot-and-Mouth Disease (vesicular lesions on palms, soles, buccal mucosa, tongue). Coxsackie A causes Herpangina (multiple small vesicles and shallow ulcers localized to the POSTERIOR oral cavity: soft palate, tonsillar pillars, uvula; high fever, sore throat). Coxsackie A24 causes acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis. 3) Coxsackievirus Group B: affects internal organs; causes epidemic pleurodynia (Bornholm disease, 'devil's grip' chest pain), acute myocarditis, and pericarditis.",
