    "originalPrint": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nSuperficial and opportunistic fungal infections: 1) Dermatophytoses: fungal infections of keratinized tissues (skin, hair, nails) caused by Trichophyton, Microsporum, and Epidermophyton. Tinea pedis ('athlete's foot'), Tinea capitis (scalp ringworm), Tinea cruris ('jock itch'), Tinea corporis ('ringworm' of body), Tinea unguium (onychomycosis, thickened yellow nails). 2) Candida albicans: dimorphic opportunistic yeast (yeast forms in normal flora, pseudohyphae/hyphae invade tissue). Pseudomembranous Candidiasis ('thrush': white curd-like plaques on oral mucosa that CAN be wiped off with gauze, leaving a red bleeding erythematous base), Erythematous candidiasis (median rhomboid glossitis, denture stomatitis), Angular cheilitis (perleche at oral commissures, often mixed Candida and Staph aureus).",
