    "originalPrint": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nHeart failure hemodynamics and clinical manifestations: 1) Left-Sided Heart Failure: Most common cause is ischemic heart disease and hypertension. Clinical consequences are driven by pulmonary congestion and decreased cardiac output: pulmonary edema, dyspnea on exertion, orthopnea (shortness of breath when lying flat relieved by multiple pillows), Paroxysmal Nocturnal Dyspnea (PND), cough with rust-colored sputum containing 'heart failure cells' (hemosiderin-laden alveolar macrophages). 2) Right-Sided Heart Failure: Most common cause is left-sided heart failure (isolated right CHF caused by lung disease = Cor Pulmonale). Consequence is systemic venous congestion: elevated Jugular Venous Distension (JVD), hepatosplenomegaly ('nutmeg liver' with centrilobular necrosis), ascites, and dependent bilateral pitting ankle/pretibial edema.",
