    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
    "summary": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nAHA Guidelines for Prevention of Infective Endocarditis (IE): IE involves bacterial colonization of heart valves (Mitral valve most commonly affected; Tricuspid valve in IV drug abusers via Staph aureus; native valves damaged by rheumatic fever colonized by oral Streptococcus viridans). Conditions requiring dental antibiotic prophylaxis: 1) Prosthetic cardiac valve or prosthetic material used in valve repair, 2) Previous history of infective endocarditis, 3) Unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease (CHD) or repaired CHD with residual shunts, 4) Cardiac transplant recipients with cardiac valvulopathy. NOT indicated for mitral valve prolapse, rheumatic heart disease, or coronary stents. Standard Regimen: Amoxicillin 2g orally 30-60 minutes before dental procedure (Children: 50 mg/kg). If allergic to penicillin: Clindamycin 600 mg, Azithromycin/Clarithromycin 500 mg, or Cephalexin 2g.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThis page covers essential clinical and scientific concepts of Cardiovascular Pathology: Infective Endocarditis & AHA Antibiotic Prophylaxis Guidelines. Understanding these foundational principles allows rapid recognition of Board examination question stems and clinical diagnostic dilemmas.\n\nPRC Board Examination Pearl:\nFocus on high-yield hallmarks, diagnostic criteria, and standard clinical dosages/classifications associated with Cardiovascular Pathology: Infective Endocarditis & AHA Antibiotic Prophylaxis Guidelines.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
