    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
    "summary": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nFetal vascular shunts and adult anatomical remnants: Fetal circulation shunts oxygenated blood from the placenta to vital organs bypassing non-functional fetal lungs and liver: 1) Ductus arteriosus (connects pulmonary artery to descending aorta) closes after birth to become the Ligamentum arteriosum. 2) Foramen ovale (interatrial septum shunt from right atrium to left atrium) closes to become the Fossa ovalis. 3) Ductus venosus (shunts umbilical vein blood directly to inferior vena cava bypassing liver sinusoids) becomes the Ligamentum venosum. 4) Umbilical vein (carries oxygenated blood from placenta to fetus) obliterates into the Ligamentum teres hepatis (round ligament of liver). 5) Umbilical arteries become the Medial umbilical ligaments. Vascular histology: Arteries possess thicker muscular tunica media and withstand higher pressure without valves; Veins have larger lumen, thinner walls, and valves to prevent backflow.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThis page covers essential clinical and scientific concepts of Cardiovascular Embryology: Fetal Circulation Shunts & Vascular Anatomy. Understanding these foundational principles allows rapid recognition of Board examination question stems and clinical diagnostic dilemmas.\n\nPRC Board Examination Pearl:\nFocus on high-yield hallmarks, diagnostic criteria, and standard clinical dosages/classifications associated with Cardiovascular Embryology: Fetal Circulation Shunts & Vascular Anatomy.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
