    "originalPrint": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nVascular flow disturbances: 1) Edema: Abnormal accumulation of fluid in interstitial tissue spaces or body cavities. Causes: increased capillary hydrostatic pressure (CHF), reduced plasma oncotic pressure (hypoalbuminemia, nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis), lymphatic obstruction (filariasis, post-radiation lymphedema), and sodium retention. Forms: Ascites (peritoneal cavity), Hydrothorax/Pleural effusion (pleural cavity), Hydropericardium/Cardiac tamponade (pericardial sac), Anasarca (severe generalized subcutaneous edema). 2) Hemorrhage: Extravasation of blood from damaged blood vessels: Petechiae (minute 1-2 mm pinpoint hemorrhages in skin or mucosa; associated with thrombocytopenia, low platelet count, or vessel wall weakness), Purpura (slightly larger 3-5 mm hemorrhages; vasculitis or trauma), Ecchymoses (subcutaneous hematoma > 1-2 cm, commonly called a 'bruise'; hemoglobin [red-blue] -> bilirubin [blue-green] -> hemosiderin [golden-brown]), Hematoma (three-dimensional mass of accumulated blood within a tissue, e.g. subdural or retroperitoneal).",
