    "originalPrint": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nRed blood cell disorders & hematologic malignancies: 1) Sickle Cell Anemia (HbS): Autosomal recessive point mutation in the beta-globin gene where Valine replaces Glutamic acid at the 6th position. Under low oxygen tension, HbS polymerizes into rigid sickle-shaped erythrocytes, causing vascular occlusion, sickle crises, splenic infarction, and avascular necrosis. Radiographic skull hallmark: prominent diploic space with 'hair-on-end' (crewcut) appearance due to compensatory bone marrow hyperplasia; osteomyelitis is frequently caused by Salmonella. 2) Erythroblastosis Fetalis (Hemolytic Disease of the Newborn): Rh-negative mother sensitized to Rh-positive fetus, anti-Rh IgG antibodies cross placenta in subsequent pregnancies causing severe fetal hemolysis, hydrops fetalis, and kernicterus. 3) Agranulocytosis: severe reduction in circulating granulocytes/neutrophils (< 500/uL), precipitating necrotic, punched-out oral ulcerations without inflammatory halos. 4) Leukemia: neoplastic overgrowth of immature white blood cells in bone marrow and peripheral blood; Acute Monocytic and Myelomonocytic Leukemia are classically associated with severe, boggy, generalized leukemic gingival enlargement and spontaneous bleeding.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThis page covers essential clinical and scientific concepts of Hematologic Pathology: Sickle Cell Anemia, Erythroblastosis Fetalis & Leukemias. Understanding these foundational principles allows rapid recognition of Board examination question stems and clinical diagnostic dilemmas.",
