    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
    "summary": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nAmides vs Esters differentiation: Amides have two 'i's in their generic name (Lidocaine, Mepivacaine, Prilocaine, Articaine, Bupivacaine); Esters have only one 'i' (Procaine, Tetracaine, Benzocaine, Cocaine). Esters metabolized in plasma by pseudocholinesterase to PABA (responsible for frequent allergic reactions); Amides metabolized primarily in liver by CYP450 (true allergy to amides is extremely rare). Articaine possesses an ester side chain, enabling 90% plasma hydrolysis and superior safety.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThis page covers essential clinical and scientific concepts of Dental Pharmacology: Chemical Classes of Local Anesthetics (Amides vs Esters). Understanding these foundational principles allows rapid recognition of Board examination question stems and clinical diagnostic dilemmas.\n\nPRC Board Examination Pearl:\nFocus on high-yield hallmarks, diagnostic criteria, and standard clinical dosages/classifications associated with Dental Pharmacology: Chemical Classes of Local Anesthetics (Amides vs Esters).\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
