    "summary": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nSedative-hypnotics: Benzodiazepines (Diazepam, Midazolam, Triazolam) enhance GABA-A receptor action by increasing the FREQUENCY of chloride channel opening, producing sedation, anxiolysis, muscle relaxation, and anterograde amnesia. High safety ceiling; reversed by Flumazenil. Barbiturates (Phenobarbital, Thiopental) increase the DURATION of chloride channel opening and can open channels directly at high doses, carrying severe risk of fatal respiratory depression.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThis page covers essential clinical and scientific concepts of Dental Pharmacology: Sedative-Hypnotics Classification. Understanding these foundational principles allows rapid recognition of Board examination question stems and clinical diagnostic dilemmas.\n\nPRC Board Examination Pearl:\nFocus on high-yield hallmarks, diagnostic criteria, and standard clinical dosages/classifications associated with Dental Pharmacology: Sedative-Hypnotics Classification.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
