    "summary": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nLocal Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity (LAST) clinical stages: 1) Initial CNS excitation: circumoral numbness, tongue paresthesia, metallic taste, tinnitus, slurred speech, shivering, muscle twitching, generalized tonic-clonic convulsions. 2) Secondary CNS depression: unconsciousness, respiratory arrest. 3) Cardiovascular collapse: bradycardia, severe hypotension, ventricular fibrillation, asystole. Emergency protocol: Stop injection, call 911, hyperventilate with 100% O2, Midazolam for seizures, administer 20% Lipid Emulsion (Intralipid).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThis page covers essential clinical and scientific concepts of Local Anesthesia: Systemic Toxicity (LAST) Progression & Management. Understanding these foundational principles allows rapid recognition of Board examination question stems and clinical diagnostic dilemmas.\n\nPRC Board Examination Pearl:\nFocus on high-yield hallmarks, diagnostic criteria, and standard clinical dosages/classifications associated with Local Anesthesia: Systemic Toxicity (LAST) Progression & Management.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
