    "summary": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nAdverse local anesthetic reactions: 1) Systemic Toxicity (vessel deposition or overdosage; early CNS signs: talkativeness, anxiety, slurred speech, dizziness; severe: seizures, coma, respiratory arrest; cardiac: bradycardia, hypotension). 2) Allergic Reactions: mediated by histamine; caused by preservatives (Methylparaben) or antioxidants (Sodium Bisulfite in vasoconstrictors). Esters (metabolized by plasma pseudocholinesterase to PABA, high allergy rate) vs Amides (metabolized by liver CYP450). Vasoconstrictors reduce systemic absorption and bleeding (Max Epi: healthy ASA I = 0.2mg; cardiac ASA III/IV = 0.04mg).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThis page covers essential clinical and scientific concepts of Local Anesthesia: Adverse Reactions, Systemic Toxicity & Vasoconstrictors. Understanding these foundational principles allows rapid recognition of Board examination question stems and clinical diagnostic dilemmas.\n\nPRC Board Examination Pearl:\nFocus on high-yield hallmarks, diagnostic criteria, and standard clinical dosages/classifications associated with Local Anesthesia: Adverse Reactions, Systemic Toxicity & Vasoconstrictors.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
