    "summary": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nTooth physiology & architecture: Contact areas stabilize arches and prevent food impaction. Embrasures (Spillways): Largest occlusal embrasure is between Maxillary Canine and 1st Premolar; Largest incisal embrasure is between Maxillary Lateral and Canine; Smallest incisal embrasure is between Mandibular Central Incisors. Height of Contour: Anteriors at cervical third facially and lingually; Posteriors at cervical third facially, middle third lingually (except mandibular molars at cervical/middle junction facially, and mandibular 2nd premolar at occlusal third lingually). CEJ curvature is greater on anterior teeth than posteriors, and always greater on the MESIAL than the DISTAL (greatest on mesial of maxillary central incisor!).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThis page covers essential clinical and scientific concepts of Tooth Physiology: Contact Areas, Embrasures, Height of Contour & CEJ. Understanding these foundational principles allows rapid recognition of Board examination question stems and clinical diagnostic dilemmas.\n\nPRC Board Examination Pearl:\nFocus on high-yield hallmarks, diagnostic criteria, and standard clinical dosages/classifications associated with Tooth Physiology: Contact Areas, Embrasures, Height of Contour & CEJ.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
