    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
    "summary": "Original Printed Textbook Content:\nMaxillary tooth morphology guide: Central Incisor (widest anterior MD, sharp 90\u00b0 mesioincisal angle). Lateral Incisor (most variable crown after 3rd molar, peg lateral, dens invaginatus, deep lingual pit). Canine (longest root, cornerstone, canine eminence, mesial cusp ridge shorter than distal). 1st Premolar (only premolar with 2 roots in 60%, deep mesial crown and root concavity, mesial marginal groove). 2nd Premolar (single root, equal cusp heights, wrinkled occlusal surface). 1st Molar (largest maxillary tooth, 4 cusps + Cusp of Carabelli on ML cusp, prominent oblique ridge, 3 divergent roots, MB root has MB2 canal in >60%).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThis page covers essential clinical and scientific concepts of Permanent Dentition Morphology: Maxillary Teeth (Incisors, Canines, Premolars, Molars). Understanding these foundational principles allows rapid recognition of Board examination question stems and clinical diagnostic dilemmas.\n\nPRC Board Examination Pearl:\nFocus on high-yield hallmarks, diagnostic criteria, and standard clinical dosages/classifications associated with Permanent Dentition Morphology: Maxillary Teeth (Incisors, Canines, Premolars, Molars).\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Key lecture pearls, high-yield highlighted terms, and memory aids noted during DMF comprehensive board coaching.",
