    "originalPrint": "Permanent Dentition Eruption Chronology:\n- 6-7 years: Mandibular 1st Molar (36, 46) & Central Incisor (31, 41)\n- 7-8 years: Maxillary 1st Molar (16, 26), Maxillary Central Incisor (11, 21), Mandibular Lateral Incisor (32, 42)\n- 8-9 years: Maxillary Lateral Incisor (12, 22)\n- 9-10 years: Mandibular Canine (33, 43)\n- 10-11 years: Maxillary 1st Premolar (14, 24), Mandibular 1st Premolar (34, 44)\n- 10-12 years: Maxillary 2nd Premolar (15, 25), Mandibular 2nd Premolar (35, 45)\n- 11-12 years: Maxillary Canine (13, 23)\n- 12-13 years: 2nd Permanent Molars (12-year molars)\n- 17-21 years: 3rd Permanent Molars (wisdom teeth)\n- Eruption Sequences:\n  * Mandibular: 6 -> 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 -> 7 -> 8\n  * Maxillary: 6 -> 1 -> 2 -> 4 -> 5 -> 3 -> 7 -> 8",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nNotice the key difference between arches: In the mandible, the canine erupts BEFORE the premolars (sequence: 6-1-2-3-4-5-7). In the maxilla, the canine erupts AFTER the premolars (sequence: 6-1-2-4-5-3-7). Because the maxillary canine has the longest path of eruption and erupts last among anterior teeth, it has the highest incidence of impaction second only to third molars.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nFavorite PRC board question: 'Which tooth erupts last in the maxillary anterior quadrant?' -> Maxillary Canine (at 11-12 years). Mandibular sequence = 6-1-2-3-4-5-7. Maxillary sequence = 6-1-2-4-5-3-7.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Maxillary canine comes LAST in maxilla (after premolars) -> high risk of impaction!\n* Mandibular canine comes BEFORE premolars!\n* First permanent tooth to erupt = mandibular 1st molar (age 6).",
    "summary": "Permanent Dentition Eruption Chronology:\n- 6-7 years: Mandibular 1st Molar (36, 46) & Central Incisor (31, 41)\n- 7-8 years: Maxillary 1st Molar (16, 26), Maxillary Central Incisor (11, 21), Mandibular Lateral Incisor (32, 42)\n- 8-9 years: Maxillary Lateral Incisor (12, 22)\n- 9-10 years: Mandibular Canine (33, 43)\n- 10-11 years: Maxillary 1st Premolar (14, 24), Mandibular 1st Premolar (34, 44)\n- 10-12 years: Maxillary 2nd Premolar (15, 25), Mandibular 2nd Premolar (35, 45)\n- 11-12 years: Maxillary Canine (13, 23)\n- 12-13 years: 2nd Permanent Molars (12-year molars)\n- 17-21 years: 3rd Permanent Molars (wisdom teeth)\n- Eruption Sequences:\n  * Mandibular: 6 -> 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 -> 7 -> 8\n  * Maxillary: 6 -> 1 -> 2 -> 4 -> 5 -> 3 -> 7 -> 8\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nNotice the key difference between arches: In the mandible, the canine erupts BEFORE the premolars (sequence: 6-1-2-3-4-5-7). In the maxilla, the canine erupts AFTER the premolars (sequence: 6-1-2-4-5-3-7). Because the maxillary canine has the longest path of eruption and erupts last among anterior teeth, it has the highest incidence of impaction second only to third molars.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nFavorite PRC board question: 'Which tooth erupts last in the maxillary anterior quadrant?' -> Maxillary Canine (at 11-12 years). Mandibular sequence = 6-1-2-3-4-5-7. Maxillary sequence = 6-1-2-4-5-3-7.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Maxillary canine comes LAST in maxilla (after premolars) -> high risk of impaction!\n* Mandibular canine comes BEFORE premolars!\n* First permanent tooth to erupt = mandibular 1st molar (age 6).",
