    "pageNumber": 105,
    "title": "Primary vs Permanent Tooth Morphology & Pulp Anatomy",
    "subject": "Pediatric Dentistry",
    "day": "Day 3 (8:00 AM \u2022 10%) - Pediatric Dentistry",
    "author": "Dr. Aileen Lim",
    "keywords": [
      "primary vs permanent",
      "enamel thickness",
      "cervical constriction",
      "pulp horns",
      "flared roots",
      "mesiobuccal pulp horn"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Differences Between Primary and Permanent Teeth:\n1. Crown Dimensions: Primary crowns are wider mesiodistally relative to their cervico-occlusal height (squat appearance).\n2. Cervical Ridge: Marked cervical constriction and prominent cervical enamel ridge (especially on buccal of primary first molars).\n3. Enamel & Dentin Thickness: Thinner enamel (~1mm vs 2.5mm) and dentin layer in primary teeth; enamel rods in cervical third incline occlusally (unlike permanent teeth where they incline apically).\n4. Pulp Cavity: Pulp chambers are proportionally larger; pulp horns are higher and closer to the outer surface, especially the mesiobuccal pulp horn of primary molars.\n5. Roots: Primary molar roots are longer, more slender, and markedly flared beyond crown width to accommodate the developing permanent premolar bicuspid buds.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe thin enamel and high pulp horns of deciduous teeth explain why proximal caries progresses rapidly to pulp exposure in pediatric patients. The pronounced cervical constriction requires light feather-edge margins rather than heavy shoulders when preparing for stainless steel crowns, and the flaring roots dictate delicate extraction technique to avoid damaging the underlying premolar follicle.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nDirection of cervical enamel rods: In primary teeth, cervical enamel rods slope OCCLUSALLY (incisally/coronally). In permanent teeth, cervical enamel rods slope APICALLY. This means no cavosurface beveling is required on primary gingival seats!",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Enamel thickness: primary = 1mm, permanent = 2.5mm!\n* MB pulp horn of primary 1st molar is closest to surface!\n* Enamel rods slope OCCLUSALLY at cervix (no gingival bevel!).",
    "summary": "Differences Between Primary and Permanent Teeth:\n1. Crown Dimensions: Primary crowns are wider mesiodistally relative to their cervico-occlusal height (squat appearance).\n2. Cervical Ridge: Marked cervical constriction and prominent cervical enamel ridge (especially on buccal of primary first molars).\n3. Enamel & Dentin Thickness: Thinner enamel (~1mm vs 2.5mm) and dentin layer in primary teeth; enamel rods in cervical third incline occlusally (unlike permanent teeth where they incline apically).\n4. Pulp Cavity: Pulp chambers are proportionally larger; pulp horns are higher and closer to the outer surface, especially the mesiobuccal pulp horn of primary molars.\n5. Roots: Primary molar roots are longer, more slender, and markedly flared beyond crown width to accommodate the developing permanent premolar bicuspid buds.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe thin enamel and high pulp horns of deciduous teeth explain why proximal caries progresses rapidly to pulp exposure in pediatric patients. The pronounced cervical constriction requires light feather-edge margins rather than heavy shoulders when preparing for stainless steel crowns, and the flaring roots dictate delicate extraction technique to avoid damaging the underlying premolar follicle.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nDirection of cervical enamel rods: In primary teeth, cervical enamel rods slope OCCLUSALLY (incisally/coronally). In permanent teeth, cervical enamel rods slope APICALLY. This means no cavosurface beveling is required on primary gingival seats!\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Enamel thickness: primary = 1mm, permanent = 2.5mm!\n* MB pulp horn of primary 1st molar is closest to surface!\n* Enamel rods slope OCCLUSALLY at cervix (no gingival bevel!).",
