    "originalPrint": "Ellis Classification of Crown Fractures & Management:\n- Ellis Class I: Enamel fracture only; Smooth rough edges, composite bonding.\n- Ellis Class II: Enamel and dentin fracture without pulp exposure; Seal dentinal tubules with glass ionomer or composite resin.\n- Ellis Class III: Enamel, dentin fracture WITH pulp exposure.\n  * Immature permanent tooth (open apex, vital): Cvek Pulpotomy (partial pulpotomy, removing 1-2mm of inflamed pulp, MTA or Ca(OH)2 cap) to preserve pulp vitality for Apexogenesis (continued physiological root lengthening and dentinal thickening).\n  * Immature permanent tooth (open apex, non-vital): Apexification (MTA apical plug or long-term Ca(OH)2 to induce apical hard tissue barrier; or regenerative endodontics).\n  * Mature permanent tooth (closed apex): Conventional root canal therapy.\n- Ellis Class IV: Non-vital traumatized tooth with or without crown loss.\n- Ellis Class V: Avulsion of tooth.\n- Ellis Class VI: Root fracture.\n- Ellis Class VII: Tooth displacement without crown/root fracture (luxations).\n- Ellis Class VIII: Complete crown fracture (en masse).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nPreserving pulp vitality in immature permanent teeth is paramount because dentin deposition stops if the pulp becomes necrotic, leaving thin, fragile root walls susceptible to cervical fracture. Apexogenesis maintains vitality of the Hertwig's Epithelial Root Sheath (HERS) and apical odontoblasts, allowing natural root completion. In contrast, apexification merely forms an artificial calcific plug at the root end without strengthening root walls.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nDifference between Apexogenesis and Apexification: Apexogenesis is performed on VITAL immature teeth (physiological root lengthening and wall thickening). Apexification is performed on NON-VITAL immature teeth (apical hard tissue closure only). Cvek pulpotomy removes only 1-2mm of inflamed superficial pulp.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Apexogenesis = VITAL pulp, open apex (allows root to keep growing!).\n* Apexification = DEAD / NON-VITAL pulp, open apex (forms calcific barrier).\n* Cvek pulpotomy = partial pulpotomy (1-2mm) for trauma with open apex!",
    "summary": "Ellis Classification of Crown Fractures & Management:\n- Ellis Class I: Enamel fracture only; Smooth rough edges, composite bonding.\n- Ellis Class II: Enamel and dentin fracture without pulp exposure; Seal dentinal tubules with glass ionomer or composite resin.\n- Ellis Class III: Enamel, dentin fracture WITH pulp exposure.\n  * Immature permanent tooth (open apex, vital): Cvek Pulpotomy (partial pulpotomy, removing 1-2mm of inflamed pulp, MTA or Ca(OH)2 cap) to preserve pulp vitality for Apexogenesis (continued physiological root lengthening and dentinal thickening).\n  * Immature permanent tooth (open apex, non-vital): Apexification (MTA apical plug or long-term Ca(OH)2 to induce apical hard tissue barrier; or regenerative endodontics).\n  * Mature permanent tooth (closed apex): Conventional root canal therapy.\n- Ellis Class IV: Non-vital traumatized tooth with or without crown loss.\n- Ellis Class V: Avulsion of tooth.\n- Ellis Class VI: Root fracture.\n- Ellis Class VII: Tooth displacement without crown/root fracture (luxations).\n- Ellis Class VIII: Complete crown fracture (en masse).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nPreserving pulp vitality in immature permanent teeth is paramount because dentin deposition stops if the pulp becomes necrotic, leaving thin, fragile root walls susceptible to cervical fracture. Apexogenesis maintains vitality of the Hertwig's Epithelial Root Sheath (HERS) and apical odontoblasts, allowing natural root completion. In contrast, apexification merely forms an artificial calcific plug at the root end without strengthening root walls.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nDifference between Apexogenesis and Apexification: Apexogenesis is performed on VITAL immature teeth (physiological root lengthening and wall thickening). Apexification is performed on NON-VITAL immature teeth (apical hard tissue closure only). Cvek pulpotomy removes only 1-2mm of inflamed superficial pulp.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Apexogenesis = VITAL pulp, open apex (allows root to keep growing!).\n* Apexification = DEAD / NON-VITAL pulp, open apex (forms calcific barrier).\n* Cvek pulpotomy = partial pulpotomy (1-2mm) for trauma with open apex!",
