    "originalPrint": "Behavior Guidance in Pediatric Dentistry:\n- Non-Pharmacologic Behavior Management Techniques:\n  1. Tell-Show-Do: Explanation in child-friendly language (Tell), demonstration in non-threatening setting (Show), execution of procedure (Do).\n  2. Positive Reinforcement: Praising appropriate behavior to reinforce repetition.\n  3. Voice Control: Controlled alteration of voice volume, tone, or pace to gain child's attention and establish authority.\n  4. Distraction: Diverting child's attention away from unpleasant sensations.\n  5. Non-Verbal Communication: Eye contact, facial expression, body posture.\n  6. Modeling: Observing a cooperative peer or sibling undergoing dental treatment.\n- Frankl Behavior Rating Scale:\n  * Rating 1 (Definitely Negative --): Refusal of treatment, forceful crying, fearful, extreme resistance.\n  * Rating 2 (Negative -): Reluctant to accept treatment, uncooperative, sullen, withdrawn.\n  * Rating 3 (Positive +): Accepts treatment, willing to comply with reservation, follows directions.\n  * Rating 4 (Definitely Positive ++): Good rapport with dentist, interested in procedures, laughter and enjoyment.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nChild dental anxiety stems primarily from fear of the unknown and perceived loss of control. Tell-Show-Do demystifies instruments and sensory inputs before they reach the mouth, converting unpredictable threats into familiar experiences. The Frankl scale categorizes cooperation objectively, guiding whether non-pharmacologic guidance, nitrous oxide conscious sedation, or general anesthesia is indicated.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nFrankl Rating Scale memory: 1 is Definitely Negative (refusal, thrashing), 4 is Definitely Positive (cooperative, enjoys visit). Tell-Show-Do is the cornerstone and most widely accepted behavior guidance technique.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Frankl 1 = Definitely Negative (--), Frankl 4 = Definitely Positive (++).\n* Tell-Show-Do: #1 most effective and accepted behavior technique!\n* Voice control = change in tone/volume to gain attention (not shouting!).",
    "summary": "Behavior Guidance in Pediatric Dentistry:\n- Non-Pharmacologic Behavior Management Techniques:\n  1. Tell-Show-Do: Explanation in child-friendly language (Tell), demonstration in non-threatening setting (Show), execution of procedure (Do).\n  2. Positive Reinforcement: Praising appropriate behavior to reinforce repetition.\n  3. Voice Control: Controlled alteration of voice volume, tone, or pace to gain child's attention and establish authority.\n  4. Distraction: Diverting child's attention away from unpleasant sensations.\n  5. Non-Verbal Communication: Eye contact, facial expression, body posture.\n  6. Modeling: Observing a cooperative peer or sibling undergoing dental treatment.\n- Frankl Behavior Rating Scale:\n  * Rating 1 (Definitely Negative --): Refusal of treatment, forceful crying, fearful, extreme resistance.\n  * Rating 2 (Negative -): Reluctant to accept treatment, uncooperative, sullen, withdrawn.\n  * Rating 3 (Positive +): Accepts treatment, willing to comply with reservation, follows directions.\n  * Rating 4 (Definitely Positive ++): Good rapport with dentist, interested in procedures, laughter and enjoyment.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nChild dental anxiety stems primarily from fear of the unknown and perceived loss of control. Tell-Show-Do demystifies instruments and sensory inputs before they reach the mouth, converting unpredictable threats into familiar experiences. The Frankl scale categorizes cooperation objectively, guiding whether non-pharmacologic guidance, nitrous oxide conscious sedation, or general anesthesia is indicated.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nFrankl Rating Scale memory: 1 is Definitely Negative (refusal, thrashing), 4 is Definitely Positive (cooperative, enjoys visit). Tell-Show-Do is the cornerstone and most widely accepted behavior guidance technique.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Frankl 1 = Definitely Negative (--), Frankl 4 = Definitely Positive (++).\n* Tell-Show-Do: #1 most effective and accepted behavior technique!\n* Voice control = change in tone/volume to gain attention (not shouting!).",
