    "originalPrint": "Gypsum Products Classification (ADA Specification No. 25):\n- Mineral Gypsum: Calcium Sulfate Dihydrate (CaSO4\u00b72H2O); Heated by calcination to drive off water, forming Calcium Sulfate Hemihydrate (CaSO4\u00b71/2H2O).\n- ADA Types & W/P Ratios:\n  * Type I: Impression Plaster - W/P ratio 0.50 - 0.75 (beta-hemihydrate, fast setting, low strength).\n  * Type II: Model Plaster - W/P ratio 0.45 - 0.50 (beta-hemihydrate, irregular porous particles; for study models and articulators).\n  * Type III: Dental Stone (Class I Stone / Hydrocal) - W/P ratio 0.28 - 0.30 (alpha-hemihydrate, uniform prismatic particles; for diagnostic casts and denture processing).\n  * Type IV: High-Strength Dental Stone (Die Stone / Densite) - W/P ratio 0.22 - 0.24 (modified alpha-hemihydrate, dense cuboidal crystals; for crown and bridge dies).\n  * Type V: High-Strength, High-Expansion Dental Stone - W/P ratio 0.18 - 0.20 (highest compressive strength, high setting expansion to compensate for casting shrinkage of base metal alloys).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe difference among gypsum types lies in the physical shape and porosity of the hemihydrate crystals. Beta-hemihydrate (Plaster) consists of irregular, spongy, porous particles requiring large amounts of excess water (0.50 W/P) to lubricate the mix, resulting in low dry compressive strength. Alpha-hemihydrate (Stone and Die Stone) has dense, smooth, non-porous prisms requiring far less gauging water (0.22 W/P), yielding minimal porosity and maximal compressive strength.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nRemember W/P ratio hierarchy: Type I (0.60) > Type II (0.50) > Type III (0.30) > Type IV (0.24) > Type V (0.20)! Lower W/P ratio = higher compressive strength and hardness. Type V has high expansion for base metal alloy shrinkage.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Plaster = Beta-hemihydrate (porous, weak, needs lots of water).\n* Die stone (Type IV/V) = Alpha-hemihydrate (dense, strong, low W/P ratio).\n* Type IV = Die stone for crown/bridge! Type V = high expansion for base metal alloys.",
    "summary": "Gypsum Products Classification (ADA Specification No. 25):\n- Mineral Gypsum: Calcium Sulfate Dihydrate (CaSO4\u00b72H2O); Heated by calcination to drive off water, forming Calcium Sulfate Hemihydrate (CaSO4\u00b71/2H2O).\n- ADA Types & W/P Ratios:\n  * Type I: Impression Plaster - W/P ratio 0.50 - 0.75 (beta-hemihydrate, fast setting, low strength).\n  * Type II: Model Plaster - W/P ratio 0.45 - 0.50 (beta-hemihydrate, irregular porous particles; for study models and articulators).\n  * Type III: Dental Stone (Class I Stone / Hydrocal) - W/P ratio 0.28 - 0.30 (alpha-hemihydrate, uniform prismatic particles; for diagnostic casts and denture processing).\n  * Type IV: High-Strength Dental Stone (Die Stone / Densite) - W/P ratio 0.22 - 0.24 (modified alpha-hemihydrate, dense cuboidal crystals; for crown and bridge dies).\n  * Type V: High-Strength, High-Expansion Dental Stone - W/P ratio 0.18 - 0.20 (highest compressive strength, high setting expansion to compensate for casting shrinkage of base metal alloys).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe difference among gypsum types lies in the physical shape and porosity of the hemihydrate crystals. Beta-hemihydrate (Plaster) consists of irregular, spongy, porous particles requiring large amounts of excess water (0.50 W/P) to lubricate the mix, resulting in low dry compressive strength. Alpha-hemihydrate (Stone and Die Stone) has dense, smooth, non-porous prisms requiring far less gauging water (0.22 W/P), yielding minimal porosity and maximal compressive strength.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nRemember W/P ratio hierarchy: Type I (0.60) > Type II (0.50) > Type III (0.30) > Type IV (0.24) > Type V (0.20)! Lower W/P ratio = higher compressive strength and hardness. Type V has high expansion for base metal alloy shrinkage.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Plaster = Beta-hemihydrate (porous, weak, needs lots of water).\n* Die stone (Type IV/V) = Alpha-hemihydrate (dense, strong, low W/P ratio).\n* Type IV = Die stone for crown/bridge! Type V = high expansion for base metal alloys.",
