    "originalPrint": "Gypsum Setting Reaction & Modifiers:\n- Chemical Reaction:\n  CaSO4\u00b71/2H2O (Hemihydrate) + 1.5 H2O -> CaSO4\u00b72H2O (Dihydrate) + Heat (Exothermic: 3,900 cal/g-mol).\n- Setting Mechanism: Dissolution of hemihydrate -> Hemihydrate exceeds solubility limit -> Precipitation of dihydrate crystals around nucleation centers -> Intermeshing needle-like dihydrate crystals.\n- Modifiers of Setting Time:\n  * Accelerators: Decrease setting time.\n    - Potassium Sulfate (K2SO4, 2-4%) - most effective chemical accelerator.\n    - Slurry water / Terra alba (ground gypsum dihydrate crystals) - provides ready-made nucleation sites.\n    - Increased spatulation speed/time.\n  * Retarders: Increase setting time.\n    - Borax (Sodium tetraborate, 1-2%) - poisons nucleation sites by forming insoluble calcium borate.\n    - Organic colloids: Blood, Saliva, Agar, Alginate (why impressions must be thoroughly rinsed).\n- Hygroscopic Setting Expansion: Expansion that occurs when gypsum sets while immersed in water (increases expansion by 2-3 times).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nGypsum sets because dihydrate is four times less soluble than hemihydrate at room temperature. As hemihydrate dissolves into water, dihydrate rapidly precipitates out around crystal nuclei. If blood, saliva, or alginate residues remain on the impression surface, organic proteins coat these nucleation seeds, severely inhibiting crystal growth and leaving a soft, chalky, powdery die surface.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nTop board accelerator: Potassium Sulfate (K2SO4)! Top board retarder: Borax! Why must impressions be rinsed clean before pouring? Saliva and blood act as organic retarders that poison crystal nucleation, causing chalky models.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Accelerator = Potassium Sulfate (K2SO4) & Slurry water.\n* Retarder = BORAX (Sodium tetraborate)!\n* Blood and saliva retard gypsum -> ALWAYS rinse impressions thoroughly!",
    "summary": "Gypsum Setting Reaction & Modifiers:\n- Chemical Reaction:\n  CaSO4\u00b71/2H2O (Hemihydrate) + 1.5 H2O -> CaSO4\u00b72H2O (Dihydrate) + Heat (Exothermic: 3,900 cal/g-mol).\n- Setting Mechanism: Dissolution of hemihydrate -> Hemihydrate exceeds solubility limit -> Precipitation of dihydrate crystals around nucleation centers -> Intermeshing needle-like dihydrate crystals.\n- Modifiers of Setting Time:\n  * Accelerators: Decrease setting time.\n    - Potassium Sulfate (K2SO4, 2-4%) - most effective chemical accelerator.\n    - Slurry water / Terra alba (ground gypsum dihydrate crystals) - provides ready-made nucleation sites.\n    - Increased spatulation speed/time.\n  * Retarders: Increase setting time.\n    - Borax (Sodium tetraborate, 1-2%) - poisons nucleation sites by forming insoluble calcium borate.\n    - Organic colloids: Blood, Saliva, Agar, Alginate (why impressions must be thoroughly rinsed).\n- Hygroscopic Setting Expansion: Expansion that occurs when gypsum sets while immersed in water (increases expansion by 2-3 times).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nGypsum sets because dihydrate is four times less soluble than hemihydrate at room temperature. As hemihydrate dissolves into water, dihydrate rapidly precipitates out around crystal nuclei. If blood, saliva, or alginate residues remain on the impression surface, organic proteins coat these nucleation seeds, severely inhibiting crystal growth and leaving a soft, chalky, powdery die surface.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nTop board accelerator: Potassium Sulfate (K2SO4)! Top board retarder: Borax! Why must impressions be rinsed clean before pouring? Saliva and blood act as organic retarders that poison crystal nucleation, causing chalky models.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Accelerator = Potassium Sulfate (K2SO4) & Slurry water.\n* Retarder = BORAX (Sodium tetraborate)!\n* Blood and saliva retard gypsum -> ALWAYS rinse impressions thoroughly!",
    "image": "/book/page_119.webp"
