    "pageNumber": 123,
    "title": "Dental Composite Resins: Organic Matrix, Fillers & Polymerization Chemistry",
    "subject": "Dental Materials",
    "day": "Day 2 (8:00 AM \u2022 10%) - Materials & Prostho",
    "author": "Dr. Mickhail Q. Francisco",
    "keywords": [
      "composite resin",
      "bis-gma",
      "tegdma",
      "silane coupling agent",
      "camphorquinone",
      "free radical polymerization",
      "shrinkage"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Dental Composite Resins Composition & Chemistry:\n- Three Main Components of Composite Resins:\n  1. Organic Polymer Matrix:\n     * Bis-GMA (Bowen's resin - bisphenol A-glycidyl methacrylate): High molecular weight, viscous.\n     * UDMA (Urethane dimethacrylate): Lower viscosity.\n     * TEGDMA (Triethylene glycol dimethacrylate): Viscosity diluent added to allow filler incorporation (increases polymerization shrinkage).\n  2. Inorganic Filler Particles:\n     * Silica, Barium glass, Strontium glass, Zirconia.\n     * Functions: Increases compressive strength, hardness, wear resistance; Decreases polymerization shrinkage, thermal expansion, water sorption.\n  3. Coupling Agent (The Bridge):\n     * Organosilane (gamma-methacryloxypropyltrimethoxysilane).\n     * Binds inorganic filler particles to organic resin matrix (prevents filler pull-out and water degradation).\n- Polymerization Mechanism: Free radical addition polymerization initiated by:\n  * Light-cure: Camphorquinone (photoinitiator, absorption peak 468 nm in blue visible light) + Tertiary amine accelerator (DMAEMA).\n  * Chemical-cure: Benzoyl peroxide + Aromatic tertiary amine.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nBis-GMA's rigid aromatic backbone provides mechanical stiffness but is too viscous to pack into cavities without diluents. TEGDMA lowers viscosity, enabling high filler loading; however, its small molecular size increases double-bond conversion density, increasing volumetric polymerization shrinkage. The silane coupling agent possesses bifunctional ends: silanol groups condense with hydroxyls on the filler silica, while methacrylate groups copolymerize with the resin matrix, transferring masticatory stresses from matrix to filler.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nPeak absorption wavelength of Camphorquinone: 468 nm (blue visible light spectrum)! What is the coupling agent in composite? Organosilane (gamma-methacryloxypropyltrimethoxysilane). Higher filler content = lower polymerization shrinkage.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Camphorquinone absorbs BLUE light at 468 nm!\n* Silane coupling agent connects filler to resin matrix.\n* More filler = STRONGER + LESS shrinkage!",
    "summary": "Dental Composite Resins Composition & Chemistry:\n- Three Main Components of Composite Resins:\n  1. Organic Polymer Matrix:\n     * Bis-GMA (Bowen's resin - bisphenol A-glycidyl methacrylate): High molecular weight, viscous.\n     * UDMA (Urethane dimethacrylate): Lower viscosity.\n     * TEGDMA (Triethylene glycol dimethacrylate): Viscosity diluent added to allow filler incorporation (increases polymerization shrinkage).\n  2. Inorganic Filler Particles:\n     * Silica, Barium glass, Strontium glass, Zirconia.\n     * Functions: Increases compressive strength, hardness, wear resistance; Decreases polymerization shrinkage, thermal expansion, water sorption.\n  3. Coupling Agent (The Bridge):\n     * Organosilane (gamma-methacryloxypropyltrimethoxysilane).\n     * Binds inorganic filler particles to organic resin matrix (prevents filler pull-out and water degradation).\n- Polymerization Mechanism: Free radical addition polymerization initiated by:\n  * Light-cure: Camphorquinone (photoinitiator, absorption peak 468 nm in blue visible light) + Tertiary amine accelerator (DMAEMA).\n  * Chemical-cure: Benzoyl peroxide + Aromatic tertiary amine.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nBis-GMA's rigid aromatic backbone provides mechanical stiffness but is too viscous to pack into cavities without diluents. TEGDMA lowers viscosity, enabling high filler loading; however, its small molecular size increases double-bond conversion density, increasing volumetric polymerization shrinkage. The silane coupling agent possesses bifunctional ends: silanol groups condense with hydroxyls on the filler silica, while methacrylate groups copolymerize with the resin matrix, transferring masticatory stresses from matrix to filler.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nPeak absorption wavelength of Camphorquinone: 468 nm (blue visible light spectrum)! What is the coupling agent in composite? Organosilane (gamma-methacryloxypropyltrimethoxysilane). Higher filler content = lower polymerization shrinkage.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Camphorquinone absorbs BLUE light at 468 nm!\n* Silane coupling agent connects filler to resin matrix.\n* More filler = STRONGER + LESS shrinkage!",
    "image": "/book/page_123.webp"
