    "originalPrint": "Composite Resin Classifications & Handling:\n- Structural Classifications:\n  * Macrofilled (10-50 microns): Rough surface, poor polishability, high wear; obsolete.\n  * Microfilled (0.04 microns, colloidal silica): Highly polishable, high esthetics (anterior Class III/V), low mechanical strength (flexible, low modulus), high water sorption.\n  * Hybrid / Microhybrid (0.5-2.0 microns + colloidal silica): Good strength and moderate polishability (all-around anterior/posterior).\n  * Nanofill / Nanohybrid (1-100 nm clusters): Superior polish retention, high filler loading (75-80% by weight), high strength, versatile for both anterior and posterior restorations.\n  * Bulk-Fill Composites: Increased depth of cure (4-5mm) due to enhanced translucency and novel photoinitiators; low polymerization stress.\n- Configuration Factor (C-Factor):\n  * Ratio of bonded surfaces to unbonded (free) surfaces in a cavity preparation.\n  * C-Factor = Bonded / Unbonded.\n  * Class I cavity has HIGHEST C-Factor (5 bonded walls / 1 unbonded wall = 5); High stress induces marginal gap formation and post-operative sensitivity.\n  * Solution: Incremental placement technique (max 2mm oblique layers).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nDuring polymerization, composite shrinks toward the center of mass. Unbonded surfaces relieve shrinkage stress by flowing elastically. In a Class I occlusal box, five rigid tooth walls resist deformation, leaving only one free occlusal surface to dissipate stress (C-factor = 5). High internal tension breaks the adhesive hybrid layer at the margins, creating microleakage, enamel rod fractures, and hypersensitivity. Oblique layering contacts fewer opposing walls simultaneously, drastically lowering the C-factor per increment.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nWhich cavity preparation has the highest C-factor? Class I cavity (C-factor = 5/1 = 5)! Which composite has the best polishability and esthetic luster? Microfilled / Nanofilled composites. Incremental layering maximum thickness: 2mm.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Highest C-Factor = CLASS I (5 bonded / 1 unbonded = 5) -> highest stress!\n* Layer in 2mm oblique increments to avoid shrinking pulling away from walls.\n* Nanofill = best strength AND best polish retention!",
    "summary": "Composite Resin Classifications & Handling:\n- Structural Classifications:\n  * Macrofilled (10-50 microns): Rough surface, poor polishability, high wear; obsolete.\n  * Microfilled (0.04 microns, colloidal silica): Highly polishable, high esthetics (anterior Class III/V), low mechanical strength (flexible, low modulus), high water sorption.\n  * Hybrid / Microhybrid (0.5-2.0 microns + colloidal silica): Good strength and moderate polishability (all-around anterior/posterior).\n  * Nanofill / Nanohybrid (1-100 nm clusters): Superior polish retention, high filler loading (75-80% by weight), high strength, versatile for both anterior and posterior restorations.\n  * Bulk-Fill Composites: Increased depth of cure (4-5mm) due to enhanced translucency and novel photoinitiators; low polymerization stress.\n- Configuration Factor (C-Factor):\n  * Ratio of bonded surfaces to unbonded (free) surfaces in a cavity preparation.\n  * C-Factor = Bonded / Unbonded.\n  * Class I cavity has HIGHEST C-Factor (5 bonded walls / 1 unbonded wall = 5); High stress induces marginal gap formation and post-operative sensitivity.\n  * Solution: Incremental placement technique (max 2mm oblique layers).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nDuring polymerization, composite shrinks toward the center of mass. Unbonded surfaces relieve shrinkage stress by flowing elastically. In a Class I occlusal box, five rigid tooth walls resist deformation, leaving only one free occlusal surface to dissipate stress (C-factor = 5). High internal tension breaks the adhesive hybrid layer at the margins, creating microleakage, enamel rod fractures, and hypersensitivity. Oblique layering contacts fewer opposing walls simultaneously, drastically lowering the C-factor per increment.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nWhich cavity preparation has the highest C-factor? Class I cavity (C-factor = 5/1 = 5)! Which composite has the best polishability and esthetic luster? Microfilled / Nanofilled composites. Incremental layering maximum thickness: 2mm.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Highest C-Factor = CLASS I (5 bonded / 1 unbonded = 5) -> highest stress!\n* Layer in 2mm oblique increments to avoid shrinking pulling away from walls.\n* Nanofill = best strength AND best polish retention!",
