    "originalPrint": "Dental Amalgam: Alloy Composition & Phases:\n- Amalgam = Alloy of Mercury (Hg) with Silver-Tin-Copper powder.\n- Key Components of Amalgam Alloy Powder:\n  * Silver (Ag, 40-70%): Increases strength, expansion, corrosion resistance.\n  * Tin (Sn, 12-30%): Decreases strength and hardness, increases setting contraction and working time.\n  * Copper (Cu): High-copper amalgam (>12% Cu, up to 30%) ELIMINATES the corrosion-prone Gamma-2 phase.\n  * Zinc (Zn, 0-2%): Deoxidizer during manufacturing; Moisture contamination of zinc-containing amalgam causes DELAYED EXPANSION (hydrogen gas generation pushing restoration out of cavity).\n- Metallurgical Phases:\n  * Gamma (Ag3Sn): Unreacted alloy particle (strongest phase).\n  * Gamma-1 (Ag2Hg3): Silver-mercury matrix (intermediate strength).\n  * Gamma-2 (Sn8Hg): Tin-mercury phase - WEAKEST, highest corrosion rate, prone to creep and marginal breakdown; ELIMINATED in high-copper alloys by forming Eta phase (Cu6Sn5).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nLow-copper amalgams set by forming the disastrous Gamma-2 phase (Sn8Hg), which acts as an electrochemical anode, rapidly corroding and causing marginal ditching, creep, and bulk fracture. High-copper amalgams (>12% Cu) alter the reaction thermodynamics: copper preferentially binds tin to form Cu6Sn5 (eta phase), completely suppressing Sn-Hg crystallization and yielding superior compressive strength and edge stability.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nWhich phase is the weakest and most prone to corrosion in amalgam? -> Gamma-2 phase (Sn8Hg)! How does high-copper amalgam eliminate Gamma-2? By forming Eta phase (Cu6Sn5). What causes delayed expansion in zinc-containing amalgam? Moisture contamination during condensation!",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* WEAKEST & most corrosive phase = GAMMA-2 (Sn8Hg)!\n* High-copper amalgam (>12% Cu) ELIMINATES Gamma-2 -> forms Cu6Sn5!\n* Moisture + Zinc = delayed expansion (H2 gas pushes amalgam out!).",
    "summary": "Dental Amalgam: Alloy Composition & Phases:\n- Amalgam = Alloy of Mercury (Hg) with Silver-Tin-Copper powder.\n- Key Components of Amalgam Alloy Powder:\n  * Silver (Ag, 40-70%): Increases strength, expansion, corrosion resistance.\n  * Tin (Sn, 12-30%): Decreases strength and hardness, increases setting contraction and working time.\n  * Copper (Cu): High-copper amalgam (>12% Cu, up to 30%) ELIMINATES the corrosion-prone Gamma-2 phase.\n  * Zinc (Zn, 0-2%): Deoxidizer during manufacturing; Moisture contamination of zinc-containing amalgam causes DELAYED EXPANSION (hydrogen gas generation pushing restoration out of cavity).\n- Metallurgical Phases:\n  * Gamma (Ag3Sn): Unreacted alloy particle (strongest phase).\n  * Gamma-1 (Ag2Hg3): Silver-mercury matrix (intermediate strength).\n  * Gamma-2 (Sn8Hg): Tin-mercury phase - WEAKEST, highest corrosion rate, prone to creep and marginal breakdown; ELIMINATED in high-copper alloys by forming Eta phase (Cu6Sn5).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nLow-copper amalgams set by forming the disastrous Gamma-2 phase (Sn8Hg), which acts as an electrochemical anode, rapidly corroding and causing marginal ditching, creep, and bulk fracture. High-copper amalgams (>12% Cu) alter the reaction thermodynamics: copper preferentially binds tin to form Cu6Sn5 (eta phase), completely suppressing Sn-Hg crystallization and yielding superior compressive strength and edge stability.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nWhich phase is the weakest and most prone to corrosion in amalgam? -> Gamma-2 phase (Sn8Hg)! How does high-copper amalgam eliminate Gamma-2? By forming Eta phase (Cu6Sn5). What causes delayed expansion in zinc-containing amalgam? Moisture contamination during condensation!\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* WEAKEST & most corrosive phase = GAMMA-2 (Sn8Hg)!\n* High-copper amalgam (>12% Cu) ELIMINATES Gamma-2 -> forms Cu6Sn5!\n* Moisture + Zinc = delayed expansion (H2 gas pushes amalgam out!).",
    "image": "/book/page_125.webp"
