    "originalPrint": "Amalgam Physical Properties & Clinical Manipulation:\n- Creep:\n  * Definition: Time-dependent permanent plastic deformation of set amalgam under continuous static or cyclic masticatory stresses.\n  * Low creep (<1%) is directly correlated with resistance to marginal ditching and long-term marginal integrity; High-copper amalgams have significantly lower creep than conventional low-copper amalgams.\n- Trituration (Mechanical Mixing in Amalgamator):\n  * Normal Mix: Shiny, warm, coherent plastic mass that does not stick to capsule.\n  * Undertriturated Mix: Dull, crumbly, dry, low tensile strength, fast setting.\n  * Overtriturated Mix: Soupy, hot, sticky, clings to capsule, shorter working time, higher setting contraction.\n- Condensation & Burnishing:\n  * Condensation pressure: 3 to 4 lbs pressure with overlapping strokes to eliminate voids and express mercury-rich matrix to surface.\n  * Pre-carve burnishing: Adapts margins, brings excess mercury to surface for removal.\n  * Post-carve burnishing: Produces smooth surface, reduces initial corrosion.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nTrituration rubs off the protective oxide film coating the silver-tin particles, exposing pristine alloy surfaces to react with liquid mercury. Proper condensation forces alloy particles together into tight mechanical contact, squeezing unreacted mercury upward to be carved away. Retaining excess mercury (>54%) weakens the restoration exponentially by generating excessive Gamma-1 and residual porosity.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nDefinition of Creep: Time-dependent plastic deformation under compressive stress. Undertriturated amalgam is dull and crumbly; overtriturated is soupy and sticky. Low creep correlates directly with superior marginal integrity.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Creep = slow deformation over time under biting forces (causes marginal ditching).\n* Undertriturated = dull & crumbly (weak!). Overtriturated = soupy & sticky.\n* Condense firmly to squeeze out excess mercury!",
    "summary": "Amalgam Physical Properties & Clinical Manipulation:\n- Creep:\n  * Definition: Time-dependent permanent plastic deformation of set amalgam under continuous static or cyclic masticatory stresses.\n  * Low creep (<1%) is directly correlated with resistance to marginal ditching and long-term marginal integrity; High-copper amalgams have significantly lower creep than conventional low-copper amalgams.\n- Trituration (Mechanical Mixing in Amalgamator):\n  * Normal Mix: Shiny, warm, coherent plastic mass that does not stick to capsule.\n  * Undertriturated Mix: Dull, crumbly, dry, low tensile strength, fast setting.\n  * Overtriturated Mix: Soupy, hot, sticky, clings to capsule, shorter working time, higher setting contraction.\n- Condensation & Burnishing:\n  * Condensation pressure: 3 to 4 lbs pressure with overlapping strokes to eliminate voids and express mercury-rich matrix to surface.\n  * Pre-carve burnishing: Adapts margins, brings excess mercury to surface for removal.\n  * Post-carve burnishing: Produces smooth surface, reduces initial corrosion.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nTrituration rubs off the protective oxide film coating the silver-tin particles, exposing pristine alloy surfaces to react with liquid mercury. Proper condensation forces alloy particles together into tight mechanical contact, squeezing unreacted mercury upward to be carved away. Retaining excess mercury (>54%) weakens the restoration exponentially by generating excessive Gamma-1 and residual porosity.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nDefinition of Creep: Time-dependent plastic deformation under compressive stress. Undertriturated amalgam is dull and crumbly; overtriturated is soupy and sticky. Low creep correlates directly with superior marginal integrity.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Creep = slow deformation over time under biting forces (causes marginal ditching).\n* Undertriturated = dull & crumbly (weak!). Overtriturated = soupy & sticky.\n* Condense firmly to squeeze out excess mercury!",
