    "pageNumber": 149,
    "title": "Matrix Systems for Class II Restorations: Tofflemire & Sectional Matrices",
    "subject": "Restorative Dentistry",
    "day": "Day 1 (2:00 PM \u2022 10%) - Restorative Dentistry",
    "author": "Dr. Claire Aguiba Guerrero - Pineda",
    "keywords": [
      "tofflemire",
      "sectional matrix",
      "matrix band",
      "palodent",
      "garrison",
      "contact point",
      "proximal contour"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Matrix Systems for Class II Restorations:\n- Objectives: Restore anatomic proximal contour, re-establish tight physiologic proximal contact, prevent gingival overhang, confine restorative material during condensation.\n- Universal Tofflemire Matrix System:\n  * Components: Head with U-shaped guide slots, Slide, Rotating spindle, Outer nut (locks band in spindle), Inner nut (adjusts loop diameter).\n  * Placement: Slot open toward the GINGIVA (for removal occlusally); Contra-angle retainer placed lingually; Straight retainer placed buccally.\n  * Band thickness: 0.0015 inch (0.038 mm) regular; 0.0010 inch dead-soft ultra-thin.\n  * Best for AMALGAM restorations (allows firm lateral condensation).\n- Sectional Matrix Systems (e.g., Palodent, Garrison Composi-Tight):\n  * Kidney-shaped contoured sectional band + Separating ring (produces tooth separation to compensate for band thickness) + Anatomical wedge.\n  * Gold standard for POSTERIOR COMPOSITE restorations (guarantees tight proximal contacts).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nAmalgam can be firmly condensed laterally against a circumferential Tofflemire band, plastically stretching the metal into intimate contact with the adjacent tooth. Composite resin is a viscous liquid/paste that cannot be condensed to exert lateral pressure; placed in a circumferential band, composite inevitably produces open proximal contacts when the band is removed. Sectional matrix rings physically force adjacent teeth apart by 50-70 microns during placement; upon ring removal, natural periodontal ligament elastic rebound pulls the teeth together into a tight contact.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nWhy are Sectional Matrix rings superior for posterior composites? Because composite cannot be condensed to push the matrix band, sectional rings produce tooth separation to ensure tight proximal contact! Tofflemire guide slots always point GINGIVALLY.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* For COMPOSITE -> use SECTIONAL MATRIX + RING (prevents open contacts!).\n* For AMALGAM -> use TOFFLEMIRE matrix.\n* Tofflemire slot always points GINGIVALLY!",
    "summary": "Matrix Systems for Class II Restorations:\n- Objectives: Restore anatomic proximal contour, re-establish tight physiologic proximal contact, prevent gingival overhang, confine restorative material during condensation.\n- Universal Tofflemire Matrix System:\n  * Components: Head with U-shaped guide slots, Slide, Rotating spindle, Outer nut (locks band in spindle), Inner nut (adjusts loop diameter).\n  * Placement: Slot open toward the GINGIVA (for removal occlusally); Contra-angle retainer placed lingually; Straight retainer placed buccally.\n  * Band thickness: 0.0015 inch (0.038 mm) regular; 0.0010 inch dead-soft ultra-thin.\n  * Best for AMALGAM restorations (allows firm lateral condensation).\n- Sectional Matrix Systems (e.g., Palodent, Garrison Composi-Tight):\n  * Kidney-shaped contoured sectional band + Separating ring (produces tooth separation to compensate for band thickness) + Anatomical wedge.\n  * Gold standard for POSTERIOR COMPOSITE restorations (guarantees tight proximal contacts).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nAmalgam can be firmly condensed laterally against a circumferential Tofflemire band, plastically stretching the metal into intimate contact with the adjacent tooth. Composite resin is a viscous liquid/paste that cannot be condensed to exert lateral pressure; placed in a circumferential band, composite inevitably produces open proximal contacts when the band is removed. Sectional matrix rings physically force adjacent teeth apart by 50-70 microns during placement; upon ring removal, natural periodontal ligament elastic rebound pulls the teeth together into a tight contact.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nWhy are Sectional Matrix rings superior for posterior composites? Because composite cannot be condensed to push the matrix band, sectional rings produce tooth separation to ensure tight proximal contact! Tofflemire guide slots always point GINGIVALLY.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* For COMPOSITE -> use SECTIONAL MATRIX + RING (prevents open contacts!).\n* For AMALGAM -> use TOFFLEMIRE matrix.\n* Tofflemire slot always points GINGIVALLY!",
    "image": "/book/page_149.webp"
