    "originalPrint": "Developmental Soft Tissue Anomalies:\n- Fordyce Granules:\n  * Ectopic sebaceous glands occurring in the oral mucosa (buccal mucosa and vermilion of upper lip); Present in >80% of adults; Multiple asymptomatic tiny yellow-white papules; Normal anatomical variation, requires no treatment.\n- Leukoedema:\n  * Generalized milky, grayish-white opalescence of the buccal mucosa; Common in African-American individuals; Disappears or fades significantly when the cheek is stretched; Normal variation.\n- Geographic Tongue (Benign Migratory Glossitis / Erythema Migrans):\n  * Multiple well-demarcated areas of erythema (atrophy of FILIFORM papillae) bordered by a raised, yellowish-white circinate margin; Changes pattern over time ('wandering'); Asymptomatic or burning with spicy foods; Often associated with Fissured Tongue.\n- Lingual Thyroid: Nodule of ectopic thyroid tissue on dorsum of posterior tongue at foramen cecum due to failure of thyroglossal duct migration.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nLeukoedema represents intracellular edema of the spinous epithelial layer with prominent fluid-filled vacuolated cells. The stretching test is pathognomonic: physically elongating the mucosal tissue thins the intracellular fluid layer, immediately dissipating the grayish opacity. Geographic tongue involves intermittent neutrophilic exocytosis (Munro microabscesses) localized within the superficial epithelial layers, selectively shedding filiform papillae while leaving fungiform papillae intact as red dots.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nPathognomonic test for Leukoedema: The white opalescence DISAPPEARS WHEN THE CHEEK IS STRETCHED! Geographic tongue involves loss / atrophy of FILIFORM PAPILLAE. Fordyce granules = ectopic sebaceous glands (yellowish papules on buccal mucosa).",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Leukoedema = disappears when stretched!\n* Geographic tongue = loss of FILIFORM papillae (wandering red patches).\n* Fordyce granules = normal ectopic oil glands (yellow dots on cheeks) -> DO NOT TREAT!",
    "summary": "Developmental Soft Tissue Anomalies:\n- Fordyce Granules:\n  * Ectopic sebaceous glands occurring in the oral mucosa (buccal mucosa and vermilion of upper lip); Present in >80% of adults; Multiple asymptomatic tiny yellow-white papules; Normal anatomical variation, requires no treatment.\n- Leukoedema:\n  * Generalized milky, grayish-white opalescence of the buccal mucosa; Common in African-American individuals; Disappears or fades significantly when the cheek is stretched; Normal variation.\n- Geographic Tongue (Benign Migratory Glossitis / Erythema Migrans):\n  * Multiple well-demarcated areas of erythema (atrophy of FILIFORM papillae) bordered by a raised, yellowish-white circinate margin; Changes pattern over time ('wandering'); Asymptomatic or burning with spicy foods; Often associated with Fissured Tongue.\n- Lingual Thyroid: Nodule of ectopic thyroid tissue on dorsum of posterior tongue at foramen cecum due to failure of thyroglossal duct migration.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nLeukoedema represents intracellular edema of the spinous epithelial layer with prominent fluid-filled vacuolated cells. The stretching test is pathognomonic: physically elongating the mucosal tissue thins the intracellular fluid layer, immediately dissipating the grayish opacity. Geographic tongue involves intermittent neutrophilic exocytosis (Munro microabscesses) localized within the superficial epithelial layers, selectively shedding filiform papillae while leaving fungiform papillae intact as red dots.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nPathognomonic test for Leukoedema: The white opalescence DISAPPEARS WHEN THE CHEEK IS STRETCHED! Geographic tongue involves loss / atrophy of FILIFORM PAPILLAE. Fordyce granules = ectopic sebaceous glands (yellowish papules on buccal mucosa).\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Leukoedema = disappears when stretched!\n* Geographic tongue = loss of FILIFORM papillae (wandering red patches).\n* Fordyce granules = normal ectopic oil glands (yellow dots on cheeks) -> DO NOT TREAT!",
