    "originalPrint": "Benign Odontogenic Tumors (Epithelial Origin):\n- Ameloblastoma:\n  * Most common clinically significant odontogenic tumor; Benign but locally aggressive and destructive; High recurrence if treated by simple curettage.\n  * Site: Mandibular molar-ramus region (80-85%).\n  * Radiograph: Multilocular 'SOAP-BUBBLE' or 'HONEYCOMB' radiolucency; Cortical expansion, root resorption, tooth displacement.\n  * Histopathology: Follicular vs Plexiform; Peripheral palisaded columnar ameloblast-like cells showing REVERSE POLARITY of nuclei (nuclei polarized away from basement membrane) with central stellate reticulum-like cells.\n- Calcifying Epithelial Odontogenic Tumor (CEOT / Pindborg Tumor):\n  * Polyhedral epithelial cells with nuclear pleomorphism, eosinophilic AMYLOID-LIKE material (stains with Congo Red, apple-green birefringence), and concentric calcified LIESEGANG RINGS.\n- Adenomatoid Odontogenic Tumor (AOT):\n  * 'Two-Thirds Tumor': 2/3 in young females, 2/3 in anterior maxilla, 2/3 associated with unerupted canine; Duct-like tubular structures surrounded by whorled polyhedral cells; Perfectly benign, does not recur.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nAmeloblastoma replicates the embryonic enamel organ structure without depositing enamel matrix. The peripheral columnar cells exhibit reverse nuclear polarity (subnuclear vacuole pushing the nucleus to the cell pole opposite the basement membrane), mimicking pre-ameloblasts during tooth development. The lack of a true capsule enables neoplastic islands to infiltrate marrow spaces 2-3mm beyond apparent radiographic margins, necessitating segmental resection with 1cm clear bony margins.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nHistopathology of Ameloblastoma: Columnar cells with reverse nuclear polarity and stellate reticulum centers! Pindborg tumor (CEOT) pathognomonic feature: LIESEGANG RINGS and amyloid-like material! AOT: 'Two-thirds tumor' in anterior maxilla around impacted canines.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Ameloblastoma = 'SOAP BUBBLE' / 'HONEYCOMB' radiolucency, reverse nuclear polarity!\n* Pindborg Tumor (CEOT) = LIESEGANG RINGS + Amyloid!\n* AOT = 'Two-Thirds Tumor' (females, front teeth, canine, does not recur!).",
    "summary": "Benign Odontogenic Tumors (Epithelial Origin):\n- Ameloblastoma:\n  * Most common clinically significant odontogenic tumor; Benign but locally aggressive and destructive; High recurrence if treated by simple curettage.\n  * Site: Mandibular molar-ramus region (80-85%).\n  * Radiograph: Multilocular 'SOAP-BUBBLE' or 'HONEYCOMB' radiolucency; Cortical expansion, root resorption, tooth displacement.\n  * Histopathology: Follicular vs Plexiform; Peripheral palisaded columnar ameloblast-like cells showing REVERSE POLARITY of nuclei (nuclei polarized away from basement membrane) with central stellate reticulum-like cells.\n- Calcifying Epithelial Odontogenic Tumor (CEOT / Pindborg Tumor):\n  * Polyhedral epithelial cells with nuclear pleomorphism, eosinophilic AMYLOID-LIKE material (stains with Congo Red, apple-green birefringence), and concentric calcified LIESEGANG RINGS.\n- Adenomatoid Odontogenic Tumor (AOT):\n  * 'Two-Thirds Tumor': 2/3 in young females, 2/3 in anterior maxilla, 2/3 associated with unerupted canine; Duct-like tubular structures surrounded by whorled polyhedral cells; Perfectly benign, does not recur.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nAmeloblastoma replicates the embryonic enamel organ structure without depositing enamel matrix. The peripheral columnar cells exhibit reverse nuclear polarity (subnuclear vacuole pushing the nucleus to the cell pole opposite the basement membrane), mimicking pre-ameloblasts during tooth development. The lack of a true capsule enables neoplastic islands to infiltrate marrow spaces 2-3mm beyond apparent radiographic margins, necessitating segmental resection with 1cm clear bony margins.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nHistopathology of Ameloblastoma: Columnar cells with reverse nuclear polarity and stellate reticulum centers! Pindborg tumor (CEOT) pathognomonic feature: LIESEGANG RINGS and amyloid-like material! AOT: 'Two-thirds tumor' in anterior maxilla around impacted canines.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Ameloblastoma = 'SOAP BUBBLE' / 'HONEYCOMB' radiolucency, reverse nuclear polarity!\n* Pindborg Tumor (CEOT) = LIESEGANG RINGS + Amyloid!\n* AOT = 'Two-Thirds Tumor' (females, front teeth, canine, does not recur!).",
