    "originalPrint": "Benign Non-Odontogenic Bone Lesions:\n- Torus Palatinus & Torus Mandibularis:\n  * Exostoses (benign bony outgrowths); Non-neoplastic.\n  * Torus Palatinus: Midline of the hard palate; 20-25% of population; More common in females.\n  * Torus Mandibularis: Lingual surface of mandible in premolar/canine region above mylohyoid line; Bilateral in >90% of cases.\n  * Surgical removal indicated only when interfering with speech, swallowing, or complete/partial denture fabrication.\n- Osteoma:\n  * Benign neoplasm composed of mature compact (ivory) or cancellous bone.\n- Gardner's Syndrome (Autosomal Dominant - APC gene):\n  * Multiple Osteomas of the skull and jaws (mandible angle).\n  * Multiple Gastrointestinal Adenomatous Polyps (100% risk of malignant transformation into colorectal adenocarcinoma by age 40!).\n  * Multiple impacted supernumerary teeth and odontomas.\n  * Epidermoid cysts and desmoid tumors of skin.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nOral manifestations of Gardner's syndrome precede gastrointestinal symptoms by over a decade. Dentists who identify multiple osteomas on panoramic radiographs or observe multiple impacted supernumerary teeth and permanent tooth retention must urgently refer the patient for colonoscopy. Because colorectal polyps undergo 100% malignant degeneration into colorectal cancer, early recognition of these jaw signs is literally life-saving.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nGardner's Syndrome Tetrad: 1) Multiple Osteomas of the jaws; 2) Multiple impacted supernumerary teeth; 3) Epidermoid cysts; 4) Multiple Gastrointestinal Adenomatous Polyps (100% malignant conversion to colon cancer!). Torus mandibularis occurs on lingual premolar mandible bilaterally.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Gardner's Syndrome = Jaw osteomas + impacted supernumerary teeth + COLON POLYPS (turn into cancer!).\n* Torus palatinus = hard palate midline; Torus mandibularis = lingual premolars bilaterally.\n* Remove tori only if making a denture!",
    "summary": "Benign Non-Odontogenic Bone Lesions:\n- Torus Palatinus & Torus Mandibularis:\n  * Exostoses (benign bony outgrowths); Non-neoplastic.\n  * Torus Palatinus: Midline of the hard palate; 20-25% of population; More common in females.\n  * Torus Mandibularis: Lingual surface of mandible in premolar/canine region above mylohyoid line; Bilateral in >90% of cases.\n  * Surgical removal indicated only when interfering with speech, swallowing, or complete/partial denture fabrication.\n- Osteoma:\n  * Benign neoplasm composed of mature compact (ivory) or cancellous bone.\n- Gardner's Syndrome (Autosomal Dominant - APC gene):\n  * Multiple Osteomas of the skull and jaws (mandible angle).\n  * Multiple Gastrointestinal Adenomatous Polyps (100% risk of malignant transformation into colorectal adenocarcinoma by age 40!).\n  * Multiple impacted supernumerary teeth and odontomas.\n  * Epidermoid cysts and desmoid tumors of skin.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nOral manifestations of Gardner's syndrome precede gastrointestinal symptoms by over a decade. Dentists who identify multiple osteomas on panoramic radiographs or observe multiple impacted supernumerary teeth and permanent tooth retention must urgently refer the patient for colonoscopy. Because colorectal polyps undergo 100% malignant degeneration into colorectal cancer, early recognition of these jaw signs is literally life-saving.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nGardner's Syndrome Tetrad: 1) Multiple Osteomas of the jaws; 2) Multiple impacted supernumerary teeth; 3) Epidermoid cysts; 4) Multiple Gastrointestinal Adenomatous Polyps (100% malignant conversion to colon cancer!). Torus mandibularis occurs on lingual premolar mandible bilaterally.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Gardner's Syndrome = Jaw osteomas + impacted supernumerary teeth + COLON POLYPS (turn into cancer!).\n* Torus palatinus = hard palate midline; Torus mandibularis = lingual premolars bilaterally.\n* Remove tori only if making a denture!",
