    "originalPrint": "Giant Cell Lesions of the Jaws:\n- Central Giant Cell Granuloma (CGCG):\n  * Non-neoplastic, osteolytic lesion; More common in females under 30 years; Predilection for ANTERIOR MANDIBLE (frequently crosses the midline).\n  * Radiograph: Unilocular or multilocular radiolucency with scalloped borders.\n  * Histology: Abundant multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells distributed within a background of spindle-shaped stromal cells with prominent hemorrhage and hemosiderin.\n- Brown Tumor of Hyperparathyroidism:\n  * Histopathologically IDENTICAL to CGCG; Caused by excessive parathyroid hormone (PTH) stimulating generalized osteoclastic bone resorption; Brown color due to hemosiderin deposition.\n  * Must order serum Calcium, Phosphate, and PTH to rule out hyperparathyroidism when CGCG is diagnosed!\n- Cherubism (Autosomal Dominant - SH3BP2 gene):\n  * Bilateral, symmetric multilocular expansion of mandibular angles and maxilla in early childhood (ages 2-7); Produces chubby cheeks and 'EYES TOWARD HEAVEN' gaze due to upward displacement of orbital floor; Regresses spontaneously after puberty.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nUnder high circulating PTH in primary hyperparathyroidism (parathyroid adenoma) or secondary hyperparathyroidism (end-stage renal disease), osteoclasts resorb bone rapidly, creating vascular lytic bone cavities filled with extravasated red blood cells. Breakdown of hemoglobin into brown hemosiderin pigment gives the lesion its 'brown tumor' name. Cherubism's upward gaze is caused by symmetric expansion of orbital floors stretching the lower eyelids downward.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nWhen CGCG is diagnosed on biopsy, what laboratory tests must be ordered? Serum Calcium, Phosphate, and Parathyroid Hormone (PTH) to rule out Hyperparathyroidism (Brown Tumor)! Cherubism produces bilateral mandibular swelling with 'eyes toward heaven' appearance.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Giant Cell Granuloma crosses the mandibular midline!\n* Always check CALCIUM & PTH to rule out Brown Tumor of Hyperparathyroidism!\n* Cherubism = bilateral chubby cheeks + 'eyes toward heaven' gaze (shrinks after puberty).",
    "summary": "Giant Cell Lesions of the Jaws:\n- Central Giant Cell Granuloma (CGCG):\n  * Non-neoplastic, osteolytic lesion; More common in females under 30 years; Predilection for ANTERIOR MANDIBLE (frequently crosses the midline).\n  * Radiograph: Unilocular or multilocular radiolucency with scalloped borders.\n  * Histology: Abundant multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells distributed within a background of spindle-shaped stromal cells with prominent hemorrhage and hemosiderin.\n- Brown Tumor of Hyperparathyroidism:\n  * Histopathologically IDENTICAL to CGCG; Caused by excessive parathyroid hormone (PTH) stimulating generalized osteoclastic bone resorption; Brown color due to hemosiderin deposition.\n  * Must order serum Calcium, Phosphate, and PTH to rule out hyperparathyroidism when CGCG is diagnosed!\n- Cherubism (Autosomal Dominant - SH3BP2 gene):\n  * Bilateral, symmetric multilocular expansion of mandibular angles and maxilla in early childhood (ages 2-7); Produces chubby cheeks and 'EYES TOWARD HEAVEN' gaze due to upward displacement of orbital floor; Regresses spontaneously after puberty.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nUnder high circulating PTH in primary hyperparathyroidism (parathyroid adenoma) or secondary hyperparathyroidism (end-stage renal disease), osteoclasts resorb bone rapidly, creating vascular lytic bone cavities filled with extravasated red blood cells. Breakdown of hemoglobin into brown hemosiderin pigment gives the lesion its 'brown tumor' name. Cherubism's upward gaze is caused by symmetric expansion of orbital floors stretching the lower eyelids downward.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nWhen CGCG is diagnosed on biopsy, what laboratory tests must be ordered? Serum Calcium, Phosphate, and Parathyroid Hormone (PTH) to rule out Hyperparathyroidism (Brown Tumor)! Cherubism produces bilateral mandibular swelling with 'eyes toward heaven' appearance.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Giant Cell Granuloma crosses the mandibular midline!\n* Always check CALCIUM & PTH to rule out Brown Tumor of Hyperparathyroidism!\n* Cherubism = bilateral chubby cheeks + 'eyes toward heaven' gaze (shrinks after puberty).",
