    "title": "Salivary Gland Tumors: Pleomorphic Adenoma & Warthin's Tumor",
    "subject": "Oral Pathology",
    "day": "Day 1 (11:00 AM \u2022 15%) - Oral Pathology",
    "author": "Dr. Pamela Marize Moreno",
    "keywords": [
      "pleomorphic adenoma",
      "warthins tumor",
      "parotid gland",
      "benign mixed tumor",
      "facial nerve",
      "bilayered oncocytic"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Salivary Gland Neoplasms (Benign):\n- General Rules: 80% of all salivary tumors occur in PAROTID GLAND; 80% of parotid tumors are Pleomorphic Adenomas; 80% of pleomorphic adenomas occur in the superficial lobe.\n- Pleomorphic Adenoma (Benign Mixed Tumor):\n  * The MOST COMMON salivary gland neoplasm (both major and minor glands).\n  * Site: Parotid gland (tail/superficial lobe); Minor glands: Palate (junction of hard and soft palate).\n  * Clinical: Slow-growing, painless, firm, movable, lobulated mass.\n  * Histology: 'Pleomorphic' / mixed appearance; Ductal epithelial and myoepithelial cells arranged in sheets, ducts, or cords within a mesenchymal-like background (chondroid, myxoid, hyaline stroma).\n  * Malignant potential: Can transform into Carcinoma Ex-Pleomorphic Adenoma if neglected for decades.\n- Warthin's Tumor (Papillary Cystadenoma Lymphomatosum):\n  * Second most common benign parotid tumor; Almost exclusively in PAROTID gland; Male predilection; Strongly associated with CIGARETTE SMOKING; Bilateral in 10-15%; Bilayered oncocytic epithelium lining cystic spaces surrounded by a dense lymphoid stroma with germinal centers.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nPleomorphic adenoma is termed 'mixed' because neoplastic myoepithelial cells modify their surrounding extracellular matrix into cartilage-like (chondroid), bone-like (osseous), or mucus-like (myxoid) tissue. Enucleation is contraindicated because tumor micro-extensions penetrate the thin pseudocapsule; simple shell-out leaves microscopic pseudopodia that recur multifocally. Superficial parotidectomy with facial nerve preservation is mandatory.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\n#1 most common salivary gland tumor overall: Pleomorphic Adenoma (Benign Mixed Tumor)! Most common minor salivary gland site: Posterior palate. Warthin's tumor: Strongly associated with SMOKING in older males, often bilateral, lymphoid stroma with oncocytic epithelium.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* #1 Salivary tumor = Pleomorphic Adenoma (superficial parotid, or palate for minor glands).\n* Warthin's Tumor = SMOKERS! (lymphoid stroma, can be on BOTH sides!).\n* Never pop it out (enucleate) -> will recur! Must remove with safe margin!",
    "summary": "Salivary Gland Neoplasms (Benign):\n- General Rules: 80% of all salivary tumors occur in PAROTID GLAND; 80% of parotid tumors are Pleomorphic Adenomas; 80% of pleomorphic adenomas occur in the superficial lobe.\n- Pleomorphic Adenoma (Benign Mixed Tumor):\n  * The MOST COMMON salivary gland neoplasm (both major and minor glands).\n  * Site: Parotid gland (tail/superficial lobe); Minor glands: Palate (junction of hard and soft palate).\n  * Clinical: Slow-growing, painless, firm, movable, lobulated mass.\n  * Histology: 'Pleomorphic' / mixed appearance; Ductal epithelial and myoepithelial cells arranged in sheets, ducts, or cords within a mesenchymal-like background (chondroid, myxoid, hyaline stroma).\n  * Malignant potential: Can transform into Carcinoma Ex-Pleomorphic Adenoma if neglected for decades.\n- Warthin's Tumor (Papillary Cystadenoma Lymphomatosum):\n  * Second most common benign parotid tumor; Almost exclusively in PAROTID gland; Male predilection; Strongly associated with CIGARETTE SMOKING; Bilateral in 10-15%; Bilayered oncocytic epithelium lining cystic spaces surrounded by a dense lymphoid stroma with germinal centers.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nPleomorphic adenoma is termed 'mixed' because neoplastic myoepithelial cells modify their surrounding extracellular matrix into cartilage-like (chondroid), bone-like (osseous), or mucus-like (myxoid) tissue. Enucleation is contraindicated because tumor micro-extensions penetrate the thin pseudocapsule; simple shell-out leaves microscopic pseudopodia that recur multifocally. Superficial parotidectomy with facial nerve preservation is mandatory.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\n#1 most common salivary gland tumor overall: Pleomorphic Adenoma (Benign Mixed Tumor)! Most common minor salivary gland site: Posterior palate. Warthin's tumor: Strongly associated with SMOKING in older males, often bilateral, lymphoid stroma with oncocytic epithelium.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* #1 Salivary tumor = Pleomorphic Adenoma (superficial parotid, or palate for minor glands).\n* Warthin's Tumor = SMOKERS! (lymphoid stroma, can be on BOTH sides!).\n* Never pop it out (enucleate) -> will recur! Must remove with safe margin!",
