    "originalPrint": "Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC):\n- Incidence: Accounts for >90% to 95% of all oral malignancies.\n- High-Risk Anatomic Locations:\n  1. Lateral border and ventral surface of the tongue (most common intraoral site in Western countries, ~50%).\n  2. Floor of the mouth (highest risk for second primary tumors).\n  3. Lower lip vermilion (sun / UV light exposure).\n  4. Soft palate and tonsillar pillars.\n- Major Risk Factors:\n  * Tobacco use (smoking and smokeless/snuff) - dose-response relationship.\n  * Alcohol consumption (synergistic effect with tobacco; alcohol acts as solvent increasing mucosal permeability to tobacco carcinogens).\n  * Betel Quid / Areca Nut chewing (major cause in South/Southeast Asia; produces Oral Submucous Fibrosis).\n  * High-Risk Human Papillomavirus (HPV 16 and 18): Predominantly oropharyngeal SCC (base of tongue, tonsils).\n- Clinical Presentation: Non-healing ulcer >2-3 weeks, exophytic mass with rolled everted borders, induration (firm, hard on palpation), fixation to underlying bone/muscle, unexplained cervical lymphadenopathy.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nEthanol itself is not a direct mutagen, but its primary metabolite acetaldehyde damages DNA repair mechanisms. Crucially, alcohol dissolves the lipid-rich permeability barrier of oral mucosal keratinocytes, allowing lipid-soluble tobacco carcinogens (nitrosamines, benzo[a]pyrene) to penetrate into basal stem cell nuclei. This synergistic interaction multiplies oral cancer risk up to 15-fold compared to either habit alone.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nAccounts for >90% of all oral cancers: Squamous Cell Carcinoma! Most common intraoral site: Lateral border and ventral surface of the tongue! Most dangerous synergistic combination: Tobacco + Alcohol.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* 90% of all oral cancers = Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC)!\n* Most common site: Side & belly of the TONGUE and floor of mouth!\n* Any ulcer not healing after 2 WEEKS must be BIOPSIED!",
    "summary": "Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC):\n- Incidence: Accounts for >90% to 95% of all oral malignancies.\n- High-Risk Anatomic Locations:\n  1. Lateral border and ventral surface of the tongue (most common intraoral site in Western countries, ~50%).\n  2. Floor of the mouth (highest risk for second primary tumors).\n  3. Lower lip vermilion (sun / UV light exposure).\n  4. Soft palate and tonsillar pillars.\n- Major Risk Factors:\n  * Tobacco use (smoking and smokeless/snuff) - dose-response relationship.\n  * Alcohol consumption (synergistic effect with tobacco; alcohol acts as solvent increasing mucosal permeability to tobacco carcinogens).\n  * Betel Quid / Areca Nut chewing (major cause in South/Southeast Asia; produces Oral Submucous Fibrosis).\n  * High-Risk Human Papillomavirus (HPV 16 and 18): Predominantly oropharyngeal SCC (base of tongue, tonsils).\n- Clinical Presentation: Non-healing ulcer >2-3 weeks, exophytic mass with rolled everted borders, induration (firm, hard on palpation), fixation to underlying bone/muscle, unexplained cervical lymphadenopathy.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nEthanol itself is not a direct mutagen, but its primary metabolite acetaldehyde damages DNA repair mechanisms. Crucially, alcohol dissolves the lipid-rich permeability barrier of oral mucosal keratinocytes, allowing lipid-soluble tobacco carcinogens (nitrosamines, benzo[a]pyrene) to penetrate into basal stem cell nuclei. This synergistic interaction multiplies oral cancer risk up to 15-fold compared to either habit alone.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nAccounts for >90% of all oral cancers: Squamous Cell Carcinoma! Most common intraoral site: Lateral border and ventral surface of the tongue! Most dangerous synergistic combination: Tobacco + Alcohol.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* 90% of all oral cancers = Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC)!\n* Most common site: Side & belly of the TONGUE and floor of mouth!\n* Any ulcer not healing after 2 WEEKS must be BIOPSIED!",
