    "originalPrint": "Malignant Bone Diseases of the Jaws:\n- Osteosarcoma (Osteogenic Sarcoma):\n  * The MOST COMMON primary malignant bone tumor in young adults (mean age 30-35 years in jaws, a decade older than long bones).\n  * Diagnostic Criterion: Direct production of malignant OSTEOID (bone matrix) by malignant mesenchymal osteoblasts.\n  * Symptoms: Rapidly enlarging painful swelling, tooth mobility, paresthesia of inferior alveolar nerve (numb chin syndrome).\n  * Radiographic Triad:\n    1. Symmetrical widening of the periodontal ligament (PDL) space around one or more teeth (GARRINGTON'S SIGN - earliest sign!).\n    2. Ill-defined mixed radiolucent-radiopaque lesion.\n    3. Classic 'SUNBURST' or 'sun-ray' periosteal reaction.\n- Multiple Myeloma:\n  * Malignant clonal proliferation of antibody-secreting PLASMA CELLS in bone marrow; Older adults (>60 years).\n  * Radiograph: Multiple, well-demarcated, uncorticated, 'PUNCHED-OUT' radiolucencies in skull, jaws, and ribs.\n  * Laboratory Findings: Monoclonal paraprotein spike (M-spike on serum electrophoresis), BENCE JONES PROTEINURIA (free immunoglobulin light chains in urine), hypercalcemia, anemia.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nAs malignant osteosarcoma cells proliferate along the root, they infiltrate the vascular PDL space before destroying adjacent mineralized cortical bone. This early tumor infiltration widens the periodontal ligament space uniformly (Garrington's sign); seeing a widened PDL around an otherwise intact tooth without trauma or infection warrants immediate biopsy. The 'sunburst' pattern represents perpendicular calcified spicules of bone forming along stretched periosteal Sharpey's fibers.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nEarliest radiographic sign of Osteosarcoma: Symmetrical widening of the PDL space (Garrington's sign)! Advanced sign: 'SUNBURST' periosteal reaction. Multiple Myeloma hallmarks: 'PUNCHED-OUT' radiolucencies and BENCE JONES PROTEINS in urine.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Osteosarcoma = 'SUNBURST' bone pattern + symmetric widening of PDL space!\n* Multiple Myeloma = 'PUNCHED-OUT' holes in jaw/skull + Bence Jones proteins in urine!\n* Plasma cell cancer.",
    "summary": "Malignant Bone Diseases of the Jaws:\n- Osteosarcoma (Osteogenic Sarcoma):\n  * The MOST COMMON primary malignant bone tumor in young adults (mean age 30-35 years in jaws, a decade older than long bones).\n  * Diagnostic Criterion: Direct production of malignant OSTEOID (bone matrix) by malignant mesenchymal osteoblasts.\n  * Symptoms: Rapidly enlarging painful swelling, tooth mobility, paresthesia of inferior alveolar nerve (numb chin syndrome).\n  * Radiographic Triad:\n    1. Symmetrical widening of the periodontal ligament (PDL) space around one or more teeth (GARRINGTON'S SIGN - earliest sign!).\n    2. Ill-defined mixed radiolucent-radiopaque lesion.\n    3. Classic 'SUNBURST' or 'sun-ray' periosteal reaction.\n- Multiple Myeloma:\n  * Malignant clonal proliferation of antibody-secreting PLASMA CELLS in bone marrow; Older adults (>60 years).\n  * Radiograph: Multiple, well-demarcated, uncorticated, 'PUNCHED-OUT' radiolucencies in skull, jaws, and ribs.\n  * Laboratory Findings: Monoclonal paraprotein spike (M-spike on serum electrophoresis), BENCE JONES PROTEINURIA (free immunoglobulin light chains in urine), hypercalcemia, anemia.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nAs malignant osteosarcoma cells proliferate along the root, they infiltrate the vascular PDL space before destroying adjacent mineralized cortical bone. This early tumor infiltration widens the periodontal ligament space uniformly (Garrington's sign); seeing a widened PDL around an otherwise intact tooth without trauma or infection warrants immediate biopsy. The 'sunburst' pattern represents perpendicular calcified spicules of bone forming along stretched periosteal Sharpey's fibers.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nEarliest radiographic sign of Osteosarcoma: Symmetrical widening of the PDL space (Garrington's sign)! Advanced sign: 'SUNBURST' periosteal reaction. Multiple Myeloma hallmarks: 'PUNCHED-OUT' radiolucencies and BENCE JONES PROTEINS in urine.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Osteosarcoma = 'SUNBURST' bone pattern + symmetric widening of PDL space!\n* Multiple Myeloma = 'PUNCHED-OUT' holes in jaw/skull + Bence Jones proteins in urine!\n* Plasma cell cancer.",
