    "originalPrint": "Systemic Manifestations in the Oral Cavity:\n- Hematologic Disorders:\n  * Acute Leukemia: Diffuse boggy gingival enlargement (leukemic infiltration), spontaneous gingival bleeding (thrombocytopenia), mucosal petechiae, pale oral tissues (anemia), neutropenic ulcers.\n  * Plummer-Vinson Syndrome (Paterson-Kelly): Iron deficiency anemia + Dysphagia (esophageal webs) + Atrophic glossitis (bald red tongue); Predisposes to oral and esophageal carcinoma!\n- Endocrine Disorders:\n  * Diabetes Mellitus: Rapidly progressive severe periodontitis, multiple periodontal abscesses, impaired wound healing, xerostomia, candidiasis.\n  * Addison's Disease (Adrenocortical insufficiency): Bronzing of skin and diffuse, patchy melanotic hyperpigmentation ('bronze-like / bluish-black macules') of buccal mucosa and gingiva due to elevated ACTH / MSH.\n- Nutritional Deficiencies:\n  * Vitamin C Deficiency (Scurvy): Scorbutic gingivitis (swollen, bleeding, ulcerated spongy gingiva), loss of teeth due to impaired collagen synthesis and defective PDL.\n  * Vitamin B12 / Folate Deficiency (Pernicious Anemia): Hunter's glossitis (painful, beefy red, smooth atrophic tongue).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nIn Addison's disease, primary adrenal destruction drops circulating cortisol. The pituitary gland attempts compensation by hypersecreting Pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC), the common precursor for both Adrenocorticotropic Hormone (ACTH) and Melanocyte-Stimulating Hormone (MSH). Elevated circulating MSH stimulates basal melanocytes in oral mucosa to produce excess melanin, creating characteristic patchy bronze-brown macules on buccal mucosa.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nTriad of Plummer-Vinson Syndrome: 1) Iron deficiency anemia; 2) Dysphagia (esophageal webs); 3) Atrophic glossitis (high oral cancer risk!). Addison's disease oral feature: Bronze / diffuse brown mucosal hyperpigmentation from elevated ACTH.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Plummer-Vinson = Iron deficiency + Esophageal webs (swallowing difficulty) + Bald tongue (CANCER RISK!).\n* Addison's Disease = BRONZE skin & brown patches in mouth (high ACTH).\n* Scurvy = Vitamin C deficiency (spongy, bleeding gingiva).",
    "summary": "Systemic Manifestations in the Oral Cavity:\n- Hematologic Disorders:\n  * Acute Leukemia: Diffuse boggy gingival enlargement (leukemic infiltration), spontaneous gingival bleeding (thrombocytopenia), mucosal petechiae, pale oral tissues (anemia), neutropenic ulcers.\n  * Plummer-Vinson Syndrome (Paterson-Kelly): Iron deficiency anemia + Dysphagia (esophageal webs) + Atrophic glossitis (bald red tongue); Predisposes to oral and esophageal carcinoma!\n- Endocrine Disorders:\n  * Diabetes Mellitus: Rapidly progressive severe periodontitis, multiple periodontal abscesses, impaired wound healing, xerostomia, candidiasis.\n  * Addison's Disease (Adrenocortical insufficiency): Bronzing of skin and diffuse, patchy melanotic hyperpigmentation ('bronze-like / bluish-black macules') of buccal mucosa and gingiva due to elevated ACTH / MSH.\n- Nutritional Deficiencies:\n  * Vitamin C Deficiency (Scurvy): Scorbutic gingivitis (swollen, bleeding, ulcerated spongy gingiva), loss of teeth due to impaired collagen synthesis and defective PDL.\n  * Vitamin B12 / Folate Deficiency (Pernicious Anemia): Hunter's glossitis (painful, beefy red, smooth atrophic tongue).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nIn Addison's disease, primary adrenal destruction drops circulating cortisol. The pituitary gland attempts compensation by hypersecreting Pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC), the common precursor for both Adrenocorticotropic Hormone (ACTH) and Melanocyte-Stimulating Hormone (MSH). Elevated circulating MSH stimulates basal melanocytes in oral mucosa to produce excess melanin, creating characteristic patchy bronze-brown macules on buccal mucosa.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nTriad of Plummer-Vinson Syndrome: 1) Iron deficiency anemia; 2) Dysphagia (esophageal webs); 3) Atrophic glossitis (high oral cancer risk!). Addison's disease oral feature: Bronze / diffuse brown mucosal hyperpigmentation from elevated ACTH.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Plummer-Vinson = Iron deficiency + Esophageal webs (swallowing difficulty) + Bald tongue (CANCER RISK!).\n* Addison's Disease = BRONZE skin & brown patches in mouth (high ACTH).\n* Scurvy = Vitamin C deficiency (spongy, bleeding gingiva).",
