    "originalPrint": "DMF Dental Training Center Cover:\nOral Radiology - By: Dr. Mickhail Q. Francisco\nShort History of Dental Radiography:\n- 1895: Wilhelm Conrad ROENTGEN discovered X-rays (received first Nobel Prize in Physics; 'Father of X-rays').\n- 1896: Otto WALKHOFF took the first dental radiograph in human history (placed small glass photographic plate in his own mouth, 25-minute exposure).\n- 1896: William J. MORTON took the first dental radiograph in the United States on a skull.\n- 1896: C. Edmund KELLS took the first dental radiograph in the US on a living patient (advocated clinical use; lost fingers/arm to radiation necrosis).\n- 1901: William H. ROLLINS published the first paper warning of radiation dangers and designed the first dental X-ray unit.\n- 1904: Weston A. PRICE introduced the Bisecting Technique.\n- 1913: William D. COOLIDGE invented the hot-cathode tungsten filament X-ray tube (foundation of modern X-ray tubes).\n- 1920: Eastman KODAK Company introduced the first pre-wrapped commercial dental film packets.\n- 1925: Howard Riley RAPER introduced the Bitewing technique.\n- 1947: Gordon M. FITZGERALD introduced the Long-Cone Paralleling Technique ('Father of modern dental radiography').",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nEarly radiography required dangerous exposure times (25 minutes) because low-intensity vacuum tubes lacked thermionic electron emission. Coolidge's 1913 tungsten filament revolutionized radiology by allowing independent control of tube current (mA, thermionic emission rate) and tube voltage (kVp, electron kinetic energy), dramatically slashing exposure duration and patient radiation dose.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nHistorical milestones must know: Discovered X-rays (1895): Wilhelm Conrad Roentgen. First dental radiograph (1896): Otto Walkhoff. Father of Bitewings (1925): Howard Riley Raper. Father of Long-Cone Paralleling Technique (1947): Gordon Fitzgerald.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Roentgen = 1895 discovered X-rays (Father of X-rays)!\n* Walkhoff = first dental X-ray (on himself, took 25 mins!).\n* Raper = Bitewing technique!\n* Fitzgerald = Long-cone paralleling technique!",
    "summary": "DMF Dental Training Center Cover:\nOral Radiology - By: Dr. Mickhail Q. Francisco\nShort History of Dental Radiography:\n- 1895: Wilhelm Conrad ROENTGEN discovered X-rays (received first Nobel Prize in Physics; 'Father of X-rays').\n- 1896: Otto WALKHOFF took the first dental radiograph in human history (placed small glass photographic plate in his own mouth, 25-minute exposure).\n- 1896: William J. MORTON took the first dental radiograph in the United States on a skull.\n- 1896: C. Edmund KELLS took the first dental radiograph in the US on a living patient (advocated clinical use; lost fingers/arm to radiation necrosis).\n- 1901: William H. ROLLINS published the first paper warning of radiation dangers and designed the first dental X-ray unit.\n- 1904: Weston A. PRICE introduced the Bisecting Technique.\n- 1913: William D. COOLIDGE invented the hot-cathode tungsten filament X-ray tube (foundation of modern X-ray tubes).\n- 1920: Eastman KODAK Company introduced the first pre-wrapped commercial dental film packets.\n- 1925: Howard Riley RAPER introduced the Bitewing technique.\n- 1947: Gordon M. FITZGERALD introduced the Long-Cone Paralleling Technique ('Father of modern dental radiography').\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nEarly radiography required dangerous exposure times (25 minutes) because low-intensity vacuum tubes lacked thermionic electron emission. Coolidge's 1913 tungsten filament revolutionized radiology by allowing independent control of tube current (mA, thermionic emission rate) and tube voltage (kVp, electron kinetic energy), dramatically slashing exposure duration and patient radiation dose.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nHistorical milestones must know: Discovered X-rays (1895): Wilhelm Conrad Roentgen. First dental radiograph (1896): Otto Walkhoff. Father of Bitewings (1925): Howard Riley Raper. Father of Long-Cone Paralleling Technique (1947): Gordon Fitzgerald.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Roentgen = 1895 discovered X-rays (Father of X-rays)!\n* Walkhoff = first dental X-ray (on himself, took 25 mins!).\n* Raper = Bitewing technique!\n* Fitzgerald = Long-cone paralleling technique!",
