    "originalPrint": "Dental X-Ray Controls & Image Characteristics:\n- Milliamperage (mA):\n  * Controls the QUANTITY (intensity) of electrons emitted by thermionic emission at the cathode.\n  * Increasing mA increases the total number of photons produced without changing their energy; Directly controls RADIOGRAPHIC DENSITY (overall blackness of the film).\n- Exposure Time (Seconds / Impulses):\n  * 1 impulse = 1/60th of a second (in 60 Hz AC current).\n  * Combined Parameter: Milliampere-Seconds (mAs = mA x time); Doubling mA and halving time produces identical radiographic density ('Reciprocity Law').\n- Kilovoltage Peak (kVp):\n  * Controls the QUALITY (penetrating power and kinetic speed) of the electrons moving from cathode to anode.\n  * Higher kVp produces shorter wavelengths with higher penetrating energy.\n  * Affects BOTH density and RADIOGRAPHIC CONTRAST:\n    - Low kVp (65-70 kVp): High contrast / Short-scale contrast (crisp black and white, few gray shades; ideal for CARIES detection).\n    - High kVp (80-90 kVp): Low contrast / Long-scale contrast (many shades of gray; ideal for PERIODONTAL bone level assessment).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nKilovoltage dictates photon wavelength (wavelength = 1.24 / keV in nm). High kVp produces penetrating photons that traverse soft tissue, enamel, and cortical bone with low differential attenuation, casting many shades of gray (long-scale contrast, low subject contrast). Low kVp produces less penetrating photons that are absorbed completely by bone but penetrate demineralized areas cleanly, creating stark black-and-white demarcation (short-scale contrast), which is visually ideal for identifying subtle proximal caries triangles.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nkVp controls beam QUALITY (penetrating power) and CONTRAST! mA and exposure time (mAs) control beam QUANTITY and DENSITY (overall blackness). For caries detection: Low kVp (high contrast / short scale). For periodontal bone: High kVp (low contrast / long scale).",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* kVp = QUALITY / penetrating power & CONTRAST!\n* mA x seconds (mAs) = QUANTITY / overall blackness (DENSITY)!\n* Caries check = Low kVp (crisp black & white contrast).\n* Perio bone check = High kVp (lots of gray shades).",
    "summary": "Dental X-Ray Controls & Image Characteristics:\n- Milliamperage (mA):\n  * Controls the QUANTITY (intensity) of electrons emitted by thermionic emission at the cathode.\n  * Increasing mA increases the total number of photons produced without changing their energy; Directly controls RADIOGRAPHIC DENSITY (overall blackness of the film).\n- Exposure Time (Seconds / Impulses):\n  * 1 impulse = 1/60th of a second (in 60 Hz AC current).\n  * Combined Parameter: Milliampere-Seconds (mAs = mA x time); Doubling mA and halving time produces identical radiographic density ('Reciprocity Law').\n- Kilovoltage Peak (kVp):\n  * Controls the QUALITY (penetrating power and kinetic speed) of the electrons moving from cathode to anode.\n  * Higher kVp produces shorter wavelengths with higher penetrating energy.\n  * Affects BOTH density and RADIOGRAPHIC CONTRAST:\n    - Low kVp (65-70 kVp): High contrast / Short-scale contrast (crisp black and white, few gray shades; ideal for CARIES detection).\n    - High kVp (80-90 kVp): Low contrast / Long-scale contrast (many shades of gray; ideal for PERIODONTAL bone level assessment).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nKilovoltage dictates photon wavelength (wavelength = 1.24 / keV in nm). High kVp produces penetrating photons that traverse soft tissue, enamel, and cortical bone with low differential attenuation, casting many shades of gray (long-scale contrast, low subject contrast). Low kVp produces less penetrating photons that are absorbed completely by bone but penetrate demineralized areas cleanly, creating stark black-and-white demarcation (short-scale contrast), which is visually ideal for identifying subtle proximal caries triangles.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nkVp controls beam QUALITY (penetrating power) and CONTRAST! mA and exposure time (mAs) control beam QUANTITY and DENSITY (overall blackness). For caries detection: Low kVp (high contrast / short scale). For periodontal bone: High kVp (low contrast / long scale).\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* kVp = QUALITY / penetrating power & CONTRAST!\n* mA x seconds (mAs) = QUANTITY / overall blackness (DENSITY)!\n* Caries check = Low kVp (crisp black & white contrast).\n* Perio bone check = High kVp (lots of gray shades).",
