    "originalPrint": "Dental Film Processing Chemistry:\n- Processing Sequence: Developer -> Rinse (manual only) -> Fixer -> Wash -> Dry.\n- Developer Solution Chemistry (Converts latent image into visible black metallic silver):\n  1. Developing Agents (Reducing Agents):\n     * Hydroquinone: Generates black tones, sharp CONTRAST; Highly temperature-sensitive (optimum 68\u00b0F / 20\u00b0C).\n     * Elon (Metol) / Phenidone: Generates gray tones, rapid action, density.\n  2. Preservative: Sodium Sulfite (prevents oxidation of developing agents by air).\n  3. Accelerator / Activator: Sodium Carbonate (creates alkaline pH ~10 to swell and soften gelatin emulsion).\n  4. Restrainer: Potassium Bromide (prevents developer from reducing unexposed crystals; prevents chemical fog).\n- Fixer Solution Chemistry (Removes unexposed silver halide crystals & hardens emulsion):\n  1. Fixing Agent (Clearing Agent): Sodium Thiosulfate / Ammonium Thiosulfate ('Hypo'; dissolves unexposed, unenergized silver halide crystals, making white areas transparent).\n  2. Acidifier: Acetic Acid (neutralizes alkaline developer, stops development).\n  3. Hardener: Potassium Alum / Aluminum Chloride (hardens and shrinks soft gelatin).\n  4. Preservative: Sodium Sulfite.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nDeveloper is a selective chemical reducing agent that donates electrons exclusively to silver halide crystals containing metallic silver sensitivity specks, multiplying them a billion-fold into opaque black silver grains. Fixer chemically removes unexposed, unenergized silver halide crystals by forming soluble silver-thiosulfate complexes washed away in water; if fixing is omitted, remaining photosensitive silver halide turns brown-black when exposed to ambient room light, ruining the radiograph.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nDeveloper reducing agents: Hydroquinone (contrast, black tones) and Elon (gray tones, speed). Fixer clearing agent: Sodium/Ammonium Thiosulfate (removes unexposed crystals). Optimum manual processing temperature: 68\u00b0F (20\u00b0C) for 5 minutes in developer.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Developer = Hydroquinone (black tones/contrast) + Elon (gray shades). Alkaline pH 10!\n* Fixer = Sodium Thiosulfate ('Hypo' clears unexposed crystals). Acidic pH!\n* Ideal manual developing time/temp = 5 minutes at 68\u00b0F (20\u00b0C)!",
    "summary": "Dental Film Processing Chemistry:\n- Processing Sequence: Developer -> Rinse (manual only) -> Fixer -> Wash -> Dry.\n- Developer Solution Chemistry (Converts latent image into visible black metallic silver):\n  1. Developing Agents (Reducing Agents):\n     * Hydroquinone: Generates black tones, sharp CONTRAST; Highly temperature-sensitive (optimum 68\u00b0F / 20\u00b0C).\n     * Elon (Metol) / Phenidone: Generates gray tones, rapid action, density.\n  2. Preservative: Sodium Sulfite (prevents oxidation of developing agents by air).\n  3. Accelerator / Activator: Sodium Carbonate (creates alkaline pH ~10 to swell and soften gelatin emulsion).\n  4. Restrainer: Potassium Bromide (prevents developer from reducing unexposed crystals; prevents chemical fog).\n- Fixer Solution Chemistry (Removes unexposed silver halide crystals & hardens emulsion):\n  1. Fixing Agent (Clearing Agent): Sodium Thiosulfate / Ammonium Thiosulfate ('Hypo'; dissolves unexposed, unenergized silver halide crystals, making white areas transparent).\n  2. Acidifier: Acetic Acid (neutralizes alkaline developer, stops development).\n  3. Hardener: Potassium Alum / Aluminum Chloride (hardens and shrinks soft gelatin).\n  4. Preservative: Sodium Sulfite.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nDeveloper is a selective chemical reducing agent that donates electrons exclusively to silver halide crystals containing metallic silver sensitivity specks, multiplying them a billion-fold into opaque black silver grains. Fixer chemically removes unexposed, unenergized silver halide crystals by forming soluble silver-thiosulfate complexes washed away in water; if fixing is omitted, remaining photosensitive silver halide turns brown-black when exposed to ambient room light, ruining the radiograph.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nDeveloper reducing agents: Hydroquinone (contrast, black tones) and Elon (gray tones, speed). Fixer clearing agent: Sodium/Ammonium Thiosulfate (removes unexposed crystals). Optimum manual processing temperature: 68\u00b0F (20\u00b0C) for 5 minutes in developer.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Developer = Hydroquinone (black tones/contrast) + Elon (gray shades). Alkaline pH 10!\n* Fixer = Sodium Thiosulfate ('Hypo' clears unexposed crystals). Acidic pH!\n* Ideal manual developing time/temp = 5 minutes at 68\u00b0F (20\u00b0C)!",
