    "title": "Radiographic Interpretation: Caries Detection & Periodontal Bone Loss",
    "subject": "Oral Radiology",
    "day": "Day 2 (11:00 AM \u2022 10%) - Roentgenology",
    "author": "Dr. Mickhail Q. Francisco",
    "keywords": [
      "caries detection",
      "cervical burnout",
      "incipient caries",
      "horizontal bone loss",
      "vertical bone loss",
      "furcation classes"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Radiographic Caries & Periodontal Assessment:\n- Dental Caries Radiographic Appearance:\n  * Enamel Caries: Proximal caries appears as an INVERTED TRIANGLE with its BASE at the enamel outer surface and APEX pointing toward the DEJ.\n  * Dentinal Caries: Once caries crosses the DEJ, it spreads laterally along the junction and forms a second triangle with its BASE at the DEJ and APEX toward the pulp.\n  * Caries requires at least 30% to 40% demineralization before becoming visible radiographically (clinical lesion is always deeper than radiographic shadow!).\n  * Cervical Burnout: An optical illusion / artifact appearing as a collar-shaped radiolucency at the cervical neck of teeth between enamel cap and alveolar bone; Due to anatomical decrease in tooth thickness.\n- Periodontal Bone Loss Assessment:\n  * Normal Bone Level: Alveolar crest lies 1.5 to 2.0 mm apical to the CEJ.\n  * Horizontal Bone Loss: Bone resorption parallel to an imaginary line connecting CEJs of adjacent teeth.\n  * Vertical (Angular) Bone Loss: Infrabony pocket; Bone level is angled/oblique relative to the CEJ line.\n  * Furcation Involvement: Class I (incipient), Class II (cul-de-sac), Class III ('through-and-through' bone loss), Class IV (through-and-through with clinically visible recession).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nCervical burnout occurs because the tooth neck has a cylindrical, narrow cross-section and lacks both the thick enamel crown cap and the surrounding alveolar bone sheath. More X-ray photons pass through this thin cervical zone unhindered, darkening the film. Clinicians distinguish burnout from true root caries because burnout displays an intact root outline with no cavitation, fading gradually toward the pulp chamber without breaking the root surface.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nDemineralization required for radiographic visibility: At least 30-40% mineral loss! Cervical burnout is an optical illusion at the neck of the tooth. Normal alveolar bone crest is 1.5 to 2.0 mm apical to the CEJ.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Cavities need 30-40% demineralization before showing on X-ray! (Clinical cavity is always bigger!).\n* Cervical burnout = optical illusion shadow at tooth neck (do not drill healthy teeth!).\n* Normal bone crest = 1.5 - 2.0mm below CEJ.",
    "summary": "Radiographic Caries & Periodontal Assessment:\n- Dental Caries Radiographic Appearance:\n  * Enamel Caries: Proximal caries appears as an INVERTED TRIANGLE with its BASE at the enamel outer surface and APEX pointing toward the DEJ.\n  * Dentinal Caries: Once caries crosses the DEJ, it spreads laterally along the junction and forms a second triangle with its BASE at the DEJ and APEX toward the pulp.\n  * Caries requires at least 30% to 40% demineralization before becoming visible radiographically (clinical lesion is always deeper than radiographic shadow!).\n  * Cervical Burnout: An optical illusion / artifact appearing as a collar-shaped radiolucency at the cervical neck of teeth between enamel cap and alveolar bone; Due to anatomical decrease in tooth thickness.\n- Periodontal Bone Loss Assessment:\n  * Normal Bone Level: Alveolar crest lies 1.5 to 2.0 mm apical to the CEJ.\n  * Horizontal Bone Loss: Bone resorption parallel to an imaginary line connecting CEJs of adjacent teeth.\n  * Vertical (Angular) Bone Loss: Infrabony pocket; Bone level is angled/oblique relative to the CEJ line.\n  * Furcation Involvement: Class I (incipient), Class II (cul-de-sac), Class III ('through-and-through' bone loss), Class IV (through-and-through with clinically visible recession).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nCervical burnout occurs because the tooth neck has a cylindrical, narrow cross-section and lacks both the thick enamel crown cap and the surrounding alveolar bone sheath. More X-ray photons pass through this thin cervical zone unhindered, darkening the film. Clinicians distinguish burnout from true root caries because burnout displays an intact root outline with no cavitation, fading gradually toward the pulp chamber without breaking the root surface.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nDemineralization required for radiographic visibility: At least 30-40% mineral loss! Cervical burnout is an optical illusion at the neck of the tooth. Normal alveolar bone crest is 1.5 to 2.0 mm apical to the CEJ.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Cavities need 30-40% demineralization before showing on X-ray! (Clinical cavity is always bigger!).\n* Cervical burnout = optical illusion shadow at tooth neck (do not drill healthy teeth!).\n* Normal bone crest = 1.5 - 2.0mm below CEJ.",
