    "title": "Surgical Flap Design: Principles, Full-Thickness & Types",
    "subject": "Oral Surgery",
    "day": "Day 2 (11:00 AM \u2022 10%) - Oral Surgery",
    "author": "Gerard Romeo A. Millo, DMD",
    "keywords": [
      "surgical flaps",
      "mucoperiosteal flap",
      "full thickness",
      "envelope flap",
      "triangular flap",
      "trapezoidal flap",
      "flap base"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Surgical Flap Design & Principles in Oral Surgery:\n- Principles of Proper Flap Design:\n  1. Blood Supply: The BASE of the flap must ALWAYS be WIDER than the free margin to maintain adequate axial arterial and capillary perfusion; Avoid compromising major vessels.\n  2. Sound Bony Support: Flap margins and releasing incisions must rest on SOUND, HEALTHY BONE at least 4 to 5 mm away from the surgical bony defect; Never place an incision over a bone defect or directly over a bony prominence (canine eminence).\n  3. Full-Thickness Mucoperiosteal Flap: Incision includes Mucosa + Submucosa + Periosteum reflected cleanly from bone in a single layer.\n  4. Vertical Releasing Incisions: Placed at the line angles of teeth (never at the mid-facial gingival margin or mid-papilla) to prevent gingival recession and poor esthetics.\n- Flap Classifications:\n  * Envelope Flap: Horizontal sulcular incision along teeth without releasing incisions; Easiest to close, minimal swelling, but limited apical access.\n  * Two-Sided (Triangular) Flap: Envelope + ONE vertical releasing incision (mesial release); Good access.\n  * Three-Sided (Trapezoidal) Flap: Envelope + TWO vertical releasing incisions; Maximum access; Higher swelling.\n  * Semilunar Flap: Curved incision in movable mucosa; Used for apicoectomy.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nFlap failure occurs when the vascular supply is choked at the base or when margins are closed over empty bone defects. A wide base preserves subperiosteal arteriolar plexuses. Resting suture lines on sound bone prevents the flap from collapsing into the defect during postoperative mastication, which would otherwise tear sutures, lead to wound dehiscence, and create chronic bony defects.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nGolden rule of flap design: The base of the flap must be WIDER than the apex to ensure adequate blood supply! Vertical releasing incisions are always placed at the line angles of teeth (never mid-radicular or splitting papilla). Suture margins must rest on sound bone.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Base of flap MUST BE WIDER than the top (so blood supply isn't cut off!).\n* Flap stitches must rest on SOUND BONE (4-5mm away from hole!).\n* Never cut mid-tooth or through the middle of the papilla (cut at line angles).",
    "summary": "Surgical Flap Design & Principles in Oral Surgery:\n- Principles of Proper Flap Design:\n  1. Blood Supply: The BASE of the flap must ALWAYS be WIDER than the free margin to maintain adequate axial arterial and capillary perfusion; Avoid compromising major vessels.\n  2. Sound Bony Support: Flap margins and releasing incisions must rest on SOUND, HEALTHY BONE at least 4 to 5 mm away from the surgical bony defect; Never place an incision over a bone defect or directly over a bony prominence (canine eminence).\n  3. Full-Thickness Mucoperiosteal Flap: Incision includes Mucosa + Submucosa + Periosteum reflected cleanly from bone in a single layer.\n  4. Vertical Releasing Incisions: Placed at the line angles of teeth (never at the mid-facial gingival margin or mid-papilla) to prevent gingival recession and poor esthetics.\n- Flap Classifications:\n  * Envelope Flap: Horizontal sulcular incision along teeth without releasing incisions; Easiest to close, minimal swelling, but limited apical access.\n  * Two-Sided (Triangular) Flap: Envelope + ONE vertical releasing incision (mesial release); Good access.\n  * Three-Sided (Trapezoidal) Flap: Envelope + TWO vertical releasing incisions; Maximum access; Higher swelling.\n  * Semilunar Flap: Curved incision in movable mucosa; Used for apicoectomy.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nFlap failure occurs when the vascular supply is choked at the base or when margins are closed over empty bone defects. A wide base preserves subperiosteal arteriolar plexuses. Resting suture lines on sound bone prevents the flap from collapsing into the defect during postoperative mastication, which would otherwise tear sutures, lead to wound dehiscence, and create chronic bony defects.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nGolden rule of flap design: The base of the flap must be WIDER than the apex to ensure adequate blood supply! Vertical releasing incisions are always placed at the line angles of teeth (never mid-radicular or splitting papilla). Suture margins must rest on sound bone.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Base of flap MUST BE WIDER than the top (so blood supply isn't cut off!).\n* Flap stitches must rest on SOUND BONE (4-5mm away from hole!).\n* Never cut mid-tooth or through the middle of the papilla (cut at line angles).",
