    "title": "Post-Extraction Complications: Alveolar Osteitis (Dry Socket)",
    "subject": "Oral Surgery",
    "day": "Day 2 (11:00 AM \u2022 10%) - Oral Surgery",
    "author": "Gerard Romeo A. Millo, DMD",
    "keywords": [
      "alveolar osteitis",
      "dry socket",
      "fibrinolysis",
      "eugenol",
      "alvogyl",
      "smoking",
      "oral contraceptives"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Alveolar Osteitis (Dry Socket / Fibrinolytic Alveolitis):\n- Etiology: Failure or premature dissolution (FIBRINOLYSIS) of the initial blood clot in the extraction socket before it can be replaced by granulation tissue; High tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) activity converts plasminogen to plasmin, lysing the fibrin clot.\n- High-Risk Factors:\n  * Extraction of Mandibular Third Molars (10-30% incidence).\n  * CIGARETTE SMOKING (both chemical nicotine vasoconstriction and mechanical suction dislodging clot).\n  * Use of ORAL CONTRACEPTIVES (estrogen increases clotting factor synthesis and elevates fibrinolytic activity).\n  * Traumatic extraction, excessive bone removal, excessive irrigation.\n- Clinical Features: Occurs 3 to 5 DAYS POST-EXTRACTION; Excruciating, throbbing, radiating pain (to ear or temple) unresponsive to mild analgesics; FOUL ODOR (halitosis) and bad taste; Socket is devoid of blood clot, exposing bare, sensitive yellow-white bone.\n- Management:\n  1. Gently irrigate socket with warm sterile saline (remove food debris; DO NOT CURETTE BARE BONE!).\n  2. Place palliative sedative dressing containing Eugenol (e.g., Alvogyl or zinc oxide eugenol on iodoform gauze) loosely into socket.\n  3. Change dressing every 24-48 hours until pain subsides.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nDry socket is fundamentally a problem of premature fibrinolysis, not acute bacterial osteomyelitis. Curetting a dry socket is a severe clinical error: curettage scrapes exposed, highly sensitized nociceptive bone nerve endings and destroys the microscopic healing cambium without generating a new clot. The goal of management is strictly palliative: gentle saline flushing to remove necrotic debris and applying eugenol-impregnated gauze to obtund denuded nerve fibers.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nDry socket occurs: 3 to 5 days post-extraction! Cause: Premature lysis of the blood clot (fibrinolysis). Management: Warm saline irrigation + Eugenol sedative dressing (DO NOT CURETTE!). Risk factors: Mandibular 3rd molars, smoking, and oral contraceptives.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Dry Socket = happens 3-5 DAYS after extraction! (Foul odor + throbbing pain radiating to ear).\n* Cause = Clot dissolved too early (fibrinolysis).\n* NEVER CURETTE A DRY SOCKET! Flush with warm saline & pack Alvogyl/Eugenol gauze!",
    "summary": "Alveolar Osteitis (Dry Socket / Fibrinolytic Alveolitis):\n- Etiology: Failure or premature dissolution (FIBRINOLYSIS) of the initial blood clot in the extraction socket before it can be replaced by granulation tissue; High tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) activity converts plasminogen to plasmin, lysing the fibrin clot.\n- High-Risk Factors:\n  * Extraction of Mandibular Third Molars (10-30% incidence).\n  * CIGARETTE SMOKING (both chemical nicotine vasoconstriction and mechanical suction dislodging clot).\n  * Use of ORAL CONTRACEPTIVES (estrogen increases clotting factor synthesis and elevates fibrinolytic activity).\n  * Traumatic extraction, excessive bone removal, excessive irrigation.\n- Clinical Features: Occurs 3 to 5 DAYS POST-EXTRACTION; Excruciating, throbbing, radiating pain (to ear or temple) unresponsive to mild analgesics; FOUL ODOR (halitosis) and bad taste; Socket is devoid of blood clot, exposing bare, sensitive yellow-white bone.\n- Management:\n  1. Gently irrigate socket with warm sterile saline (remove food debris; DO NOT CURETTE BARE BONE!).\n  2. Place palliative sedative dressing containing Eugenol (e.g., Alvogyl or zinc oxide eugenol on iodoform gauze) loosely into socket.\n  3. Change dressing every 24-48 hours until pain subsides.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nDry socket is fundamentally a problem of premature fibrinolysis, not acute bacterial osteomyelitis. Curetting a dry socket is a severe clinical error: curettage scrapes exposed, highly sensitized nociceptive bone nerve endings and destroys the microscopic healing cambium without generating a new clot. The goal of management is strictly palliative: gentle saline flushing to remove necrotic debris and applying eugenol-impregnated gauze to obtund denuded nerve fibers.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nDry socket occurs: 3 to 5 days post-extraction! Cause: Premature lysis of the blood clot (fibrinolysis). Management: Warm saline irrigation + Eugenol sedative dressing (DO NOT CURETTE!). Risk factors: Mandibular 3rd molars, smoking, and oral contraceptives.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Dry Socket = happens 3-5 DAYS after extraction! (Foul odor + throbbing pain radiating to ear).\n* Cause = Clot dissolved too early (fibrinolysis).\n* NEVER CURETTE A DRY SOCKET! Flush with warm saline & pack Alvogyl/Eugenol gauze!",
