    "pageNumber": 211,
    "title": "Maxillary Sinus Complications: Oroantral Communication (OAC) & Fistula",
    "subject": "Oral Surgery",
    "day": "Day 2 (11:00 AM \u2022 10%) - Oral Surgery",
    "author": "Gerard Romeo A. Millo, DMD",
    "keywords": [
      "oroantral communication",
      "oac",
      "oroantral fistula",
      "valsalva maneuver",
      "sinus precautions",
      "buccal advancement flap"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Oroantral Communication (OAC) & Fistula (OAF):\n- Definition: An unnatural open communication between the maxillary sinus cavity and the oral cavity resulting from extraction of maxillary posterior teeth (most commonly Maxillary First Molar).\n- Diagnostic Tests:\n  * Nose-Blowing Test (Valsalva Maneuver): Patient pinches nostrils and blows gently; Air bubbles or hissing sound at the extraction socket confirms OAC.\n  * Note: NEVER probe the socket with an explorer or probe! (Will push mucosal fragments into sinus or puncture intact Schneiderian membrane).\n- Management by Defect Size:\n  * Small Defect (< 2 mm):\n    - Spontaneous healing; Place gelfoam and figure-of-8 suture; Prescribe SINUS PRECAUTIONS:\n      1. No nose blowing for 10-14 days.\n      2. Sneeze with mouth OPEN.\n      3. No smoking, no drinking through a straw.\n      4. Prescribe decongestants (pseudoephedrine) and Amoxicillin.\n  * Moderate Defect (2 to 6 mm): Figure-of-8 suture, collagen plug/gelfoam, sinus precautions, antibiotics.\n  * Large Defect (> 6 mm) or Chronic Oroantral Fistula (epithelialized tract):\n    - Requires surgical flap closure: BUCCAL ADVANCEMENT FLAP (Rehrmann flap with periosteal releasing incision to relieve tension) or Palatal Pedicle Island Flap.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nIf an acute OAC is not sealed within 48-72 hours, oral stratified squamous epithelium migrates down the socket walls to meet the pseudostratified ciliated respiratory epithelium of the Schneiderian membrane, creating a permanent epithelialized tunnel ('oroantral fistula'). Once epithelialized, spontaneous closure is impossible; the epithelial tract must be completely excised down to bone before advancing a tension-free buccal periosteal-released flap.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nDiagnostic test for OAC: Nose-blowing test (Valsalva maneuver) - NEVER probe socket with an instrument! Small OAC (<2mm) resolves with sinus precautions (no nose blowing, sneeze with mouth open). Large OAC (>6mm) requires a Buccal Advancement Flap.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* DO NOT PROBE THE SOCKET with an explorer! Do Valsalva nose-blow test.\n* Small hole (<2mm): Sinus precautions (sneeze with mouth open, no blowing nose, no straws!).\n* Big hole (>6mm): Buccal advancement flap (must release periosteum for zero tension!).",
    "summary": "Oroantral Communication (OAC) & Fistula (OAF):\n- Definition: An unnatural open communication between the maxillary sinus cavity and the oral cavity resulting from extraction of maxillary posterior teeth (most commonly Maxillary First Molar).\n- Diagnostic Tests:\n  * Nose-Blowing Test (Valsalva Maneuver): Patient pinches nostrils and blows gently; Air bubbles or hissing sound at the extraction socket confirms OAC.\n  * Note: NEVER probe the socket with an explorer or probe! (Will push mucosal fragments into sinus or puncture intact Schneiderian membrane).\n- Management by Defect Size:\n  * Small Defect (< 2 mm):\n    - Spontaneous healing; Place gelfoam and figure-of-8 suture; Prescribe SINUS PRECAUTIONS:\n      1. No nose blowing for 10-14 days.\n      2. Sneeze with mouth OPEN.\n      3. No smoking, no drinking through a straw.\n      4. Prescribe decongestants (pseudoephedrine) and Amoxicillin.\n  * Moderate Defect (2 to 6 mm): Figure-of-8 suture, collagen plug/gelfoam, sinus precautions, antibiotics.\n  * Large Defect (> 6 mm) or Chronic Oroantral Fistula (epithelialized tract):\n    - Requires surgical flap closure: BUCCAL ADVANCEMENT FLAP (Rehrmann flap with periosteal releasing incision to relieve tension) or Palatal Pedicle Island Flap.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nIf an acute OAC is not sealed within 48-72 hours, oral stratified squamous epithelium migrates down the socket walls to meet the pseudostratified ciliated respiratory epithelium of the Schneiderian membrane, creating a permanent epithelialized tunnel ('oroantral fistula'). Once epithelialized, spontaneous closure is impossible; the epithelial tract must be completely excised down to bone before advancing a tension-free buccal periosteal-released flap.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nDiagnostic test for OAC: Nose-blowing test (Valsalva maneuver) - NEVER probe socket with an instrument! Small OAC (<2mm) resolves with sinus precautions (no nose blowing, sneeze with mouth open). Large OAC (>6mm) requires a Buccal Advancement Flap.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* DO NOT PROBE THE SOCKET with an explorer! Do Valsalva nose-blow test.\n* Small hole (<2mm): Sinus precautions (sneeze with mouth open, no blowing nose, no straws!).\n* Big hole (>6mm): Buccal advancement flap (must release periosteum for zero tension!).",
