    "originalPrint": "AAE Consensus Classification of Apical (Periapical) Pathosis:\n1. Normal Apical Tissues: Negative to percussion and palpation; Intact lamina dura and uniform PDL space.\n2. Symptomatic Apical Periodontitis (SAP):\n   * Symptoms: EXTREME PAIN to biting, chewing, or tapping (PERCUSSION);\n   * Radiograph: May show normal PDL or slightly widened apical PDL space; May or may not have apical radiolucency.\n   * Etiology: Extension of pulpal inflammation/toxins into PDL, occlusal trauma, or over-instrumentation during RCT.\n3. Asymptomatic Apical Periodontitis (AAP):\n   * Symptoms: Asymptomatic; Negative or mild response to biting/percussion.\n   * Radiograph: Definite, well-circumscribed APICAL RADIOLUCENCY (periapical granuloma or radicular cyst); Tooth tests non-vital.\n4. Acute Apical Abscess (AAA):\n   * Rapid onset, spontaneous severe throbbing pain, extreme tenderness to percussion; Swelling of adjacent soft tissues; Systemic manifestations (fever, malaise, lymphadenopathy).\n5. Chronic Apical Abscess (CAA):\n   * Characterized by GRADUAL onset, little to no discomfort, and continuous or intermittent discharge of pus through an associated SINUS TRACT (parulis / gum boil); Gutta-percha tracing confirms offending tooth.\n6. Condensing Osteitis: Localized bony reaction to low-grade inflammatory stimulus; Concentric radiopacity at root apex.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nSymptomatic apical periodontitis triggers intense mechanical pain because sensory mechanoreceptors and Ruffini endings in the apical PDL become hyper-sensitized by inflammatory cytokines, firing aggressively under light axial loads. Chronic apical abscess remains painless because the sinus tract functions as a natural decompression vent, releasing purulent exudate and preventing the buildup of intraluminal hydrostatic pressure.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nPathognomonic sign of Symptomatic Apical Periodontitis: Marked PAIN TO PERCUSSION! Chronic Apical Abscess is characterized by a SINUS TRACT (parulis/fistula) with continuous pus discharge. Trace a sinus tract with a #25 gutta-percha cone on a radiograph.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Symptomatic Apical Periodontitis = PAIN TO TAPPING / PERCUSSION!\n* Chronic Apical Abscess = GUM BOIL / SINUS TRACT (pus drains, no pain!).\n* Trace gum boil with Gutta-Percha cone to find which tooth is dead!",
    "summary": "AAE Consensus Classification of Apical (Periapical) Pathosis:\n1. Normal Apical Tissues: Negative to percussion and palpation; Intact lamina dura and uniform PDL space.\n2. Symptomatic Apical Periodontitis (SAP):\n   * Symptoms: EXTREME PAIN to biting, chewing, or tapping (PERCUSSION);\n   * Radiograph: May show normal PDL or slightly widened apical PDL space; May or may not have apical radiolucency.\n   * Etiology: Extension of pulpal inflammation/toxins into PDL, occlusal trauma, or over-instrumentation during RCT.\n3. Asymptomatic Apical Periodontitis (AAP):\n   * Symptoms: Asymptomatic; Negative or mild response to biting/percussion.\n   * Radiograph: Definite, well-circumscribed APICAL RADIOLUCENCY (periapical granuloma or radicular cyst); Tooth tests non-vital.\n4. Acute Apical Abscess (AAA):\n   * Rapid onset, spontaneous severe throbbing pain, extreme tenderness to percussion; Swelling of adjacent soft tissues; Systemic manifestations (fever, malaise, lymphadenopathy).\n5. Chronic Apical Abscess (CAA):\n   * Characterized by GRADUAL onset, little to no discomfort, and continuous or intermittent discharge of pus through an associated SINUS TRACT (parulis / gum boil); Gutta-percha tracing confirms offending tooth.\n6. Condensing Osteitis: Localized bony reaction to low-grade inflammatory stimulus; Concentric radiopacity at root apex.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nSymptomatic apical periodontitis triggers intense mechanical pain because sensory mechanoreceptors and Ruffini endings in the apical PDL become hyper-sensitized by inflammatory cytokines, firing aggressively under light axial loads. Chronic apical abscess remains painless because the sinus tract functions as a natural decompression vent, releasing purulent exudate and preventing the buildup of intraluminal hydrostatic pressure.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nPathognomonic sign of Symptomatic Apical Periodontitis: Marked PAIN TO PERCUSSION! Chronic Apical Abscess is characterized by a SINUS TRACT (parulis/fistula) with continuous pus discharge. Trace a sinus tract with a #25 gutta-percha cone on a radiograph.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Symptomatic Apical Periodontitis = PAIN TO TAPPING / PERCUSSION!\n* Chronic Apical Abscess = GUM BOIL / SINUS TRACT (pus drains, no pain!).\n* Trace gum boil with Gutta-Percha cone to find which tooth is dead!",
