    "originalPrint": "Gingival Epithelium & The Biologic Width:\n- Three Areas of Gingival Epithelium:\n  1. Oral (Outer) Epithelium: Faces oral cavity; Stratified squamous KERATINIZED or parakeratinized; Contains rete pegs.\n  2. Sulcular Epithelium: Lines gingival sulcus; Thin, NON-KERATINIZED; Semi-permeable to bacterial toxins and GCF.\n  3. Junctional Epithelium (JE):\n     * Collar of non-keratinized epithelium attached directly to the tooth surface (enamel or cementum) via INTERNAL BASAL LAMINA and HEMIDESMOSOMES; Rapid cell turnover rate (4 to 6 days); High permeability; Forms the protective epithelial barrier.\n- The Biologic Width (Gargiulo, 1961 - Total ~2.04 mm):\n  * Definition: The vertical dimensional space occupied by the soft tissue attachment above the alveolar crest.\n  * Components:\n    1. Junctional Epithelium (Epithelial Attachment): ~0.97 mm.\n    2. Connective Tissue Attachment: ~1.07 mm.\n    * (Total Biologic Width = 0.97 + 1.07 = ~2.04 mm).\n    * Plus Gingival Sulcus (~0.69 mm) = total ~2.73 mm.\n  * Clinical Rule: Restorative crown margins must be placed at least 3.0 mm away from the alveolar bone crest to avoid VIOLATING BIOLOGIC WIDTH (violation causes chronic inflammation, bone loss, and gingival recession).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nBiologic width represents a constant physiologic seal established by the human body to protect underlying alveolar bone from oral bacteria. When a subgingival crown margin impinges closer than 2.0mm to the alveolar crest, bacteria on the restoration margin induce chronic osteoclastic bone resorption: the alveolar crest resorbs apically until an intact 2.04mm zone of connective tissue and junctional epithelium can re-establish itself.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nComponents of the Biologic Width (Gargiulo): Junctional Epithelium (0.97mm) + Connective Tissue Attachment (1.07mm) = ~2.04 mm! Violating biologic width with deep crown margins results in chronic inflammation and crestal bone resorption. Minimum distance from margin to bone crest: 3.0 mm.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Biologic Width = 2.04 mm! (Junctional epithelium 0.97mm + Connective tissue 1.07mm).\n* Crown margin must be AT LEAST 3mm away from bone crest!\n* Violating biologic width causes chronic bleeding and bone loss!",
    "summary": "Gingival Epithelium & The Biologic Width:\n- Three Areas of Gingival Epithelium:\n  1. Oral (Outer) Epithelium: Faces oral cavity; Stratified squamous KERATINIZED or parakeratinized; Contains rete pegs.\n  2. Sulcular Epithelium: Lines gingival sulcus; Thin, NON-KERATINIZED; Semi-permeable to bacterial toxins and GCF.\n  3. Junctional Epithelium (JE):\n     * Collar of non-keratinized epithelium attached directly to the tooth surface (enamel or cementum) via INTERNAL BASAL LAMINA and HEMIDESMOSOMES; Rapid cell turnover rate (4 to 6 days); High permeability; Forms the protective epithelial barrier.\n- The Biologic Width (Gargiulo, 1961 - Total ~2.04 mm):\n  * Definition: The vertical dimensional space occupied by the soft tissue attachment above the alveolar crest.\n  * Components:\n    1. Junctional Epithelium (Epithelial Attachment): ~0.97 mm.\n    2. Connective Tissue Attachment: ~1.07 mm.\n    * (Total Biologic Width = 0.97 + 1.07 = ~2.04 mm).\n    * Plus Gingival Sulcus (~0.69 mm) = total ~2.73 mm.\n  * Clinical Rule: Restorative crown margins must be placed at least 3.0 mm away from the alveolar bone crest to avoid VIOLATING BIOLOGIC WIDTH (violation causes chronic inflammation, bone loss, and gingival recession).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nBiologic width represents a constant physiologic seal established by the human body to protect underlying alveolar bone from oral bacteria. When a subgingival crown margin impinges closer than 2.0mm to the alveolar crest, bacteria on the restoration margin induce chronic osteoclastic bone resorption: the alveolar crest resorbs apically until an intact 2.04mm zone of connective tissue and junctional epithelium can re-establish itself.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nComponents of the Biologic Width (Gargiulo): Junctional Epithelium (0.97mm) + Connective Tissue Attachment (1.07mm) = ~2.04 mm! Violating biologic width with deep crown margins results in chronic inflammation and crestal bone resorption. Minimum distance from margin to bone crest: 3.0 mm.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Biologic Width = 2.04 mm! (Junctional epithelium 0.97mm + Connective tissue 1.07mm).\n* Crown margin must be AT LEAST 3mm away from bone crest!\n* Violating biologic width causes chronic bleeding and bone loss!",
