    "originalPrint": "Gingival Connective Tissue & Gingival Collagen Fiber Groups:\n- Gingival Lamina Propria: Composed predominantly of Type I Collagen fibers (60%), ground substance (proteoglycans), fibroblasts, and vessels.\n- Five Principal Gingival Fiber Groups (Gingival Apparatus):\n  1. Dentogingival Fibers: Most numerous group; Extend from cervical cementum fan-like into the free and attached gingival lamina propria.\n  2. Alveologingival Fibers: Extend from the crest of the alveolar bone coronally into the marginal and attached gingiva.\n  3. Circular Fibers: Encircle the neck of the tooth in a ring-like manner within the marginal and interdental gingiva ('pursestring effect').\n  4. Dentoperiosteal Fibers: Extend from cervical cementum over the buccal and lingual alveolar crest bone to anchor into the periosteum.\n  5. Transseptal Fibers:\n     * Extend interproximally over the crest of the interdental septum to connect the cementum of ADJACENT TEETH; Technically part of gingiva, but functionally considered an accessory PDL group because they lack bony attachment; Persist even in advanced periodontitis ('reconstructed' as bone resorbs); Implicated in orthodontic rotational relapse.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nUnlike principal PDL fibers that insert into bone, gingival fibers brace the marginal gingiva firmly against tooth enamel like a gasket. Circular fibers form a continuous purse-string band maintaining collar tension against lateral food displacement. Transseptal fibers form an uninterrupted fibrous chain linking all teeth across the arch, maintaining arch integrity and mesiodistal proximal contact tension throughout life.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nWhich fiber group connects adjacent teeth across the interdental alveolar septum? TRANSSEPTAL FIBERS (have no bony attachment)! Most numerous gingival fiber group: Dentogingival fibers. Circular fibers provide a purse-string ring around tooth necks.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Transseptal fibers = connect tooth to adjacent tooth over the bone crest (NO bone connection!).\n* Dentogingival = most numerous gingival fibers.\n* Circular fibers = purse-string around the tooth neck.",
    "summary": "Gingival Connective Tissue & Gingival Collagen Fiber Groups:\n- Gingival Lamina Propria: Composed predominantly of Type I Collagen fibers (60%), ground substance (proteoglycans), fibroblasts, and vessels.\n- Five Principal Gingival Fiber Groups (Gingival Apparatus):\n  1. Dentogingival Fibers: Most numerous group; Extend from cervical cementum fan-like into the free and attached gingival lamina propria.\n  2. Alveologingival Fibers: Extend from the crest of the alveolar bone coronally into the marginal and attached gingiva.\n  3. Circular Fibers: Encircle the neck of the tooth in a ring-like manner within the marginal and interdental gingiva ('pursestring effect').\n  4. Dentoperiosteal Fibers: Extend from cervical cementum over the buccal and lingual alveolar crest bone to anchor into the periosteum.\n  5. Transseptal Fibers:\n     * Extend interproximally over the crest of the interdental septum to connect the cementum of ADJACENT TEETH; Technically part of gingiva, but functionally considered an accessory PDL group because they lack bony attachment; Persist even in advanced periodontitis ('reconstructed' as bone resorbs); Implicated in orthodontic rotational relapse.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nUnlike principal PDL fibers that insert into bone, gingival fibers brace the marginal gingiva firmly against tooth enamel like a gasket. Circular fibers form a continuous purse-string band maintaining collar tension against lateral food displacement. Transseptal fibers form an uninterrupted fibrous chain linking all teeth across the arch, maintaining arch integrity and mesiodistal proximal contact tension throughout life.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nWhich fiber group connects adjacent teeth across the interdental alveolar septum? TRANSSEPTAL FIBERS (have no bony attachment)! Most numerous gingival fiber group: Dentogingival fibers. Circular fibers provide a purse-string ring around tooth necks.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Transseptal fibers = connect tooth to adjacent tooth over the bone crest (NO bone connection!).\n* Dentogingival = most numerous gingival fibers.\n* Circular fibers = purse-string around the tooth neck.",
