    "title": "Cementum: Composition, Acellular vs Cellular Types & CEJ Variations",
    "subject": "Periodontics",
    "day": "Day 3 (2:00 PM \u2022 10%) - Periodontics",
    "author": "Dr. Ma. Ana Lowella M. Minda\u00f1a",
    "keywords": [
      "cementum",
      "acellular cementum",
      "cellular cementum",
      "cej relationships",
      "choquet",
      "cementocytes",
      "hypercementosis"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Cementum Biology & CEJ Relationships:\n- Characteristics of Cementum:\n  * Avascular, non-innervated, non-remodeling mineralized tissue covering the anatomical roots of teeth; 45% to 50% Hydroxyapatite, 50% to 55% Organic matrix (Type I collagen) and water; Highly resistant to resorption compared to bone (allows orthodontic movement without root loss!).\n- Types of Cementum:\n  1. Acellular (Primary) Extrinsic Fiber Cementum (AEFC):\n     * Formed BEFORE tooth reaches occlusal plane; Covers cervical and middle third of root; Contains NO CELLS (no cementocytes); Primary function is ATTACHMENT (Sharpey's fibers fully embedded).\n  2. Cellular (Secondary) Intrinsic Fiber Cementum (CIFC):\n     * Formed AFTER tooth reaches occlusion; Covers APICAL third and furcations; Contains CEMENTOCYTES in lacunae; Primary function is ADAPTATION and reparative compensation for occlusal wear/attrition.\n- Cementoenamel Junction (CEJ) Relationships (Choquet's Rule):\n  * 60% to 65%: Cementum OVERLAPS enamel (most common).\n  * 30%: Cementum meets enamel EDGE-TO-EDGE.\n  * 5% to 10%: GAP between cementum and enamel (dentin is exposed; results in CERVICAL HYPERSENSITIVITY if gingiva recedes!).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nOrthodontic tooth movement is biologically possible solely because cementum is more resistant to resorption than alveolar bone under equivalent mechanical loads. Cementum lacks direct vascular supply and is protected by a continuous superficial unmineralized precementum layer coated with cementoblasts. When light orthodontic force is applied, osteoclasts resorb the vascular alveolar bone plate while the avascular cementum remains intact.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nCEJ variations: Cementum OVERLAPS enamel in 60-65% (most common); Edge-to-edge in 30%; GAP with exposed dentin in 5-10% (causes dentin hypersensitivity)! Acellular cementum covers the coronal half (attachment); Cellular cementum covers the apical third (reparative).",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* CEJ Relationships: Overlap = 60%, Edge-to-edge = 30%, GAP with exposed dentin = 10%!\n* Acellular cementum = coronal 1/2 (has NO cells, attachment).\n* Cellular cementum = apical 1/3 (has cementocyte cells, reparative).",
    "summary": "Cementum Biology & CEJ Relationships:\n- Characteristics of Cementum:\n  * Avascular, non-innervated, non-remodeling mineralized tissue covering the anatomical roots of teeth; 45% to 50% Hydroxyapatite, 50% to 55% Organic matrix (Type I collagen) and water; Highly resistant to resorption compared to bone (allows orthodontic movement without root loss!).\n- Types of Cementum:\n  1. Acellular (Primary) Extrinsic Fiber Cementum (AEFC):\n     * Formed BEFORE tooth reaches occlusal plane; Covers cervical and middle third of root; Contains NO CELLS (no cementocytes); Primary function is ATTACHMENT (Sharpey's fibers fully embedded).\n  2. Cellular (Secondary) Intrinsic Fiber Cementum (CIFC):\n     * Formed AFTER tooth reaches occlusion; Covers APICAL third and furcations; Contains CEMENTOCYTES in lacunae; Primary function is ADAPTATION and reparative compensation for occlusal wear/attrition.\n- Cementoenamel Junction (CEJ) Relationships (Choquet's Rule):\n  * 60% to 65%: Cementum OVERLAPS enamel (most common).\n  * 30%: Cementum meets enamel EDGE-TO-EDGE.\n  * 5% to 10%: GAP between cementum and enamel (dentin is exposed; results in CERVICAL HYPERSENSITIVITY if gingiva recedes!).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nOrthodontic tooth movement is biologically possible solely because cementum is more resistant to resorption than alveolar bone under equivalent mechanical loads. Cementum lacks direct vascular supply and is protected by a continuous superficial unmineralized precementum layer coated with cementoblasts. When light orthodontic force is applied, osteoclasts resorb the vascular alveolar bone plate while the avascular cementum remains intact.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nCEJ variations: Cementum OVERLAPS enamel in 60-65% (most common); Edge-to-edge in 30%; GAP with exposed dentin in 5-10% (causes dentin hypersensitivity)! Acellular cementum covers the coronal half (attachment); Cellular cementum covers the apical third (reparative).\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* CEJ Relationships: Overlap = 60%, Edge-to-edge = 30%, GAP with exposed dentin = 10%!\n* Acellular cementum = coronal 1/2 (has NO cells, attachment).\n* Cellular cementum = apical 1/3 (has cementocyte cells, reparative).",
