    "pageNumber": 231,
    "title": "Alveolar Bone Architecture: Dehiscence vs Fenestration Defects",
    "subject": "Periodontics",
    "day": "Day 3 (2:00 PM \u2022 10%) - Periodontics",
    "author": "Dr. Ma. Ana Lowella M. Minda\u00f1a",
    "keywords": [
      "alveolar bone",
      "dehiscence",
      "fenestration",
      "cribriform plate",
      "lamina dura",
      "bundle bone",
      "cortical bone"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Alveolar Process Architecture & Anatomic Bony Defects:\n- Parts of the Alveolar Process:\n  1. Alveolar Bone Proper (Cribriform Plate / Bundle Bone):\n     * Thin layer of compact bone lining the socket; Radiographically called the LAMINA DURA (continuous white radiopaque line around root); Perforated by Volkmann's canals for vessels/nerves; Contains embedded Sharpey's fibers ('bundle bone').\n  2. Supporting Alveolar Bone:\n     * Cortical plates (buccal and lingual dense compact bone) and Central Spongy (cancellous / trabecular) bone.\n- Anatomic Bony Defects of Alveolar Bone:\n  * Fenestration (Bony Window):\n    - An isolated circumscribed defect / 'window' in the cortical bone exposing the underlying root surface, where the MARGINAL BONE REMAINS INTACT (covered only by periosteum and gingiva).\n  * Dehiscence (Bony Cleft):\n    - A defect in which the denuded root area extends continuously through the MARGINAL ALVEOLAR BONE CREST (V-shaped cleft of missing crestal bone).\n  * Predisposing Factors: Prominent root contours, labially displaced teeth (maxillary canines, lower incisors), thin buccal bone plate.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nFenestration and dehiscence occur primarily on facial aspects of prominent teeth where root diameter exceeds the buccolingual width of the alveolar process. During full-thickness mucoperiosteal flap elevation, stripping periosteum off an unrecognized dehiscence deprives the denuded root of blood supply, converting a subclinical skeletal defect into permanent, irreversible postoperative gingival recession.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nDistinguish Fenestration vs Dehiscence: Fenestration is an isolated bony window defect where marginal bone is INTACT! Dehiscence is a defect where marginal crestal bone is MISSING (extends through the alveolar margin). Lamina dura is the radiographic term for alveolar bone proper.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* FENESTRATION = isolated window in bone (marginal bone is still INTACT!).\n* DEHISCENCE = bone missing all the way through the marginal crest (V-shaped cleft)!\n* Lamina Dura on X-ray = Alveolar Bone Proper.",
    "summary": "Alveolar Process Architecture & Anatomic Bony Defects:\n- Parts of the Alveolar Process:\n  1. Alveolar Bone Proper (Cribriform Plate / Bundle Bone):\n     * Thin layer of compact bone lining the socket; Radiographically called the LAMINA DURA (continuous white radiopaque line around root); Perforated by Volkmann's canals for vessels/nerves; Contains embedded Sharpey's fibers ('bundle bone').\n  2. Supporting Alveolar Bone:\n     * Cortical plates (buccal and lingual dense compact bone) and Central Spongy (cancellous / trabecular) bone.\n- Anatomic Bony Defects of Alveolar Bone:\n  * Fenestration (Bony Window):\n    - An isolated circumscribed defect / 'window' in the cortical bone exposing the underlying root surface, where the MARGINAL BONE REMAINS INTACT (covered only by periosteum and gingiva).\n  * Dehiscence (Bony Cleft):\n    - A defect in which the denuded root area extends continuously through the MARGINAL ALVEOLAR BONE CREST (V-shaped cleft of missing crestal bone).\n  * Predisposing Factors: Prominent root contours, labially displaced teeth (maxillary canines, lower incisors), thin buccal bone plate.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nFenestration and dehiscence occur primarily on facial aspects of prominent teeth where root diameter exceeds the buccolingual width of the alveolar process. During full-thickness mucoperiosteal flap elevation, stripping periosteum off an unrecognized dehiscence deprives the denuded root of blood supply, converting a subclinical skeletal defect into permanent, irreversible postoperative gingival recession.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nDistinguish Fenestration vs Dehiscence: Fenestration is an isolated bony window defect where marginal bone is INTACT! Dehiscence is a defect where marginal crestal bone is MISSING (extends through the alveolar margin). Lamina dura is the radiographic term for alveolar bone proper.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* FENESTRATION = isolated window in bone (marginal bone is still INTACT!).\n* DEHISCENCE = bone missing all the way through the marginal crest (V-shaped cleft)!\n* Lamina Dura on X-ray = Alveolar Bone Proper.",
