    "originalPrint": "Area-Specific Gracey Curettes (Dr. Clayton Gracey):\n- Unique Design Features:\n  * Face of blade is OFFSET at 60\u00b0 to 70\u00b0 to the lower terminal shank (self-angulated; automatically establishes correct 70\u00b0 cutting angle when lower shank is PARALLEL to the tooth axis!).\n  * Only ONE active cutting edge per blade (the LOWER, longer, convex outer curvature).\n  * Semicircular cross-section with rounded toe and rounded back.\n- Anatomic Numbering Sequence:\n  * Gracey 1/2 and 3/4: Anterior teeth (incisors and canines).\n  * Gracey 5/6: Anterior teeth and Premolars.\n  * Gracey 7/8 and 9/10: Posterior teeth - BUCCAL (facial) and LINGUAL surfaces.\n  * Gracey 11/12: Posterior teeth - MESIAL surfaces only.\n  * Gracey 13/14: Posterior teeth - DISTAL surfaces only.\n  * Gracey 15/16: Modified 11/12 with acute shank bend for posterior mesial.\n  * Gracey 17/18: Modified 13/14 with accentuated bend for posterior distal.\n- Special Variations:\n  * After-Five Curettes: Terminal shank is 3 mm longer (for pockets > 5 mm).\n  * Mini-Five Curettes: Blade is 50% shorter (for narrow deep pockets, furcations).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nUnlike universal curettes whose 90\u00b0 face requires manual tilting, Gracey curettes possess an engineered 70\u00b0 offset bevel. When the operator aligns the lower terminal shank parallel to the tooth's long axis, the active cutting edge automatically presents at the ideal 70\u00b0 shearing angle to root dentin. The non-cutting upper edge slides smoothly against pocket soft tissue without lacerating sulcular walls.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nAbsolute must-know Gracey numbers: Gracey 11/12 is for Posterior MESIAL surfaces! Gracey 13/14 is for Posterior DISTAL surfaces! Gracey 1/2 is for Anterior teeth. Gracey blade is offset at 60-70\u00b0 with only ONE active cutting edge.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Gracey 11/12 = POSTERIOR MESIAL!\n* Gracey 13/14 = POSTERIOR DISTAL!\n* Gracey 1/2, 3/4 = ANTERIOR teeth!\n* Gracey 7/8 = Posterior BUCCAL & LINGUAL.\n* Offset blade (70\u00b0): only ONE cutting edge per end!",
    "summary": "Area-Specific Gracey Curettes (Dr. Clayton Gracey):\n- Unique Design Features:\n  * Face of blade is OFFSET at 60\u00b0 to 70\u00b0 to the lower terminal shank (self-angulated; automatically establishes correct 70\u00b0 cutting angle when lower shank is PARALLEL to the tooth axis!).\n  * Only ONE active cutting edge per blade (the LOWER, longer, convex outer curvature).\n  * Semicircular cross-section with rounded toe and rounded back.\n- Anatomic Numbering Sequence:\n  * Gracey 1/2 and 3/4: Anterior teeth (incisors and canines).\n  * Gracey 5/6: Anterior teeth and Premolars.\n  * Gracey 7/8 and 9/10: Posterior teeth - BUCCAL (facial) and LINGUAL surfaces.\n  * Gracey 11/12: Posterior teeth - MESIAL surfaces only.\n  * Gracey 13/14: Posterior teeth - DISTAL surfaces only.\n  * Gracey 15/16: Modified 11/12 with acute shank bend for posterior mesial.\n  * Gracey 17/18: Modified 13/14 with accentuated bend for posterior distal.\n- Special Variations:\n  * After-Five Curettes: Terminal shank is 3 mm longer (for pockets > 5 mm).\n  * Mini-Five Curettes: Blade is 50% shorter (for narrow deep pockets, furcations).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nUnlike universal curettes whose 90\u00b0 face requires manual tilting, Gracey curettes possess an engineered 70\u00b0 offset bevel. When the operator aligns the lower terminal shank parallel to the tooth's long axis, the active cutting edge automatically presents at the ideal 70\u00b0 shearing angle to root dentin. The non-cutting upper edge slides smoothly against pocket soft tissue without lacerating sulcular walls.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nAbsolute must-know Gracey numbers: Gracey 11/12 is for Posterior MESIAL surfaces! Gracey 13/14 is for Posterior DISTAL surfaces! Gracey 1/2 is for Anterior teeth. Gracey blade is offset at 60-70\u00b0 with only ONE active cutting edge.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Gracey 11/12 = POSTERIOR MESIAL!\n* Gracey 13/14 = POSTERIOR DISTAL!\n* Gracey 1/2, 3/4 = ANTERIOR teeth!\n* Gracey 7/8 = Posterior BUCCAL & LINGUAL.\n* Offset blade (70\u00b0): only ONE cutting edge per end!",
