    "originalPrint": "Code of Ethics for Dentists: Core Bioethical Principles:\n- The Five Fundamental Principles of Dental Ethics:\n  1. Beneficence ('Do Good'): The dentist has a primary duty to promote the patient's welfare and provide competent, timely care; Service to humanity is the supreme obligation.\n  2. Non-Maleficence ('Do No Harm' / Primum Non Nocere): The dentist has a duty to protect the patient from harm, keep knowledge and skills current through continuing education, and know when to refer to specialists.\n  3. Autonomy ('Self-Determination'): The dentist has a duty to respect the patient's rights to self-determination and confidentiality; Involves patient in treatment decisions; Requires voluntary INFORMED CONSENT.\n  4. Justice ('Fairness'): The dentist has a duty to treat people fairly and equitably without discrimination based on race, creed, sex, or socioeconomic status.\n  5. Veracity ('Truthfulness'): The dentist has a duty to communicate truthfully, accurately, and honestly with patients and the public; Prohibition of fraudulent billing, false advertising, or deceptive claims.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nBioethical principles resolve clinical dilemmas. For instance, when a patient demands extraction of an easily restorable canine for cosmetic veneers, patient autonomy (demanding veneers) clashes directly with non-maleficence (extracting a healthy tooth causes irreversible biologic harm). Ethical dental jurisprudence dictates that non-maleficence and beneficence override autonomy when a patient demands medically unsound or destructive treatment.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nPrimum non nocere means: FIRST, DO NO HARM (Principle of Non-Maleficence)! Respect for patient self-determination and informed consent falls under: AUTONOMY. Truthfulness and avoiding fraudulent claims falls under: VERACITY.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Non-Maleficence = Primum Non Nocere (DO NO HARM)!\n* Beneficence = DO GOOD (promote patient's well-being).\n* Autonomy = respect patient's choice (Informed Consent).\n* Veracity = TRUTHFULNESS; Justice = FAIRNESS.",
    "summary": "Code of Ethics for Dentists: Core Bioethical Principles:\n- The Five Fundamental Principles of Dental Ethics:\n  1. Beneficence ('Do Good'): The dentist has a primary duty to promote the patient's welfare and provide competent, timely care; Service to humanity is the supreme obligation.\n  2. Non-Maleficence ('Do No Harm' / Primum Non Nocere): The dentist has a duty to protect the patient from harm, keep knowledge and skills current through continuing education, and know when to refer to specialists.\n  3. Autonomy ('Self-Determination'): The dentist has a duty to respect the patient's rights to self-determination and confidentiality; Involves patient in treatment decisions; Requires voluntary INFORMED CONSENT.\n  4. Justice ('Fairness'): The dentist has a duty to treat people fairly and equitably without discrimination based on race, creed, sex, or socioeconomic status.\n  5. Veracity ('Truthfulness'): The dentist has a duty to communicate truthfully, accurately, and honestly with patients and the public; Prohibition of fraudulent billing, false advertising, or deceptive claims.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nBioethical principles resolve clinical dilemmas. For instance, when a patient demands extraction of an easily restorable canine for cosmetic veneers, patient autonomy (demanding veneers) clashes directly with non-maleficence (extracting a healthy tooth causes irreversible biologic harm). Ethical dental jurisprudence dictates that non-maleficence and beneficence override autonomy when a patient demands medically unsound or destructive treatment.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nPrimum non nocere means: FIRST, DO NO HARM (Principle of Non-Maleficence)! Respect for patient self-determination and informed consent falls under: AUTONOMY. Truthfulness and avoiding fraudulent claims falls under: VERACITY.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Non-Maleficence = Primum Non Nocere (DO NO HARM)!\n* Beneficence = DO GOOD (promote patient's well-being).\n* Autonomy = respect patient's choice (Informed Consent).\n* Veracity = TRUTHFULNESS; Justice = FAIRNESS.",
