    "originalPrint": "Informed Consent in Dental Practice:\n- Definition: An autonomous authorization by a patient for a medical/dental intervention; Based on the legal doctrine of bodily self-determination.\n- Essential Elements of Valid Informed Consent:\n  1. Information Disclosure: Dentist must disclose in understandable terms:\n     * Nature of the disease / diagnosis.\n     * Proposed treatment procedure and objectives.\n     * Potential material risks, hazards, and known complications.\n     * Feasible alternative treatments (including advantages/disadvantages).\n     * Prognosis and consequences of NON-TREATMENT.\n  2. Competence (Capacity): Patient must have legal mental capacity to make healthcare decisions (adult >=18 years, conscious, sound mind; minors require parent/legal guardian consent).\n  3. Voluntariness: Decision must be free from coercion, fraud, or undue influence.\n- Legal Exceptions to Informed Consent:\n  1. Emergency Doctrine: Immediate threat to patient's life or limb, patient unconscious or unable to communicate, immediate action required to prevent death/serious injury.\n  2. Therapeutic Privilege: Rare defense where disclosure would cause severe immediate psychological/physical harm.\n  3. Valid Express Waiver by patient.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nTreating a patient without valid informed consent exposes the practitioner to civil liability for battery (unlawful physical touching) or negligence (breach of duty of disclosure). Under the modern 'reasonable patient standard', the dentist must disclose all 'material risks'\u2014defined as any risk that a reasonable person in the patient's position would consider significant in deciding whether to undergo or forgo treatment.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nOperating on a patient without informed consent constitutes: BATTERY (intentional tort) or Professional Negligence! Legal exception: Emergency doctrine (unconscious patient facing life-threatening emergency). Consent from minors requires parent or legal guardian.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* No consent = BATTERY (unauthorized physical touching)!\n* Must explain: Diagnosis, treatment, RISKS, ALTERNATIVES, and cost!\n* Emergency exception: patient is dying/unconscious and no family present.",
    "summary": "Informed Consent in Dental Practice:\n- Definition: An autonomous authorization by a patient for a medical/dental intervention; Based on the legal doctrine of bodily self-determination.\n- Essential Elements of Valid Informed Consent:\n  1. Information Disclosure: Dentist must disclose in understandable terms:\n     * Nature of the disease / diagnosis.\n     * Proposed treatment procedure and objectives.\n     * Potential material risks, hazards, and known complications.\n     * Feasible alternative treatments (including advantages/disadvantages).\n     * Prognosis and consequences of NON-TREATMENT.\n  2. Competence (Capacity): Patient must have legal mental capacity to make healthcare decisions (adult >=18 years, conscious, sound mind; minors require parent/legal guardian consent).\n  3. Voluntariness: Decision must be free from coercion, fraud, or undue influence.\n- Legal Exceptions to Informed Consent:\n  1. Emergency Doctrine: Immediate threat to patient's life or limb, patient unconscious or unable to communicate, immediate action required to prevent death/serious injury.\n  2. Therapeutic Privilege: Rare defense where disclosure would cause severe immediate psychological/physical harm.\n  3. Valid Express Waiver by patient.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nTreating a patient without valid informed consent exposes the practitioner to civil liability for battery (unlawful physical touching) or negligence (breach of duty of disclosure). Under the modern 'reasonable patient standard', the dentist must disclose all 'material risks'\u2014defined as any risk that a reasonable person in the patient's position would consider significant in deciding whether to undergo or forgo treatment.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nOperating on a patient without informed consent constitutes: BATTERY (intentional tort) or Professional Negligence! Legal exception: Emergency doctrine (unconscious patient facing life-threatening emergency). Consent from minors requires parent or legal guardian.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* No consent = BATTERY (unauthorized physical touching)!\n* Must explain: Diagnosis, treatment, RISKS, ALTERNATIVES, and cost!\n* Emergency exception: patient is dying/unconscious and no family present.",
