    "originalPrint": "Dental Records, Confidentiality & Ownership:\n- Ownership of Dental Records & Radiographs:\n  * Physical Ownership: The PHYSICAL dental chart, written clinical notes, study casts, and original radiographic films are the LEGAL PROPERTY OF THE DENTIST (custodian of records).\n  * Intellectual / Information Ownership: The CLINICAL INFORMATION contained within the record belongs to the PATIENT.\n  * Patient Rights: Patient has the legal right to inspect their records and receive a legible copy (or duplicate radiographs) upon request and payment of reasonable copying fees; Dentist CANNOT withhold records because of an unpaid balance/bill!\n- Retention Period:\n  * Dental records, charts, and radiographs must be securely preserved and retained for at least TEN (10) YEARS from the date of the last patient visit.\n- Privileged Communication & Confidentiality:\n  * Physician/Dentist-Patient Privileged Communication Rule (Rules of Court);\n  * Dentist cannot be compelled to disclose confidential communications or treatment information without patient consent, except:\n    1. Order of a court of competent jurisdiction.\n    2. When public health/safety requires (notifiable communicable diseases, gunshot wounds).\n    3. In self-defense against a malpractice lawsuit filed by the patient.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe dental record is the clinician's primary legal defense in malpractice litigation. Under the evidentiary rule 'if it was not written down, it did not happen', detailed, chronological chart notes documenting informed consent, pre-op blood pressure, local anesthetic dosage, and post-op instructions are given high probative weight by courts. Retention for 10 years matches the statutory prescription period for contract and tort actions in the Civil Code.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nWho owns the original dental records and radiographs? THE DENTIST owns the physical records, but the patient owns the information! Dentist cannot withhold copies for unpaid fees. Dental records must be retained for at least: TEN (10) YEARS.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* DENTIST OWNS the physical chart and X-ray films! Patient owns the information.\n* You CANNOT refuse to give copies just because patient hasn't paid their bill!\n* Must keep dental records for at least 10 YEARS!",
    "summary": "Dental Records, Confidentiality & Ownership:\n- Ownership of Dental Records & Radiographs:\n  * Physical Ownership: The PHYSICAL dental chart, written clinical notes, study casts, and original radiographic films are the LEGAL PROPERTY OF THE DENTIST (custodian of records).\n  * Intellectual / Information Ownership: The CLINICAL INFORMATION contained within the record belongs to the PATIENT.\n  * Patient Rights: Patient has the legal right to inspect their records and receive a legible copy (or duplicate radiographs) upon request and payment of reasonable copying fees; Dentist CANNOT withhold records because of an unpaid balance/bill!\n- Retention Period:\n  * Dental records, charts, and radiographs must be securely preserved and retained for at least TEN (10) YEARS from the date of the last patient visit.\n- Privileged Communication & Confidentiality:\n  * Physician/Dentist-Patient Privileged Communication Rule (Rules of Court);\n  * Dentist cannot be compelled to disclose confidential communications or treatment information without patient consent, except:\n    1. Order of a court of competent jurisdiction.\n    2. When public health/safety requires (notifiable communicable diseases, gunshot wounds).\n    3. In self-defense against a malpractice lawsuit filed by the patient.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe dental record is the clinician's primary legal defense in malpractice litigation. Under the evidentiary rule 'if it was not written down, it did not happen', detailed, chronological chart notes documenting informed consent, pre-op blood pressure, local anesthetic dosage, and post-op instructions are given high probative weight by courts. Retention for 10 years matches the statutory prescription period for contract and tort actions in the Civil Code.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nWho owns the original dental records and radiographs? THE DENTIST owns the physical records, but the patient owns the information! Dentist cannot withhold copies for unpaid fees. Dental records must be retained for at least: TEN (10) YEARS.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* DENTIST OWNS the physical chart and X-ray films! Patient owns the information.\n* You CANNOT refuse to give copies just because patient hasn't paid their bill!\n* Must keep dental records for at least 10 YEARS!",
