    "pageNumber": 256,
    "title": "Medical Malpractice & The Four D's of Dental Negligence",
    "subject": "Dental Jurisprudence",
    "day": "Day 3 (11:00 AM \u2022 10%) - Jurisprudence & Ethics",
    "author": "Atty. Marvin Joseph M. Ocampo",
    "keywords": [
      "malpractice",
      "negligence",
      "four d's",
      "duty",
      "dereliction",
      "direct cause",
      "damages",
      "standard of care"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Dental Malpractice & The Four D's of Negligence:\n- Malpractice:\n  * Professional negligence; The failure of a dentist to exercise that reasonable degree of skill, care, and diligence ordinarily exercised by other reputable dentists in the same or similar locality under similar circumstances.\n- The Four D's of Negligence (All 4 Must Be Proven by Plaintiff by Preponderance of Evidence!):\n  1. DUTY: The existence of a legal dentist-patient relationship; Established as soon as the dentist undertakes diagnosis or treatment of the patient.\n  2. DERELICTION (Breach of Duty): The dentist failed to conform to the accepted 'standard of care'; Act of commission (doing something a prudent dentist would not do) or omission (failing to do something a prudent dentist would do).\n  3. DIRECT CAUSATION (Proximate Cause): The breach of duty was the direct and natural cause that produced the injury in continuous sequence, without which the injury would not have occurred.\n  4. DAMAGES: The patient suffered actual, compensable injury, physical harm, disability, emotional trauma, or financial loss.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nAn adverse outcome is not synonymous with negligence. A dentist does not guarantee a cure; the legal contract is one of 'reasonable effort and standard care'. If a root canal file breaks inside a severely curved canal, negligence does NOT exist if the dentist followed standard technique, immediately informed the patient, documented the occurrence, and referred appropriately. Negligence arises only if the dentist concealed the broken file or acted recklessly.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nThe Four D's of Negligence: 1) Duty; 2) Dereliction (Breach of duty); 3) Direct Cause (Proximate causation); 4) Damages! If any one of the four elements is missing, a malpractice suit cannot succeed.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Malpractice = The 4 D's:\n  1. DUTY (Dentist-patient relationship)\n  2. DERELICTION (Broke the standard of care)\n  3. DIRECT CAUSE (Dentist's action directly caused injury)\n  4. DAMAGES (Real physical or financial harm).",
    "summary": "Dental Malpractice & The Four D's of Negligence:\n- Malpractice:\n  * Professional negligence; The failure of a dentist to exercise that reasonable degree of skill, care, and diligence ordinarily exercised by other reputable dentists in the same or similar locality under similar circumstances.\n- The Four D's of Negligence (All 4 Must Be Proven by Plaintiff by Preponderance of Evidence!):\n  1. DUTY: The existence of a legal dentist-patient relationship; Established as soon as the dentist undertakes diagnosis or treatment of the patient.\n  2. DERELICTION (Breach of Duty): The dentist failed to conform to the accepted 'standard of care'; Act of commission (doing something a prudent dentist would not do) or omission (failing to do something a prudent dentist would do).\n  3. DIRECT CAUSATION (Proximate Cause): The breach of duty was the direct and natural cause that produced the injury in continuous sequence, without which the injury would not have occurred.\n  4. DAMAGES: The patient suffered actual, compensable injury, physical harm, disability, emotional trauma, or financial loss.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nAn adverse outcome is not synonymous with negligence. A dentist does not guarantee a cure; the legal contract is one of 'reasonable effort and standard care'. If a root canal file breaks inside a severely curved canal, negligence does NOT exist if the dentist followed standard technique, immediately informed the patient, documented the occurrence, and referred appropriately. Negligence arises only if the dentist concealed the broken file or acted recklessly.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nThe Four D's of Negligence: 1) Duty; 2) Dereliction (Breach of duty); 3) Direct Cause (Proximate causation); 4) Damages! If any one of the four elements is missing, a malpractice suit cannot succeed.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Malpractice = The 4 D's:\n  1. DUTY (Dentist-patient relationship)\n  2. DERELICTION (Broke the standard of care)\n  3. DIRECT CAUSE (Dentist's action directly caused injury)\n  4. DAMAGES (Real physical or financial harm).",
