    "author": "Atty. Marvin Joseph M. Ocampo",
    "keywords": [
      "liabilities",
      "criminal liability",
      "civil liability",
      "administrative liability",
      "burden of proof",
      "quantum of evidence"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "The Three Types of Legal Liability in Dental Practice:\n- 1. Criminal Liability:\n  * Offense Against: The State / Society (violation of penal statute, e.g., Reckless Imprudence Resulting in Homicide, Illegal Practice of Dentistry).\n  * Quantum of Evidence Required: PROOF BEYOND REASONABLE DOUBT (highest standard of evidence).\n  * Penalty: Imprisonment (incarceration) and/or Criminal Fine.\n- 2. Civil Liability:\n  * Offense Against: The Private Individual (Breach of contract, Tort / Quasi-delict, Professional Negligence).\n  * Quantum of Evidence Required: PREPONDERANCE OF EVIDENCE (evidence that has greater weight or is more convincing).\n  * Penalty: DAMAGES (monetary compensation: Actual, Moral, Exemplary, Nominal, Liquidated, Temperate damages).\n- 3. Administrative Liability:\n  * Offense Against: The Profession / Regulatory Body (Violation of RA 9484, PRC Rules, Code of Ethics).\n  * Adjudicated by: Professional Regulatory Board of Dentistry (BOD) / PRC.\n  * Quantum of Evidence Required: SUBSTANTIAL EVIDENCE (relevant evidence that a reasonable mind might accept as adequate to support a conclusion).\n  * Penalty: Reprimand, SUSPENSION, or REVOCATION of Certificate of Registration.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nA single clinical catastrophe can trigger all three legal proceedings simultaneously. For example, injecting 10 carpules of local anesthetic with epinephrine into a cardiac patient who dies from ventricular fibrillation can result in: 1) Criminal prosecution for Reckless Imprudence; 2) Civil lawsuit for multi-million peso wrongful death damages; and 3) Administrative hearing revoking the practitioner's PRC license for gross negligence.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nQuantum of Evidence by court: Criminal = Proof BEYOND REASONABLE DOUBT! Civil = PREPONDERANCE OF EVIDENCE! Administrative (PRC) = SUBSTANTIAL EVIDENCE! Administrative penalty is suspension or revocation of license.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Criminal = Prison/Fine (Proof BEYOND REASONABLE DOUBT).\n* Civil = Money Damages (PREPONDERANCE of evidence).\n* Administrative (PRC) = License revoked/suspended (SUBSTANTIAL evidence).",
    "summary": "The Three Types of Legal Liability in Dental Practice:\n- 1. Criminal Liability:\n  * Offense Against: The State / Society (violation of penal statute, e.g., Reckless Imprudence Resulting in Homicide, Illegal Practice of Dentistry).\n  * Quantum of Evidence Required: PROOF BEYOND REASONABLE DOUBT (highest standard of evidence).\n  * Penalty: Imprisonment (incarceration) and/or Criminal Fine.\n- 2. Civil Liability:\n  * Offense Against: The Private Individual (Breach of contract, Tort / Quasi-delict, Professional Negligence).\n  * Quantum of Evidence Required: PREPONDERANCE OF EVIDENCE (evidence that has greater weight or is more convincing).\n  * Penalty: DAMAGES (monetary compensation: Actual, Moral, Exemplary, Nominal, Liquidated, Temperate damages).\n- 3. Administrative Liability:\n  * Offense Against: The Profession / Regulatory Body (Violation of RA 9484, PRC Rules, Code of Ethics).\n  * Adjudicated by: Professional Regulatory Board of Dentistry (BOD) / PRC.\n  * Quantum of Evidence Required: SUBSTANTIAL EVIDENCE (relevant evidence that a reasonable mind might accept as adequate to support a conclusion).\n  * Penalty: Reprimand, SUSPENSION, or REVOCATION of Certificate of Registration.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nA single clinical catastrophe can trigger all three legal proceedings simultaneously. For example, injecting 10 carpules of local anesthetic with epinephrine into a cardiac patient who dies from ventricular fibrillation can result in: 1) Criminal prosecution for Reckless Imprudence; 2) Civil lawsuit for multi-million peso wrongful death damages; and 3) Administrative hearing revoking the practitioner's PRC license for gross negligence.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nQuantum of Evidence by court: Criminal = Proof BEYOND REASONABLE DOUBT! Civil = PREPONDERANCE OF EVIDENCE! Administrative (PRC) = SUBSTANTIAL EVIDENCE! Administrative penalty is suspension or revocation of license.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Criminal = Prison/Fine (Proof BEYOND REASONABLE DOUBT).\n* Civil = Money Damages (PREPONDERANCE of evidence).\n* Administrative (PRC) = License revoked/suspended (SUBSTANTIAL evidence).",
