    "pageNumber": 261,
    "title": "Community Dentistry: Definitions & Concepts of Health",
    "subject": "Community Dentistry",
    "day": "Day 1 (2:00 PM \u2022 10%) - Community Dentistry",
    "author": "Wesley A. Bombarda, DMD",
    "keywords": [
      "community dentistry",
      "who definition of health",
      "biomedical concept",
      "ecological concept",
      "psychosocial concept",
      "holistic concept"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "DMF Dental Training Center Cover:\nCommunity Dentistry - By: Wesley A. Bombarda, DMD\nConcepts of Health & Public Health:\n- Health (WHO Definition, 1948): 'A state of complete physical, mental, and social well-being, and NOT merely the absence of disease or infirmity.'\n- The 4 Evolving Concepts of Health:\n  1. Biomedical Concept: Traditional view; Health is the absence of disease; Based on the germ theory; Body viewed as a machine; Focused only on physical pathology.\n  2. Ecological Concept: Health is a dynamic equilibrium / state of balance between MAN (Host) and his ENVIRONMENT; Maladjustment leads to disease.\n  3. Psychosocial Concept: Health is influenced not only by biology, but also by social, psychological, cultural, economic, and political factors.\n  4. Holistic Concept: Synthesis of biomedical, ecological, and psychosocial concepts; Recognizes the strength of all dimensions of health (physical, mental, emotional, spiritual, vocational).\n- Public Health (Winslow, 1920): The science and art of preventing disease, prolonging life, and promoting physical health through organized community efforts.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nCommunity dentistry shifts focus from the individual patient in a dental operatory to the aggregate population. Disease distribution is shaped by social determinants of health (income, education, water fluoridation, access to care). Winslow's definition highlights that individual curative interventions cannot solve widespread chronic diseases; sustainable oral health improvements require collective public policies, environmental engineering, and preventive community water fluoridation.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nWHO Definition of Health (1948): Complete state of physical, mental, and social well-being, not merely the absence of disease! Ecological concept views health as a dynamic balance between man and his environment. Winslow is the Father of Public Health.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* WHO Health = Physical + Mental + Social well-being (NOT just no sickness)!\n* Ecological concept = Balance between MAN and ENVIRONMENT.\n* Community dentistry treats the whole COMMUNITY, not just one chair patient!",
    "summary": "DMF Dental Training Center Cover:\nCommunity Dentistry - By: Wesley A. Bombarda, DMD\nConcepts of Health & Public Health:\n- Health (WHO Definition, 1948): 'A state of complete physical, mental, and social well-being, and NOT merely the absence of disease or infirmity.'\n- The 4 Evolving Concepts of Health:\n  1. Biomedical Concept: Traditional view; Health is the absence of disease; Based on the germ theory; Body viewed as a machine; Focused only on physical pathology.\n  2. Ecological Concept: Health is a dynamic equilibrium / state of balance between MAN (Host) and his ENVIRONMENT; Maladjustment leads to disease.\n  3. Psychosocial Concept: Health is influenced not only by biology, but also by social, psychological, cultural, economic, and political factors.\n  4. Holistic Concept: Synthesis of biomedical, ecological, and psychosocial concepts; Recognizes the strength of all dimensions of health (physical, mental, emotional, spiritual, vocational).\n- Public Health (Winslow, 1920): The science and art of preventing disease, prolonging life, and promoting physical health through organized community efforts.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nCommunity dentistry shifts focus from the individual patient in a dental operatory to the aggregate population. Disease distribution is shaped by social determinants of health (income, education, water fluoridation, access to care). Winslow's definition highlights that individual curative interventions cannot solve widespread chronic diseases; sustainable oral health improvements require collective public policies, environmental engineering, and preventive community water fluoridation.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nWHO Definition of Health (1948): Complete state of physical, mental, and social well-being, not merely the absence of disease! Ecological concept views health as a dynamic balance between man and his environment. Winslow is the Father of Public Health.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* WHO Health = Physical + Mental + Social well-being (NOT just no sickness)!\n* Ecological concept = Balance between MAN and ENVIRONMENT.\n* Community dentistry treats the whole COMMUNITY, not just one chair patient!",
