    "originalPrint": "Levels of Prevention - Part 2 (Secondary & Tertiary Prevention):\n- LEVEL 2: SECONDARY PREVENTION (Occurs during Early Pathogenesis Period):\n  * Purpose: To detect disease at the EARLIEST possible stage and halt its progression; Reduces disease prevalence by shortening duration.\n  * Two Sub-Phases of Secondary Prevention:\n    1. Early Diagnosis and Prompt Treatment:\n       * Screening examinations, bitewing radiographs for incipient proximal caries, biopsy of suspicious oral leukoplakia, Scaling and Root Planing (SRP) for gingivitis / early periodontitis, Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART), Class I and II composite/amalgam fillings.\n    2. Disability Limitation:\n       * Interventions to prevent further complications or sequelae of established disease.\n       * Dental Examples: Pulp capping, Root Canal Therapy (Endodontics) to prevent tooth loss, simple tooth extractions to prevent cellulitis/Ludwig's angina.\n- LEVEL 3: TERTIARY PREVENTION (Occurs during Late Pathogenesis / Rehabilitation Period):\n  * Purpose: To restore lost function, form, and esthetics, and rehabilitate the patient after irreversible tissue damage has occurred.\n  * Phase: REHABILITATION.\n  * Dental Examples: Complete dentures, Removable Partial Dentures (RPDs), Fixed bridges, Dental Implants, Maxillofacial prosthetics (obturators for cleft palate), Orthognathic surgery.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nClassifying interventions depends on timing relative to tissue destruction. Placing a sealant on a virgin molar is Primary Prevention. Excavating incipient decay and placing a resin filling is Secondary Prevention (Early Diagnosis and Prompt Treatment). Performing root canal therapy limits disability by preserving the root. Fabricating a fixed bridge or complete denture to restore lost masticatory function after tooth loss is Tertiary Prevention (Rehabilitation).",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nExamples by Level of Prevention: Scaling and root planing for gingivitis = SECONDARY prevention (early diagnosis/prompt treatment). Endodontics = SECONDARY prevention (disability limitation). Complete and partial dentures, implants, bridges = TERTIARY prevention (rehabilitation).",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* SECONDARY = Catch it early! (Fillings, SRP, Root Canal Therapy to save the tooth).\n* TERTIARY = REHABILITATION! (Dentures, Bridges, Implants to replace missing teeth)!",
    "summary": "Levels of Prevention - Part 2 (Secondary & Tertiary Prevention):\n- LEVEL 2: SECONDARY PREVENTION (Occurs during Early Pathogenesis Period):\n  * Purpose: To detect disease at the EARLIEST possible stage and halt its progression; Reduces disease prevalence by shortening duration.\n  * Two Sub-Phases of Secondary Prevention:\n    1. Early Diagnosis and Prompt Treatment:\n       * Screening examinations, bitewing radiographs for incipient proximal caries, biopsy of suspicious oral leukoplakia, Scaling and Root Planing (SRP) for gingivitis / early periodontitis, Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART), Class I and II composite/amalgam fillings.\n    2. Disability Limitation:\n       * Interventions to prevent further complications or sequelae of established disease.\n       * Dental Examples: Pulp capping, Root Canal Therapy (Endodontics) to prevent tooth loss, simple tooth extractions to prevent cellulitis/Ludwig's angina.\n- LEVEL 3: TERTIARY PREVENTION (Occurs during Late Pathogenesis / Rehabilitation Period):\n  * Purpose: To restore lost function, form, and esthetics, and rehabilitate the patient after irreversible tissue damage has occurred.\n  * Phase: REHABILITATION.\n  * Dental Examples: Complete dentures, Removable Partial Dentures (RPDs), Fixed bridges, Dental Implants, Maxillofacial prosthetics (obturators for cleft palate), Orthognathic surgery.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nClassifying interventions depends on timing relative to tissue destruction. Placing a sealant on a virgin molar is Primary Prevention. Excavating incipient decay and placing a resin filling is Secondary Prevention (Early Diagnosis and Prompt Treatment). Performing root canal therapy limits disability by preserving the root. Fabricating a fixed bridge or complete denture to restore lost masticatory function after tooth loss is Tertiary Prevention (Rehabilitation).\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nExamples by Level of Prevention: Scaling and root planing for gingivitis = SECONDARY prevention (early diagnosis/prompt treatment). Endodontics = SECONDARY prevention (disability limitation). Complete and partial dentures, implants, bridges = TERTIARY prevention (rehabilitation).\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* SECONDARY = Catch it early! (Fillings, SRP, Root Canal Therapy to save the tooth).\n* TERTIARY = REHABILITATION! (Dentures, Bridges, Implants to replace missing teeth)!",
