    "pageNumber": 266,
    "title": "Measures of Morbidity: Incidence vs Prevalence",
    "subject": "Community Dentistry",
    "day": "Day 1 (2:00 PM \u2022 10%) - Community Dentistry",
    "author": "Wesley A. Bombarda, DMD",
    "keywords": [
      "morbidity",
      "incidence",
      "prevalence",
      "point prevalence",
      "period prevalence",
      "attack rate",
      "formulas"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Measures of Disease Frequency: Incidence vs Prevalence:\n- INCIDENCE:\n  * Definition: The number of NEW CASES of a disease that occur in a population at risk during a specified period of time.\n  * Formula: Incidence Rate = (Number of NEW cases of disease during a specified period / Total population at risk during that period) x 1,000 (or 100,000).\n  * Key Feature: Measures the RATE OF ATTACK or risk of developing a disease; Reflects acute conditions and etiology; Best for evaluating preventive program effectiveness.\n- PREVALENCE:\n  * Definition: The TOTAL NUMBER OF EXISTING CASES (both OLD cases and NEW cases) of a disease present in a population at a specific point or period in time.\n  * Point Prevalence: Proportion of population that has disease at a single POINT in time (e.g., survey on March 1st).\n  * Period Prevalence: Proportion of population that has disease at any time during a specified PERIOD (e.g., over year 2026).\n  * Formula: Prevalence = (Total number of existing cases / Total population at that point in time) x 100 (percentage).\n- Relationship between Incidence and Prevalence:\n  * Formula: Prevalence = Incidence x Average Duration of Disease (P = I x D).\n  * If disease is incurable and chronic (like caries or periodontitis), prevalence accumulates to high levels even if incidence is low.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nImagine a bathtub: water flowing from the faucet represents Incidence (new cases entering the population). The water filling the tub represents Prevalence (total existing cases). Water draining through the tub represents Recovery or Death. For non-fatal, chronic irreversible oral diseases where recovery is impossible (an unfilled cavity never naturally disappears), prevalence remains high throughout life unless incidence is stopped at the source.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nDistinguish Incidence vs Prevalence: Incidence = NEW cases occurring over time (measures risk/rate of attack)! Prevalence = ALL EXISTING cases (both old and new) at a specific time (measures total disease burden)! Formula: Prevalence = Incidence x Duration (P = I x D).",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* INCIDENCE = NEW cases only (incoming rate)!\n* PREVALENCE = ALL existing cases (old + new) at one time!\n* Prevalence = Incidence x Duration (P = I x D).",
    "summary": "Measures of Disease Frequency: Incidence vs Prevalence:\n- INCIDENCE:\n  * Definition: The number of NEW CASES of a disease that occur in a population at risk during a specified period of time.\n  * Formula: Incidence Rate = (Number of NEW cases of disease during a specified period / Total population at risk during that period) x 1,000 (or 100,000).\n  * Key Feature: Measures the RATE OF ATTACK or risk of developing a disease; Reflects acute conditions and etiology; Best for evaluating preventive program effectiveness.\n- PREVALENCE:\n  * Definition: The TOTAL NUMBER OF EXISTING CASES (both OLD cases and NEW cases) of a disease present in a population at a specific point or period in time.\n  * Point Prevalence: Proportion of population that has disease at a single POINT in time (e.g., survey on March 1st).\n  * Period Prevalence: Proportion of population that has disease at any time during a specified PERIOD (e.g., over year 2026).\n  * Formula: Prevalence = (Total number of existing cases / Total population at that point in time) x 100 (percentage).\n- Relationship between Incidence and Prevalence:\n  * Formula: Prevalence = Incidence x Average Duration of Disease (P = I x D).\n  * If disease is incurable and chronic (like caries or periodontitis), prevalence accumulates to high levels even if incidence is low.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nImagine a bathtub: water flowing from the faucet represents Incidence (new cases entering the population). The water filling the tub represents Prevalence (total existing cases). Water draining through the tub represents Recovery or Death. For non-fatal, chronic irreversible oral diseases where recovery is impossible (an unfilled cavity never naturally disappears), prevalence remains high throughout life unless incidence is stopped at the source.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nDistinguish Incidence vs Prevalence: Incidence = NEW cases occurring over time (measures risk/rate of attack)! Prevalence = ALL EXISTING cases (both old and new) at a specific time (measures total disease burden)! Formula: Prevalence = Incidence x Duration (P = I x D).\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* INCIDENCE = NEW cases only (incoming rate)!\n* PREVALENCE = ALL existing cases (old + new) at one time!\n* Prevalence = Incidence x Duration (P = I x D).",
