    "originalPrint": "Caries Indices for the Deciduous (Primary) Dentition:\n- The 'def' Index (Gruebbel, 1944):\n  * Designed specifically for the 20 primary deciduous teeth;\n  * d = Decayed primary tooth.\n  * e = Extraction indicated (tooth so severely destroyed by caries that it cannot be restored and must be extracted).\n  * f = Filled primary tooth without recurrent caries.\n  * Note: 'm' (missing) is deliberately OMITTED in Gruebbel's original def index because in mixed dentition it is impossible to determine whether a missing primary incisor or molar was exfoliated naturally or extracted due to caries!\n- Modifications of Primary Caries Indices:\n  * dft / dfs Index: Decayed and Filled teeth/surfaces (ignores missing teeth entirely; highly reliable for young children).\n  * dmft Index: Can be used in young children under 5 years of age (before natural exfoliation begins at age 6), where any missing tooth is assumed to be extracted due to caries.\n- Total Possible Scores in Deciduous Dentition:\n  * Maximum def-t / dft = 20 teeth.\n  * Maximum def-s / dfs = 88 surfaces (8 molars x 5 surfaces = 40; 12 anterior x 4 surfaces = 48; Total = 88 surfaces).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nEpidemiological bias arises in mixed dentition when applying conventional 'M' criteria. A missing primary incisor in a 7-year-old child typically reflects normal physiologic root resorption and shedding, not dental decay. Gruebbel resolved this by eliminating 'm' and creating 'e' (extraction indicated), capturing only primary teeth currently present in the mouth that require extraction due to rampant caries.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nWhy is 'missing' (m) omitted in the 'def' index for primary teeth? Because in mixed dentition, it is impossible to distinguish between natural exfoliation and caries extraction! In 'def', 'e' means: EXTRACTION INDICATED due to caries. Max defs = 88 surfaces.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* 'def' index = for PRIMARY / baby teeth (Gruebbel)!\n* 'e' = EXTRACTION INDICATED (too rotten to save).\n* 'm' is skipped because baby teeth fall out naturally (exfoliation)!\n* Max baby teeth = 20; Max baby surfaces = 88.",
    "summary": "Caries Indices for the Deciduous (Primary) Dentition:\n- The 'def' Index (Gruebbel, 1944):\n  * Designed specifically for the 20 primary deciduous teeth;\n  * d = Decayed primary tooth.\n  * e = Extraction indicated (tooth so severely destroyed by caries that it cannot be restored and must be extracted).\n  * f = Filled primary tooth without recurrent caries.\n  * Note: 'm' (missing) is deliberately OMITTED in Gruebbel's original def index because in mixed dentition it is impossible to determine whether a missing primary incisor or molar was exfoliated naturally or extracted due to caries!\n- Modifications of Primary Caries Indices:\n  * dft / dfs Index: Decayed and Filled teeth/surfaces (ignores missing teeth entirely; highly reliable for young children).\n  * dmft Index: Can be used in young children under 5 years of age (before natural exfoliation begins at age 6), where any missing tooth is assumed to be extracted due to caries.\n- Total Possible Scores in Deciduous Dentition:\n  * Maximum def-t / dft = 20 teeth.\n  * Maximum def-s / dfs = 88 surfaces (8 molars x 5 surfaces = 40; 12 anterior x 4 surfaces = 48; Total = 88 surfaces).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nEpidemiological bias arises in mixed dentition when applying conventional 'M' criteria. A missing primary incisor in a 7-year-old child typically reflects normal physiologic root resorption and shedding, not dental decay. Gruebbel resolved this by eliminating 'm' and creating 'e' (extraction indicated), capturing only primary teeth currently present in the mouth that require extraction due to rampant caries.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nWhy is 'missing' (m) omitted in the 'def' index for primary teeth? Because in mixed dentition, it is impossible to distinguish between natural exfoliation and caries extraction! In 'def', 'e' means: EXTRACTION INDICATED due to caries. Max defs = 88 surfaces.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* 'def' index = for PRIMARY / baby teeth (Gruebbel)!\n* 'e' = EXTRACTION INDICATED (too rotten to save).\n* 'm' is skipped because baby teeth fall out naturally (exfoliation)!\n* Max baby teeth = 20; Max baby surfaces = 88.",
