    "originalPrint": "Biostatistics: Measures of Central Tendency & Dispersion:\n- Measures of Central Tendency:\n  * Mean (Arithmetic Average): Sum of all values divided by total number of observations (x-bar = sum of X / N); Heavily influenced by extreme outliers!\n  * Median: The exact MIDDLE value in a distribution of data sorted in ascending order (50th percentile); Best measure of central tendency for SKEWED data and data with outliers.\n  * Mode: The MOST FREQUENTLY OCCURRING score/value in a distribution; Only measure of central tendency usable with nominal data.\n- Measures of Dispersion (Variability):\n  * Range: Difference between highest and lowest value (Range = Max - Min).\n  * Variance: Average of squared deviations from the mean.\n  * Standard Deviation (SD): The square root of variance; The most widely used measure of dispersion; Measures how spread out numbers are around the mean.\n- The Normal Distribution (Gaussian / Bell-Shaped Curve):\n  * Symmetrical, bell-shaped distribution where Mean = Median = Mode!\n  * Empirical Rule (68-95-99.7 Rule):\n    - Mean \u00b1 1 SD encompasses 68.2% of all data values.\n    - Mean \u00b1 2 SD encompasses 95.4% of all data values.\n    - Mean \u00b1 3 SD encompasses 99.7% of all data values.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nIn a perfectly symmetrical Gaussian distribution, mean, median, and mode coincide at the exact center. When data is skewed (e.g., dental income, DMFT in low-caries populations with a few high-caries outliers), the mean is dragged heavily toward the long tail. The median is resistant to outliers because it depends on rank order rather than magnitude, making it the most truthful summary for non-normally distributed health metrics.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nNormal Bell Curve values: Mean \u00b1 1 SD = 68.2%; Mean \u00b1 2 SD = 95.4%; Mean \u00b1 3 SD = 99.7%! In a normal distribution: Mean = Median = Mode. If data is heavily skewed by extreme values, use the MEDIAN as the measure of central tendency.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Normal Bell Curve: Mean = Median = Mode in center!\n* \u00b11 SD = 68% | \u00b12 SD = 95% | \u00b13 SD = 99.7% of data!\n* If data has extreme high/low numbers -> MEDIAN is the best average to use.",
    "summary": "Biostatistics: Measures of Central Tendency & Dispersion:\n- Measures of Central Tendency:\n  * Mean (Arithmetic Average): Sum of all values divided by total number of observations (x-bar = sum of X / N); Heavily influenced by extreme outliers!\n  * Median: The exact MIDDLE value in a distribution of data sorted in ascending order (50th percentile); Best measure of central tendency for SKEWED data and data with outliers.\n  * Mode: The MOST FREQUENTLY OCCURRING score/value in a distribution; Only measure of central tendency usable with nominal data.\n- Measures of Dispersion (Variability):\n  * Range: Difference between highest and lowest value (Range = Max - Min).\n  * Variance: Average of squared deviations from the mean.\n  * Standard Deviation (SD): The square root of variance; The most widely used measure of dispersion; Measures how spread out numbers are around the mean.\n- The Normal Distribution (Gaussian / Bell-Shaped Curve):\n  * Symmetrical, bell-shaped distribution where Mean = Median = Mode!\n  * Empirical Rule (68-95-99.7 Rule):\n    - Mean \u00b1 1 SD encompasses 68.2% of all data values.\n    - Mean \u00b1 2 SD encompasses 95.4% of all data values.\n    - Mean \u00b1 3 SD encompasses 99.7% of all data values.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nIn a perfectly symmetrical Gaussian distribution, mean, median, and mode coincide at the exact center. When data is skewed (e.g., dental income, DMFT in low-caries populations with a few high-caries outliers), the mean is dragged heavily toward the long tail. The median is resistant to outliers because it depends on rank order rather than magnitude, making it the most truthful summary for non-normally distributed health metrics.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nNormal Bell Curve values: Mean \u00b1 1 SD = 68.2%; Mean \u00b1 2 SD = 95.4%; Mean \u00b1 3 SD = 99.7%! In a normal distribution: Mean = Median = Mode. If data is heavily skewed by extreme values, use the MEDIAN as the measure of central tendency.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Normal Bell Curve: Mean = Median = Mode in center!\n* \u00b11 SD = 68% | \u00b12 SD = 95% | \u00b13 SD = 99.7% of data!\n* If data has extreme high/low numbers -> MEDIAN is the best average to use.",
