    "title": "Hypothesis Testing & Statistical Significance: p-Value & Errors",
    "subject": "Community Dentistry",
    "day": "Day 1 (2:00 PM \u2022 10%) - Community Dentistry",
    "author": "Wesley A. Bombarda, DMD",
    "keywords": [
      "hypothesis testing",
      "null hypothesis",
      "type i error",
      "type ii error",
      "p value",
      "t-test",
      "chi-square"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Hypothesis Testing & Statistical Inference:\n- The Hypotheses:\n  * Null Hypothesis (H0): Statement of NO DIFFERENCE, no relationship, or no association between variables (e.g., 'Toothpaste A is no different from Toothpaste B in reducing plaque').\n  * Alternative Hypothesis (H1): Statement that a true difference or association exists.\n- Statistical Decision Errors:\n  * Type I Error (Alpha Error - False Positive):\n    - REJECTING the Null Hypothesis when it is ACTUALLY TRUE!\n    - Concluding that a treatment works when in reality it does NOT; Set by significance level (typically alpha = 0.05 or 5%).\n  * Type II Error (Beta Error - False Negative):\n    - FAILING TO REJECT (accepting) the Null Hypothesis when it is ACTUALLY FALSE!\n    - Missing a real, effective treatment effect.\n- The p-Value (Probability Value):\n  * The probability that the observed study results occurred by RANDOM CHANCE alone;\n  * If p < 0.05: Statistically SIGNIFICANT! Reject H0 (less than 5% probability that results are due to chance).\n- Statistical Test Selection:\n  * Student's t-test: Compares means of TWO continuous independent groups.\n  * ANOVA (Analysis of Variance): Compares means of THREE OR MORE groups.\n  * Chi-Square Test (X^2): Compares CATEGORICAL / nominal data (proportions, counts).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nType I and Type II errors represent the legal analogy of justice: a Type I error is convicting an innocent person (asserting an effect that does not exist), while a Type II error is acquitting a guilty criminal (failing to identify a real treatment effect). Researchers set alpha at 0.05 to ensure that false-positive claims occur less than 1 in 20 times by chance.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nType I error (Alpha) is: Rejecting a true null hypothesis (False Positive)! Type II error (Beta) is: Failing to reject a false null hypothesis (False Negative)! Student's t-test compares TWO means; ANOVA compares THREE or more means; Chi-square tests categorical proportions.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Type I Error (Alpha) = False Alarm / False Positive (rejected true null hypothesis)!\n* Type II Error (Beta) = Missed Effect / False Negative.\n* p < 0.05 = Statistically Significant!\n* t-test = 2 groups; ANOVA = 3+ groups; Chi-Square = percentages/categories.",
    "summary": "Hypothesis Testing & Statistical Inference:\n- The Hypotheses:\n  * Null Hypothesis (H0): Statement of NO DIFFERENCE, no relationship, or no association between variables (e.g., 'Toothpaste A is no different from Toothpaste B in reducing plaque').\n  * Alternative Hypothesis (H1): Statement that a true difference or association exists.\n- Statistical Decision Errors:\n  * Type I Error (Alpha Error - False Positive):\n    - REJECTING the Null Hypothesis when it is ACTUALLY TRUE!\n    - Concluding that a treatment works when in reality it does NOT; Set by significance level (typically alpha = 0.05 or 5%).\n  * Type II Error (Beta Error - False Negative):\n    - FAILING TO REJECT (accepting) the Null Hypothesis when it is ACTUALLY FALSE!\n    - Missing a real, effective treatment effect.\n- The p-Value (Probability Value):\n  * The probability that the observed study results occurred by RANDOM CHANCE alone;\n  * If p < 0.05: Statistically SIGNIFICANT! Reject H0 (less than 5% probability that results are due to chance).\n- Statistical Test Selection:\n  * Student's t-test: Compares means of TWO continuous independent groups.\n  * ANOVA (Analysis of Variance): Compares means of THREE OR MORE groups.\n  * Chi-Square Test (X^2): Compares CATEGORICAL / nominal data (proportions, counts).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nType I and Type II errors represent the legal analogy of justice: a Type I error is convicting an innocent person (asserting an effect that does not exist), while a Type II error is acquitting a guilty criminal (failing to identify a real treatment effect). Researchers set alpha at 0.05 to ensure that false-positive claims occur less than 1 in 20 times by chance.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nType I error (Alpha) is: Rejecting a true null hypothesis (False Positive)! Type II error (Beta) is: Failing to reject a false null hypothesis (False Negative)! Student's t-test compares TWO means; ANOVA compares THREE or more means; Chi-square tests categorical proportions.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Type I Error (Alpha) = False Alarm / False Positive (rejected true null hypothesis)!\n* Type II Error (Beta) = Missed Effect / False Negative.\n* p < 0.05 = Statistically Significant!\n* t-test = 2 groups; ANOVA = 3+ groups; Chi-Square = percentages/categories.",
