    "title": "Philippine National Oral Health Policies & Programs (DOH AO 2007-0007)",
    "subject": "Community Dentistry",
    "day": "Day 1 (2:00 PM \u2022 10%) - Community Dentistry",
    "author": "Wesley A. Bombarda, DMD",
    "keywords": [
      "national oral health policy",
      "doh ao 2007-0007",
      "bohc",
      "basic oral health care",
      "fit for school",
      "caries burden"
    ],
    "originalPrint": "Philippine National Oral Health Programs & DOH Policies:\n- Oral Health Burden in the Philippines:\n  * National Health Surveys indicate that DENTAL CARIES is the most common chronic disease in the Philippines, affecting >70% to 80%+ of the population, and >90% of Filipino preschool children!\n  * High burden of periodontal disease (>75% of adults).\n- DOH Administrative Order No. 2007-0007:\n  * 'Guidelines in the Implementation of the National Policy on Oral Health'.\n  * Vision: Empowered, health-conscious Filipinos with healthy mouths.\n  * Core Strategy: Basic Oral Health Care (BOHC) Package:\n    1. Maternal & Infant BOHC: Oral health education for pregnant mothers, infant oral hygiene (cleaning gums with damp washcloth before eruption).\n    2. Preschool Children (1-5 years): Fluoride varnish applications, supervised tooth brushing programs.\n    3. School-Age Children (Fit for School Program): Daily supervised tooth brushing with fluoridated toothpaste (at least 1,000 ppm F) and handwashing in public elementary schools.\n    4. Adults & Older Persons: Caries management, periodontal treatment, dentures for edentulous elderly.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nNational surveys in the Philippines revealed that children miss millions of school days annually due to severe odontogenic toothaches and untreated abscesses, impairing academic growth and nutrition. The DOH/DepEd Essential Health Care Program ('Fit for School') institutionalized daily group tooth brushing with 1,000 ppm fluoridated toothpaste in public schools, providing an evidence-based, low-cost intervention that cut caries progression by 40-50%.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nDOH Administrative Order for National Oral Health: AO No. 2007-0007! Dental caries affects over 80-90% of young Filipino children. The BOHC package covers maternal, infant, preschool, and school-age populations.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* DOH AO 2007-0007 = National Policy on Oral Health in the Philippines!\n* Dental caries affects >80-90% of Filipino kids (huge oral health burden!).\n* Supervised daily tooth brushing in DepEd public schools reduces cavities.",
    "summary": "Philippine National Oral Health Programs & DOH Policies:\n- Oral Health Burden in the Philippines:\n  * National Health Surveys indicate that DENTAL CARIES is the most common chronic disease in the Philippines, affecting >70% to 80%+ of the population, and >90% of Filipino preschool children!\n  * High burden of periodontal disease (>75% of adults).\n- DOH Administrative Order No. 2007-0007:\n  * 'Guidelines in the Implementation of the National Policy on Oral Health'.\n  * Vision: Empowered, health-conscious Filipinos with healthy mouths.\n  * Core Strategy: Basic Oral Health Care (BOHC) Package:\n    1. Maternal & Infant BOHC: Oral health education for pregnant mothers, infant oral hygiene (cleaning gums with damp washcloth before eruption).\n    2. Preschool Children (1-5 years): Fluoride varnish applications, supervised tooth brushing programs.\n    3. School-Age Children (Fit for School Program): Daily supervised tooth brushing with fluoridated toothpaste (at least 1,000 ppm F) and handwashing in public elementary schools.\n    4. Adults & Older Persons: Caries management, periodontal treatment, dentures for edentulous elderly.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nNational surveys in the Philippines revealed that children miss millions of school days annually due to severe odontogenic toothaches and untreated abscesses, impairing academic growth and nutrition. The DOH/DepEd Essential Health Care Program ('Fit for School') institutionalized daily group tooth brushing with 1,000 ppm fluoridated toothpaste in public schools, providing an evidence-based, low-cost intervention that cut caries progression by 40-50%.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nDOH Administrative Order for National Oral Health: AO No. 2007-0007! Dental caries affects over 80-90% of young Filipino children. The BOHC package covers maternal, infant, preschool, and school-age populations.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* DOH AO 2007-0007 = National Policy on Oral Health in the Philippines!\n* Dental caries affects >80-90% of Filipino kids (huge oral health burden!).\n* Supervised daily tooth brushing in DepEd public schools reduces cavities.",
