    "originalPrint": "Cellular Organelles I - Biosynthetic Machinery:\n- Nucleus & Chromatin:\n  * Nuclear Envelope: Double membrane perforated by nuclear pore complexes; outer membrane is continuous with the rough endoplasmic reticulum.\n  * Nucleolus: Non-membrane bound dense nuclear region responsible for rRNA synthesis and ribosomal subunit assembly.\n  * Chromatin: Euchromatin (active, transcriptionally open, light-staining under light microscope) vs Heterochromatin (inactive, condensed, transcriptionally silent, dark-staining, e.g., Barr body / inactive X chromosome).\n- Endoplasmic Reticulum (ER):\n  1. Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum (RER / Granular ER):\n     * Studded with surface ribosomes; abundant in cells specialized for protein secretion (e.g., pancreatic acinar cells, plasma cells [Russell bodies], salivary serous acini, fibroblasts).\n     * Functions: Synthesis of proteins destined for export/secretion, lysosomal enzymes, and integral membrane proteins; initial N-linked glycosylation of proteins.\n     * Nissl bodies: Dense aggregates of RER and free polyribosomes in neuronal cell bodies (soma).\n  2. Smooth Endoplasmic Reticulum (SER / Agranular ER):\n     * Lacks surface ribosomes; tubular network.\n     * Functions: Lipid and phospholipid biosynthesis; Steroid hormone synthesis (abundant in adrenal cortex and gonads); Detoxification of drugs, alcohol, and metabolic toxins via Cytochrome P450 enzymes (abundant in hepatocytes); Calcium ion storage and sequestration (specialized as Sarcoplasmic Reticulum in skeletal and cardiac muscle fibers).\n- Ribosomes:\n  * Eukaryotic (80S: 60S large subunit + 40S small subunit); Prokaryotic (70S: 50S + 30S).\n  * Free Polyribosomes: Synthesize cytoplasmic, structural, and mitochondrial proteins (remain inside cell).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nCellular ultrastructure directly reflects clinical cytology. Cells producing abundant protein secretions (like plasma cells synthesizing antibodies or ameloblasts secreting enamel matrix proteins) are densely packed with rough endoplasmic reticulum and an expansive Golgi apparatus. Conversely, steroid-synthesizing cells (adrenal cortex) and detoxifying hepatocytes have extensive smooth endoplasmic reticulum networks.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nRough ER = Protein secretion and lysosomal enzyme synthesis. Smooth ER = Lipid & steroid synthesis, drug detoxification (Cytochrome P450), and Ca2+ storage (sarcoplasmic reticulum). Nissl bodies in neurons = RER! Nucleolus = rRNA synthesis.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* RER = ROUGH = RIBOSOMES = PROTEIN synthesis for export (like antibodies & saliva)!\n* SER = SMOOTH = STEROIDS, LIPIDS, DETOX (Liver) & Calcium storage (Muscle)!\n* Nissl granules = RER inside nerve cells!",
    "summary": "Cellular Organelles I - Biosynthetic Machinery:\n- Nucleus & Chromatin:\n  * Nuclear Envelope: Double membrane perforated by nuclear pore complexes; outer membrane is continuous with the rough endoplasmic reticulum.\n  * Nucleolus: Non-membrane bound dense nuclear region responsible for rRNA synthesis and ribosomal subunit assembly.\n  * Chromatin: Euchromatin (active, transcriptionally open, light-staining under light microscope) vs Heterochromatin (inactive, condensed, transcriptionally silent, dark-staining, e.g., Barr body / inactive X chromosome).\n- Endoplasmic Reticulum (ER):\n  1. Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum (RER / Granular ER):\n     * Studded with surface ribosomes; abundant in cells specialized for protein secretion (e.g., pancreatic acinar cells, plasma cells [Russell bodies], salivary serous acini, fibroblasts).\n     * Functions: Synthesis of proteins destined for export/secretion, lysosomal enzymes, and integral membrane proteins; initial N-linked glycosylation of proteins.\n     * Nissl bodies: Dense aggregates of RER and free polyribosomes in neuronal cell bodies (soma).\n  2. Smooth Endoplasmic Reticulum (SER / Agranular ER):\n     * Lacks surface ribosomes; tubular network.\n     * Functions: Lipid and phospholipid biosynthesis; Steroid hormone synthesis (abundant in adrenal cortex and gonads); Detoxification of drugs, alcohol, and metabolic toxins via Cytochrome P450 enzymes (abundant in hepatocytes); Calcium ion storage and sequestration (specialized as Sarcoplasmic Reticulum in skeletal and cardiac muscle fibers).\n- Ribosomes:\n  * Eukaryotic (80S: 60S large subunit + 40S small subunit); Prokaryotic (70S: 50S + 30S).\n  * Free Polyribosomes: Synthesize cytoplasmic, structural, and mitochondrial proteins (remain inside cell).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nCellular ultrastructure directly reflects clinical cytology. Cells producing abundant protein secretions (like plasma cells synthesizing antibodies or ameloblasts secreting enamel matrix proteins) are densely packed with rough endoplasmic reticulum and an expansive Golgi apparatus. Conversely, steroid-synthesizing cells (adrenal cortex) and detoxifying hepatocytes have extensive smooth endoplasmic reticulum networks.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nRough ER = Protein secretion and lysosomal enzyme synthesis. Smooth ER = Lipid & steroid synthesis, drug detoxification (Cytochrome P450), and Ca2+ storage (sarcoplasmic reticulum). Nissl bodies in neurons = RER! Nucleolus = rRNA synthesis.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* RER = ROUGH = RIBOSOMES = PROTEIN synthesis for export (like antibodies & saliva)!\n* SER = SMOOTH = STEROIDS, LIPIDS, DETOX (Liver) & Calcium storage (Muscle)!\n* Nissl granules = RER inside nerve cells!",
