    "originalPrint": "Cellular Organelles II - Processing, Degradation & Oxidation:\n- Golgi Apparatus:\n  * Cisternae stacks with polarized faces: Cis-face (entry face receiving transport vesicles from RER) and Trans-face (exit/maturation face releasing secretory granules or lysosomes).\n  * Functions: Post-translational modification of proteins (O-linked glycosylation, sulfation, phosphorylation of mannose residues); sorting, packaging, and shipping proteins to lysosomes, secretory vesicles, or plasma membrane.\n- Lysosomes:\n  * Spherical membrane-bound organelles containing >40 ACID HYDROLASES (acid phosphatase, proteases, lipases, nucleases) with optimal activity at acidic pH (4.5 - 5.0 maintained by membrane H+ ATPase pump).\n  * Functions:\n    * Heterophagy: Degradation of extracellular material engulfed by phagocytosis or pinocytosis.\n    * Autophagy: Degradation of redundant, damaged, or aged cellular organelles (e.g., turning over old mitochondria).\n    * Crinophagy: Disposal of excess secretory granules.\n  * Lysosomal Storage Diseases: Genetic enzyme deficiencies causing toxic metabolite accumulation (e.g., Tay-Sachs disease [hexosaminidase A deficiency], Gaucher disease [glucocerebrosidase deficiency]).\n  * Residual Bodies / Lipofuscin: Indigestible lipid residues accumulating in aging non-dividing cells (cardiac myocytes, neurons; known as 'wear-and-tear' or aging pigment).\n- Peroxisomes (Microbodies):\n  * Membrane-bound organelles containing OXIDATIVE ENZYMES: Urates oxidase, D-amino acid oxidase, and abundant CATALASE.\n  * Functions: Beta-oxidation of very long-chain fatty acids (VLCFA); Synthesis of plasmalogens (phospholipids in myelin); Catalase breaks down toxic Hydrogen Peroxide (H2O2) into H2O and O2; Drug and alcohol detoxification in liver.\n  * Zellweger syndrome: Peroxisomal biogenesis defect causing fatal VLCFA accumulation.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nLysosomes protect the host through intracellular digestion, but their hydrolytic contents are strictly isolated behind a membrane with an acidic luminal microenvironment. If a lysosome ruptures, the neutral cytoplasmic pH (7.2) immediately inactivates acid hydrolases, preventing accidental autolysis of the host cell unless mass rupture occurs in ischemic necrosis.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nPeroxisomes contain CATALASE to degrade hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) and oxidize fatty acids. Lysosomes contain ACID HYDROLASES (marker: acid phosphatase). Lipofuscin = wear-and-tear aging pigment derived from lysosomal breakdown. Golgi = post-translational protein modification & packaging.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Golgi = Post office / Packaging center!\n* Lysosomes = Trash can / Digestor (Acid hydrolases, pH 4.5-5.0)!\n* Peroxisomes = CATALASE (breaks down H2O2) + oxidizes long fatty acids!\n* Lipofuscin = Wear-and-tear pigment in old heart & brain cells!",
    "summary": "Cellular Organelles II - Processing, Degradation & Oxidation:\n- Golgi Apparatus:\n  * Cisternae stacks with polarized faces: Cis-face (entry face receiving transport vesicles from RER) and Trans-face (exit/maturation face releasing secretory granules or lysosomes).\n  * Functions: Post-translational modification of proteins (O-linked glycosylation, sulfation, phosphorylation of mannose residues); sorting, packaging, and shipping proteins to lysosomes, secretory vesicles, or plasma membrane.\n- Lysosomes:\n  * Spherical membrane-bound organelles containing >40 ACID HYDROLASES (acid phosphatase, proteases, lipases, nucleases) with optimal activity at acidic pH (4.5 - 5.0 maintained by membrane H+ ATPase pump).\n  * Functions:\n    * Heterophagy: Degradation of extracellular material engulfed by phagocytosis or pinocytosis.\n    * Autophagy: Degradation of redundant, damaged, or aged cellular organelles (e.g., turning over old mitochondria).\n    * Crinophagy: Disposal of excess secretory granules.\n  * Lysosomal Storage Diseases: Genetic enzyme deficiencies causing toxic metabolite accumulation (e.g., Tay-Sachs disease [hexosaminidase A deficiency], Gaucher disease [glucocerebrosidase deficiency]).\n  * Residual Bodies / Lipofuscin: Indigestible lipid residues accumulating in aging non-dividing cells (cardiac myocytes, neurons; known as 'wear-and-tear' or aging pigment).\n- Peroxisomes (Microbodies):\n  * Membrane-bound organelles containing OXIDATIVE ENZYMES: Urates oxidase, D-amino acid oxidase, and abundant CATALASE.\n  * Functions: Beta-oxidation of very long-chain fatty acids (VLCFA); Synthesis of plasmalogens (phospholipids in myelin); Catalase breaks down toxic Hydrogen Peroxide (H2O2) into H2O and O2; Drug and alcohol detoxification in liver.\n  * Zellweger syndrome: Peroxisomal biogenesis defect causing fatal VLCFA accumulation.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nLysosomes protect the host through intracellular digestion, but their hydrolytic contents are strictly isolated behind a membrane with an acidic luminal microenvironment. If a lysosome ruptures, the neutral cytoplasmic pH (7.2) immediately inactivates acid hydrolases, preventing accidental autolysis of the host cell unless mass rupture occurs in ischemic necrosis.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nPeroxisomes contain CATALASE to degrade hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) and oxidize fatty acids. Lysosomes contain ACID HYDROLASES (marker: acid phosphatase). Lipofuscin = wear-and-tear aging pigment derived from lysosomal breakdown. Golgi = post-translational protein modification & packaging.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Golgi = Post office / Packaging center!\n* Lysosomes = Trash can / Digestor (Acid hydrolases, pH 4.5-5.0)!\n* Peroxisomes = CATALASE (breaks down H2O2) + oxidizes long fatty acids!\n* Lipofuscin = Wear-and-tear pigment in old heart & brain cells!",
