    "originalPrint": "Mitochondria & Cytoskeletal Filaments:\n- Mitochondria ('Powerhouse of the Cell'):\n  * Double-membrane organelle: Smooth outer membrane; Inner membrane folded into CRISTAE to increase surface area for the Electron Transport Chain (ETC) and ATP synthase complexes; Intermembrane space; Matrix containing circular mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA), ribosomes, and Krebs (TCA) cycle enzymes.\n  * Functions: Aerobic cellular respiration producing 30-32 ATP per glucose; Beta-oxidation of fatty acids; Regulation of APOPTOSIS (release of Cytochrome c into cytoplasm activates Apaf-1 and caspase-9/caspase-3 cascade).\n  * Genetics: Exclusively MATERNAL INHERITANCE (sperm mitochondria in tail are destroyed upon fertilization).\n- The Cytoskeleton:\n  1. Microfilaments (Actin Filaments - ~7 nm diameter):\n     * Composed of G-actin polymerizing into F-actin.\n     * Functions: Cellular motility, cytokinesis (cleavage furrow during mitosis), muscle contraction (with myosin), microvilli core support, cell cortex anchoring.\n  2. Intermediate Filaments (~10 nm diameter - High Tensile Strength):\n     * Tonofilaments / Keratin (cytokeratins in epithelial cells; forms desmosomes/hemidesmosomes).\n     * Vimentin (mesenchymal cells, fibroblasts, endothelial cells).\n     * Desmin (muscle cells: skeletal, cardiac, smooth).\n     * Glial Fibrillary Acidic Protein (GFAP - astrocytes).\n     * Neurofilaments (axons and dendrites of neurons).\n     * Nuclear Lamins (inner nuclear lamina support).\n  3. Microtubules (~25 nm diameter - Hollow cylinders composed of alpha and beta tubulin dimers):\n     * Polarized (+ and - ends); Form mitotic spindle fibers during cell division; Centrosome / Centriole (9 triplets of microtubules: 9+0 pattern).\n     * Cilia & Flagella: Axoneme arrangement of 9 doublets + 2 central microtubules (9+2 pattern) with Dynein motor arms.\n     * Molecular Motors: Kinesin (anterograde transport toward + end / cell periphery); Dynein (retrograde transport toward - end / nucleus).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe cytoskeleton provides both spatial organization and mechanical durability. In oral pathology, immunohistochemistry utilizes intermediate filament typing to determine the embryonic origin of tumors: Carcinomas express Cytokeratin; Sarcomas express Vimentin; Rhabdomyosarcomas express Desmin; Astrocytomas express GFAP. Mitochondria are central arbiters of intrinsic apoptosis; Bcl-2 prevents cytochrome c leakage, while Bax/Bak promotes it.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nMitochondrial DNA is MATERNALLY inherited! Cytochrome c release from mitochondria triggers apoptosis. Cilia/Flagella = 9+2 microtubule doublet pattern with Dynein arms. Kinesin = Anterograde transport; Dynein = Retrograde transport. Keratin = Epithelial intermediate filament.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Mitochondria = MATERNAL inheritance only! Releases Cytochrome C for apoptosis.\n* Cilia = 9+2 arrangement (Dynein motor arms)!\n* Kinesin moves OUT (Anterograde); Dynein moves IN (Retrograde)!\n* Immunohistochemistry markers: Keratin = Epithelium, Vimentin = Connective tissue/Mesenchyme!",
    "summary": "Mitochondria & Cytoskeletal Filaments:\n- Mitochondria ('Powerhouse of the Cell'):\n  * Double-membrane organelle: Smooth outer membrane; Inner membrane folded into CRISTAE to increase surface area for the Electron Transport Chain (ETC) and ATP synthase complexes; Intermembrane space; Matrix containing circular mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA), ribosomes, and Krebs (TCA) cycle enzymes.\n  * Functions: Aerobic cellular respiration producing 30-32 ATP per glucose; Beta-oxidation of fatty acids; Regulation of APOPTOSIS (release of Cytochrome c into cytoplasm activates Apaf-1 and caspase-9/caspase-3 cascade).\n  * Genetics: Exclusively MATERNAL INHERITANCE (sperm mitochondria in tail are destroyed upon fertilization).\n- The Cytoskeleton:\n  1. Microfilaments (Actin Filaments - ~7 nm diameter):\n     * Composed of G-actin polymerizing into F-actin.\n     * Functions: Cellular motility, cytokinesis (cleavage furrow during mitosis), muscle contraction (with myosin), microvilli core support, cell cortex anchoring.\n  2. Intermediate Filaments (~10 nm diameter - High Tensile Strength):\n     * Tonofilaments / Keratin (cytokeratins in epithelial cells; forms desmosomes/hemidesmosomes).\n     * Vimentin (mesenchymal cells, fibroblasts, endothelial cells).\n     * Desmin (muscle cells: skeletal, cardiac, smooth).\n     * Glial Fibrillary Acidic Protein (GFAP - astrocytes).\n     * Neurofilaments (axons and dendrites of neurons).\n     * Nuclear Lamins (inner nuclear lamina support).\n  3. Microtubules (~25 nm diameter - Hollow cylinders composed of alpha and beta tubulin dimers):\n     * Polarized (+ and - ends); Form mitotic spindle fibers during cell division; Centrosome / Centriole (9 triplets of microtubules: 9+0 pattern).\n     * Cilia & Flagella: Axoneme arrangement of 9 doublets + 2 central microtubules (9+2 pattern) with Dynein motor arms.\n     * Molecular Motors: Kinesin (anterograde transport toward + end / cell periphery); Dynein (retrograde transport toward - end / nucleus).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe cytoskeleton provides both spatial organization and mechanical durability. In oral pathology, immunohistochemistry utilizes intermediate filament typing to determine the embryonic origin of tumors: Carcinomas express Cytokeratin; Sarcomas express Vimentin; Rhabdomyosarcomas express Desmin; Astrocytomas express GFAP. Mitochondria are central arbiters of intrinsic apoptosis; Bcl-2 prevents cytochrome c leakage, while Bax/Bak promotes it.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nMitochondrial DNA is MATERNALLY inherited! Cytochrome c release from mitochondria triggers apoptosis. Cilia/Flagella = 9+2 microtubule doublet pattern with Dynein arms. Kinesin = Anterograde transport; Dynein = Retrograde transport. Keratin = Epithelial intermediate filament.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Mitochondria = MATERNAL inheritance only! Releases Cytochrome C for apoptosis.\n* Cilia = 9+2 arrangement (Dynein motor arms)!\n* Kinesin moves OUT (Anterograde); Dynein moves IN (Retrograde)!\n* Immunohistochemistry markers: Keratin = Epithelium, Vimentin = Connective tissue/Mesenchyme!",
