    "originalPrint": "The Eukaryotic Cell Cycle & Mitosis:\n- Phases of the Cell Cycle:\n  1. INTERPHASE (90% of cycle duration):\n     * G1 Phase (Gap 1): Cell growth, protein/RNA synthesis, organelle duplication.\n     * S Phase (Synthesis): Exact REPLICATION of nuclear DNA and duplication of centrosomes (chromosome number goes from 2N to 4N DNA content).\n     * G2 Phase (Gap 2): Synthesis of proteins needed for mitosis (tubulin for mitotic spindle), energy storage, final proofreading.\n     * G0 Phase: Quiescent, non-dividing state (Permanent cells: neurons, cardiac myocytes; Stable cells: hepatocytes, periosteal cells; Labile cells: oral mucosal epithelium, bone marrow stem cells).\n- Cell Cycle Checkpoints & Regulators:\n  * Cyclins and Cyclin-Dependent Kinases (CDKs): Regulate progression through checkpoints by phosphorylating target proteins.\n  * G1/S Checkpoint (Restriction Point): Most critical checkpoint; assessed by p53 (tumor suppressor 'guardian of the genome') and Retinoblastoma protein (Rb). If DNA damage is detected, p53 halts cycle via p21 (CDK inhibitor) to allow repair; if repair fails, p53 induces apoptosis via Bax.\n  * G2/M Checkpoint: Verifies accurate DNA replication before entering mitosis.\n  * Spindle Assembly Checkpoint (M Checkpoint): Ensures all chromosomes are properly attached to spindle microtubules at the metaphase plate.\n- Stages of Mitosis (Karyokinesis):\n  1. Prophase: Chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes; centrosomes migrate to opposite poles; mitotic spindle begins to form; nucleolus disappears.\n  2. Prometaphase: Nuclear envelope breaks down; kinetochores form at centromeres and attach to spindle microtubules.\n  3. Metaphase: Chromosomes align along equatorial plane (Metaphase Plate / equatorial plate).\n  4. Anaphase: Sister chromatids separate at centromere and are pulled toward opposite spindle poles by shortening kinetochore microtubules.\n  5. Telophase: Chromatids arrive at poles; nuclear envelope reassembles around each daughter nucleus; chromosomes decondense back into chromatin; nucleoli reappear.\n- Cytokinesis: Division of cytoplasm via contractile ring of actin and myosin creating a cleavage furrow.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nCell cycle control is the primary defense against malignant transformation. Loss of heterozygosity or mutation in TP53 or RB1 removes the G1/S restriction point checkpoint, allowing cells with damaged DNA to replicate uncontrollably\u2014a universal hallmark of oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC). Chemotherapeutic agents exploit cell cycle kinetics: Antimetabolites (Methotrexate) target S phase; Vinca alkaloids (Vincristine) target M phase by disrupting mitotic spindle microtubules.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nDNA replication occurs exclusively during S PHASE of interphase! Chromosomes align at equatorial plate during METAPHASE. Sister chromatids separate during ANAPHASE. p53 is the 'Guardian of the Genome' acting at G1/S checkpoint.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* S Phase = SYNTHESIS of DNA (replication)!\n* Stages of Mitosis: PMAT (Prophase, Metaphase, Anaphase, Telophase)!\n* Metaphase = Middle alignment!\n* Anaphase = Apart / Away (chromatids separate)!\n* p53 = Guardian of the genome (G1/S checkpoint)!",
    "summary": "The Eukaryotic Cell Cycle & Mitosis:\n- Phases of the Cell Cycle:\n  1. INTERPHASE (90% of cycle duration):\n     * G1 Phase (Gap 1): Cell growth, protein/RNA synthesis, organelle duplication.\n     * S Phase (Synthesis): Exact REPLICATION of nuclear DNA and duplication of centrosomes (chromosome number goes from 2N to 4N DNA content).\n     * G2 Phase (Gap 2): Synthesis of proteins needed for mitosis (tubulin for mitotic spindle), energy storage, final proofreading.\n     * G0 Phase: Quiescent, non-dividing state (Permanent cells: neurons, cardiac myocytes; Stable cells: hepatocytes, periosteal cells; Labile cells: oral mucosal epithelium, bone marrow stem cells).\n- Cell Cycle Checkpoints & Regulators:\n  * Cyclins and Cyclin-Dependent Kinases (CDKs): Regulate progression through checkpoints by phosphorylating target proteins.\n  * G1/S Checkpoint (Restriction Point): Most critical checkpoint; assessed by p53 (tumor suppressor 'guardian of the genome') and Retinoblastoma protein (Rb). If DNA damage is detected, p53 halts cycle via p21 (CDK inhibitor) to allow repair; if repair fails, p53 induces apoptosis via Bax.\n  * G2/M Checkpoint: Verifies accurate DNA replication before entering mitosis.\n  * Spindle Assembly Checkpoint (M Checkpoint): Ensures all chromosomes are properly attached to spindle microtubules at the metaphase plate.\n- Stages of Mitosis (Karyokinesis):\n  1. Prophase: Chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes; centrosomes migrate to opposite poles; mitotic spindle begins to form; nucleolus disappears.\n  2. Prometaphase: Nuclear envelope breaks down; kinetochores form at centromeres and attach to spindle microtubules.\n  3. Metaphase: Chromosomes align along equatorial plane (Metaphase Plate / equatorial plate).\n  4. Anaphase: Sister chromatids separate at centromere and are pulled toward opposite spindle poles by shortening kinetochore microtubules.\n  5. Telophase: Chromatids arrive at poles; nuclear envelope reassembles around each daughter nucleus; chromosomes decondense back into chromatin; nucleoli reappear.\n- Cytokinesis: Division of cytoplasm via contractile ring of actin and myosin creating a cleavage furrow.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nCell cycle control is the primary defense against malignant transformation. Loss of heterozygosity or mutation in TP53 or RB1 removes the G1/S restriction point checkpoint, allowing cells with damaged DNA to replicate uncontrollably\u2014a universal hallmark of oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC). Chemotherapeutic agents exploit cell cycle kinetics: Antimetabolites (Methotrexate) target S phase; Vinca alkaloids (Vincristine) target M phase by disrupting mitotic spindle microtubules.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nDNA replication occurs exclusively during S PHASE of interphase! Chromosomes align at equatorial plate during METAPHASE. Sister chromatids separate during ANAPHASE. p53 is the 'Guardian of the Genome' acting at G1/S checkpoint.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* S Phase = SYNTHESIS of DNA (replication)!\n* Stages of Mitosis: PMAT (Prophase, Metaphase, Anaphase, Telophase)!\n* Metaphase = Middle alignment!\n* Anaphase = Apart / Away (chromatids separate)!\n* p53 = Guardian of the genome (G1/S checkpoint)!",
