    "originalPrint": "Epithelial Tissues: Characteristics & Simple Epithelia:\n- General Characteristics of Epithelium:\n  1. High Cellularity: Densely packed cells with minimal extracellular matrix (scant intercellular cement).\n  2. Polarity: Distinct apical surface (faces lumen or external environment), lateral surfaces (intercellular junctions), and basal surface (rests on basement membrane).\n  3. Avascularity: Lacks blood vessels; receives oxygen and nutrients entirely by diffusion from underlying vascularized connective tissue (lamina propria / dermis).\n  4. Basement Membrane: Anchoring extracellular sheet composed of Basal Lamina (Type IV collagen, laminin, perlecan - produced by epithelium) and Reticular Lamina (Type III collagen / reticular fibers - produced by fibroblasts).\n  5. High Regenerative Capacity: High mitotic rate via stem cells.\n- Classification of Epithelia by Cell Layers:\n  * Simple (Single cell layer resting on basement membrane) vs Stratified (Two or more cell layers; classified by shape of the outermost/apical layer).\n- Types of Simple Epithelium:\n  1. Simple Squamous Epithelium (Thin, flat, plate-like cells with flattened nuclei):\n     * Functions: Rapid passive diffusion, filtration, lubrication.\n     * Locations:\n       - Endothelium: Lines lumen of all blood and lymphatic vessels and heart chambers.\n       - Mesothelium: Lines serous body cavities (pleura, peritoneum, pericardium).\n       - Bowman's capsule (parietal layer) in renal corpuscles and Alveoli of lungs (Type I pneumocytes).\n  2. Simple Cuboidal Epithelium (Box-shaped cells, equal height and width, central spherical nucleus):\n     * Functions: Secretion and absorption.\n     * Locations: Renal tubules (proximal and distal convoluted tubules), Thyroid follicles, Surface of ovary (germinal epithelium), Intercalated ducts of salivary glands.\n  3. Simple Columnar Epithelium (Rectangular cells, taller than wide, oval nuclei located in basal third):\n     * Functions: Active absorption and high secretion; may possess apical modifications (microvilli or cilia).\n     * Locations: Non-ciliated lines gastrointestinal tract (stomach, small intestine, large intestine, gall bladder); Ciliated lines fallopian tubes (oviducts) and uterus.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nEpithelial structure precisely matches physiological function. Simple squamous epithelium maximizes diffusion velocity across alveoli and capillaries. Simple cuboidal cells in salivary intercalated ducts actively exchange electrolytes. Simple columnar cells in the intestines maximize absorptive surface area through apical brush border microvilli and secrete protective mucus through interspersed goblet cells.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nEndothelium (blood vessels) and Mesothelium (body cavities) = SIMPLE SQUAMOUS epithelium! Salivary intercalated ducts & renal tubules = SIMPLE CUBOIDAL. GI tract lining (stomach to colon) = SIMPLE COLUMNAR with microvilli. Basement membrane contains Type IV collagen and laminin.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Epithelium is AVASCULAR (no blood vessels, feeds by diffusion)!\n* Blood vessels lining = ENDOTHELIUM = Simple Squamous!\n* Serous cavities = MESOTHELIUM = Simple Squamous!\n* GI tract (Stomach to Colon) = Simple Columnar!\n* Salivary intercalated ducts = Simple Cuboidal!",
    "summary": "Epithelial Tissues: Characteristics & Simple Epithelia:\n- General Characteristics of Epithelium:\n  1. High Cellularity: Densely packed cells with minimal extracellular matrix (scant intercellular cement).\n  2. Polarity: Distinct apical surface (faces lumen or external environment), lateral surfaces (intercellular junctions), and basal surface (rests on basement membrane).\n  3. Avascularity: Lacks blood vessels; receives oxygen and nutrients entirely by diffusion from underlying vascularized connective tissue (lamina propria / dermis).\n  4. Basement Membrane: Anchoring extracellular sheet composed of Basal Lamina (Type IV collagen, laminin, perlecan - produced by epithelium) and Reticular Lamina (Type III collagen / reticular fibers - produced by fibroblasts).\n  5. High Regenerative Capacity: High mitotic rate via stem cells.\n- Classification of Epithelia by Cell Layers:\n  * Simple (Single cell layer resting on basement membrane) vs Stratified (Two or more cell layers; classified by shape of the outermost/apical layer).\n- Types of Simple Epithelium:\n  1. Simple Squamous Epithelium (Thin, flat, plate-like cells with flattened nuclei):\n     * Functions: Rapid passive diffusion, filtration, lubrication.\n     * Locations:\n       - Endothelium: Lines lumen of all blood and lymphatic vessels and heart chambers.\n       - Mesothelium: Lines serous body cavities (pleura, peritoneum, pericardium).\n       - Bowman's capsule (parietal layer) in renal corpuscles and Alveoli of lungs (Type I pneumocytes).\n  2. Simple Cuboidal Epithelium (Box-shaped cells, equal height and width, central spherical nucleus):\n     * Functions: Secretion and absorption.\n     * Locations: Renal tubules (proximal and distal convoluted tubules), Thyroid follicles, Surface of ovary (germinal epithelium), Intercalated ducts of salivary glands.\n  3. Simple Columnar Epithelium (Rectangular cells, taller than wide, oval nuclei located in basal third):\n     * Functions: Active absorption and high secretion; may possess apical modifications (microvilli or cilia).\n     * Locations: Non-ciliated lines gastrointestinal tract (stomach, small intestine, large intestine, gall bladder); Ciliated lines fallopian tubes (oviducts) and uterus.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nEpithelial structure precisely matches physiological function. Simple squamous epithelium maximizes diffusion velocity across alveoli and capillaries. Simple cuboidal cells in salivary intercalated ducts actively exchange electrolytes. Simple columnar cells in the intestines maximize absorptive surface area through apical brush border microvilli and secrete protective mucus through interspersed goblet cells.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nEndothelium (blood vessels) and Mesothelium (body cavities) = SIMPLE SQUAMOUS epithelium! Salivary intercalated ducts & renal tubules = SIMPLE CUBOIDAL. GI tract lining (stomach to colon) = SIMPLE COLUMNAR with microvilli. Basement membrane contains Type IV collagen and laminin.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Epithelium is AVASCULAR (no blood vessels, feeds by diffusion)!\n* Blood vessels lining = ENDOTHELIUM = Simple Squamous!\n* Serous cavities = MESOTHELIUM = Simple Squamous!\n* GI tract (Stomach to Colon) = Simple Columnar!\n* Salivary intercalated ducts = Simple Cuboidal!",
