    "originalPrint": "Stratified & Specialized Epithelia:\n- Stratified Squamous Epithelium (Designed to withstand mechanical friction and abrasion):\n  1. Stratified Squamous Non-Keratinized Epithelium:\n     * Surface cells remain nucleated, viable, and moist.\n     * Locations: Oral lining mucosa (buccal mucosa, labial mucosa, ventral surface of tongue, floor of mouth, soft palate), Esophagus, Vagina, Cornea, Anal canal.\n  2. Stratified Squamous Keratinized / Parakeratinized Epithelium:\n     * Surface cells lose nuclei and organelles, replaced by tough, water-impermeable KERATIN filaments (orthokeratinized = enucleated; parakeratinized = retained pyknotic nuclei).\n     * Locations: Epidermis of skin (orthokeratinized), Oral masticatory mucosa (hard palate and attached gingiva - predominantly parakeratinized).\n- Stratified Cuboidal & Stratified Columnar Epithelium:\n  * Rare; 2-3 layers of cells.\n  * Locations: Large excretory ducts of major salivary glands (Stensen's, Wharton's), ducts of sweat glands, male urethra.\n- Specialized Epithelia:\n  1. Pseudostratified Ciliated Columnar Epithelium ('Respiratory Epithelium'):\n     * All cells contact the basement membrane, but nuclei lie at varying levels, giving the false illusion of stratification; not all cells reach the apical lumen.\n     * Equipped with apical CILIA and interspersed mucus-secreting GOBLET CELLS.\n     * Locations: Nasal cavity, nasopharynx, trachea, primary and secondary bronchi, maxillary sinus lining (Schneiderian membrane).\n  2. Transitional Epithelium (Urothelium):\n     * Stratified epithelium capable of extreme distension and volume fluctuation without cellular detachment.\n     * Outermost layer consists of large, dome-shaped, bi-nucleated 'UMBRELLA CELLS' with specialized superficial plaques (uroplakins).\n     * Locations: Renal calyces, renal pelvis, ureters, urinary bladder, prostatic urethra.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nIn the oral cavity, mucosal architecture reflects functional biomechanics. Masticatory mucosa (gingiva and hard palate) undergoes severe shear forces during bolus mastication, requiring keratinized stratified squamous epithelium firmly bound to underlying periosteum (mucoperiosteum). In contrast, lining mucosa (buccal, alveolar) must stretch freely during phonation and swallowing, requiring non-keratinized epithelium with an elastic submucosa.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nRespiratory epithelium = PSEUDOSTRATIFIED CILIATED COLUMNAR with goblet cells (Schneiderian membrane of maxillary sinus)! Urothelium = TRANSITIONAL epithelium with UMBRELLA CELLS (urinary bladder). Masticatory oral mucosa = Keratinized/Parakeratinized; Lining mucosa = Non-keratinized.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Respiratory epithelium = Pseudostratified Ciliated Columnar + Goblet cells!\n* Maxillary sinus lining = Schneiderian membrane = Respiratory epithelium!\n* Urinary bladder = Transitional / Urothelium (Umbrella cells)!\n* Oral lining (cheeks, lips) = Non-keratinized; Hard palate & gingiva = Keratinized!",
    "summary": "Stratified & Specialized Epithelia:\n- Stratified Squamous Epithelium (Designed to withstand mechanical friction and abrasion):\n  1. Stratified Squamous Non-Keratinized Epithelium:\n     * Surface cells remain nucleated, viable, and moist.\n     * Locations: Oral lining mucosa (buccal mucosa, labial mucosa, ventral surface of tongue, floor of mouth, soft palate), Esophagus, Vagina, Cornea, Anal canal.\n  2. Stratified Squamous Keratinized / Parakeratinized Epithelium:\n     * Surface cells lose nuclei and organelles, replaced by tough, water-impermeable KERATIN filaments (orthokeratinized = enucleated; parakeratinized = retained pyknotic nuclei).\n     * Locations: Epidermis of skin (orthokeratinized), Oral masticatory mucosa (hard palate and attached gingiva - predominantly parakeratinized).\n- Stratified Cuboidal & Stratified Columnar Epithelium:\n  * Rare; 2-3 layers of cells.\n  * Locations: Large excretory ducts of major salivary glands (Stensen's, Wharton's), ducts of sweat glands, male urethra.\n- Specialized Epithelia:\n  1. Pseudostratified Ciliated Columnar Epithelium ('Respiratory Epithelium'):\n     * All cells contact the basement membrane, but nuclei lie at varying levels, giving the false illusion of stratification; not all cells reach the apical lumen.\n     * Equipped with apical CILIA and interspersed mucus-secreting GOBLET CELLS.\n     * Locations: Nasal cavity, nasopharynx, trachea, primary and secondary bronchi, maxillary sinus lining (Schneiderian membrane).\n  2. Transitional Epithelium (Urothelium):\n     * Stratified epithelium capable of extreme distension and volume fluctuation without cellular detachment.\n     * Outermost layer consists of large, dome-shaped, bi-nucleated 'UMBRELLA CELLS' with specialized superficial plaques (uroplakins).\n     * Locations: Renal calyces, renal pelvis, ureters, urinary bladder, prostatic urethra.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nIn the oral cavity, mucosal architecture reflects functional biomechanics. Masticatory mucosa (gingiva and hard palate) undergoes severe shear forces during bolus mastication, requiring keratinized stratified squamous epithelium firmly bound to underlying periosteum (mucoperiosteum). In contrast, lining mucosa (buccal, alveolar) must stretch freely during phonation and swallowing, requiring non-keratinized epithelium with an elastic submucosa.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nRespiratory epithelium = PSEUDOSTRATIFIED CILIATED COLUMNAR with goblet cells (Schneiderian membrane of maxillary sinus)! Urothelium = TRANSITIONAL epithelium with UMBRELLA CELLS (urinary bladder). Masticatory oral mucosa = Keratinized/Parakeratinized; Lining mucosa = Non-keratinized.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Respiratory epithelium = Pseudostratified Ciliated Columnar + Goblet cells!\n* Maxillary sinus lining = Schneiderian membrane = Respiratory epithelium!\n* Urinary bladder = Transitional / Urothelium (Umbrella cells)!\n* Oral lining (cheeks, lips) = Non-keratinized; Hard palate & gingiva = Keratinized!",
