    "originalPrint": "Glandular Epithelium: Classification & Secretory Mechanisms:\n- Overview of Glands:\n  * Originate from invagination of surface epithelium into underlying connective tissue.\n  * Endocrine Glands: Ductless glands; secrete chemical hormones directly into blood or lymph capillaries (pituitary, thyroid, adrenal, parathyroid, islets of Langerhans).\n  * Exocrine Glands: Retain connection to surface via excretory DUCTS that deliver secretions to apical surface or mucosal lumen (salivary glands, sweat glands, pancreas).\n- Classification of Exocrine Glands by Secretory Mechanism:\n  1. MEROCRINE (ECCRINE) Secretion:\n     * Secretory product is packaged into membrane-bound vesicles and discharged via EXOCYTOSIS at apical membrane.\n     * NO loss of cellular cytoplasm or cellular integrity.\n     * Examples: Major and minor SALIVARY GLANDS, Exocrine Pancreas, Eccrine sweat glands.\n  2. APOCRINE Secretion:\n     * Secretory product accumulates at the apical pole of the cell; the APICAL PORTION OF THE CELL PINCHES OFF and is released along with the secretion; cell repairs itself.\n     * Examples: Lactating Mammary glands (lipid droplet secretion), Apocrine sweat glands in axilla/groin, Ceruminous glands of external ear canal, Glands of Moll in eyelids.\n  3. HOLOCRINE Secretion:\n     * The ENTIRE CELL fills with secretory product, disintegrates, and dies; the dead cell and its accumulated contents constitute the secretion.\n     * Requires continuous cell renewal from basal stem cells.\n     * Examples: SEBACEOUS GLANDS of skin, Meibomian (tarsal) glands of eyelids.\n- Structural Classification of Exocrine Glands:\n  * Simple (single unbranched duct: simple tubular, simple acinar) vs Compound (branched duct system: compound acinar, compound tubuloacinar, compound tubular).\n  * Major Salivary Glands: Compound Tubuloacinar (Compound Tubuloalveolar) glands.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe mode of secretion determines the cellular survival dynamic. Merocrine secretion allows continuous, rapid protein production with zero cell loss (ideal for saliva). Holocrine secretion requires high cell turnover because every secretory event destroys the glandular epithelial cell. Sebaceous glands (holocrine) on the vermilion border of the lips or buccal mucosa are known as Fordyce Granules (ectopic sebaceous glands).",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nSecretory Mechanisms:\n1. Merocrine (Eccrine) = Exocytosis with NO cell damage (Salivary glands & Pancreas)!\n2. Apocrine = Apical cytoplasm pinches off (Mammary glands)!\n3. Holocrine = Entire cell dies and disintegrates (Sebaceous glands & Meibomian glands)!\nSalivary glands are Compound Tubuloacinar Merocrine glands.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* MEROCRINE = Exocytosis, zero cell loss (Salivary glands)!\n* APOCRINE = Apical pinch off (Mammary glands)!\n* HOLOCRINE = WHOLE cell destroyed/sacrificed (Sebaceous glands)!\n* Fordyce spots = Ectopic sebaceous glands in mouth!",
    "summary": "Glandular Epithelium: Classification & Secretory Mechanisms:\n- Overview of Glands:\n  * Originate from invagination of surface epithelium into underlying connective tissue.\n  * Endocrine Glands: Ductless glands; secrete chemical hormones directly into blood or lymph capillaries (pituitary, thyroid, adrenal, parathyroid, islets of Langerhans).\n  * Exocrine Glands: Retain connection to surface via excretory DUCTS that deliver secretions to apical surface or mucosal lumen (salivary glands, sweat glands, pancreas).\n- Classification of Exocrine Glands by Secretory Mechanism:\n  1. MEROCRINE (ECCRINE) Secretion:\n     * Secretory product is packaged into membrane-bound vesicles and discharged via EXOCYTOSIS at apical membrane.\n     * NO loss of cellular cytoplasm or cellular integrity.\n     * Examples: Major and minor SALIVARY GLANDS, Exocrine Pancreas, Eccrine sweat glands.\n  2. APOCRINE Secretion:\n     * Secretory product accumulates at the apical pole of the cell; the APICAL PORTION OF THE CELL PINCHES OFF and is released along with the secretion; cell repairs itself.\n     * Examples: Lactating Mammary glands (lipid droplet secretion), Apocrine sweat glands in axilla/groin, Ceruminous glands of external ear canal, Glands of Moll in eyelids.\n  3. HOLOCRINE Secretion:\n     * The ENTIRE CELL fills with secretory product, disintegrates, and dies; the dead cell and its accumulated contents constitute the secretion.\n     * Requires continuous cell renewal from basal stem cells.\n     * Examples: SEBACEOUS GLANDS of skin, Meibomian (tarsal) glands of eyelids.\n- Structural Classification of Exocrine Glands:\n  * Simple (single unbranched duct: simple tubular, simple acinar) vs Compound (branched duct system: compound acinar, compound tubuloacinar, compound tubular).\n  * Major Salivary Glands: Compound Tubuloacinar (Compound Tubuloalveolar) glands.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe mode of secretion determines the cellular survival dynamic. Merocrine secretion allows continuous, rapid protein production with zero cell loss (ideal for saliva). Holocrine secretion requires high cell turnover because every secretory event destroys the glandular epithelial cell. Sebaceous glands (holocrine) on the vermilion border of the lips or buccal mucosa are known as Fordyce Granules (ectopic sebaceous glands).\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nSecretory Mechanisms:\n1. Merocrine (Eccrine) = Exocytosis with NO cell damage (Salivary glands & Pancreas)!\n2. Apocrine = Apical cytoplasm pinches off (Mammary glands)!\n3. Holocrine = Entire cell dies and disintegrates (Sebaceous glands & Meibomian glands)!\nSalivary glands are Compound Tubuloacinar Merocrine glands.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* MEROCRINE = Exocytosis, zero cell loss (Salivary glands)!\n* APOCRINE = Apical pinch off (Mammary glands)!\n* HOLOCRINE = WHOLE cell destroyed/sacrificed (Sebaceous glands)!\n* Fordyce spots = Ectopic sebaceous glands in mouth!",
