    "originalPrint": "Cells of the Connective Tissue:\n- Resident (Fixed) Cells:\n  1. Fibroblasts:\n     * Most abundant cell type in connective tissue; spindle-shaped with tapering cytoplasmic processes and elongated, oval nucleus.\n     * Function: Synthesizes almost all ECM components (tropocollagen, elastic fibers, reticular fibers, and ground substance GAGs).\n     * Active Fibroblasts (plump, basophilic cytoplasm, active protein synthesis) vs Inactive Fibrocytes (smaller, spindle-shaped, dark condensed nucleus).\n     * Myofibroblasts: Contain actin and non-muscle myosin; mediate wound contraction in granulation tissue.\n  2. Adipocytes (Fat Cells):\n     * White (Unilocular) Adipocytes: Single large lipid droplet displacing nucleus and cytoplasm to periphery ('signet-ring cell'); energy storage, thermal insulation, endocrine secretion (leptin).\n     * Brown (Multilocular) Adipocytes: Multiple small lipid droplets, abundant mitochondria, central nucleus; specialized for non-shivering thermogenesis via Thermogenin (UCP-1); abundant in newborns and hibernating mammals.\n  3. Mast Cells:\n     * Bone marrow origin; densely packed with METACHROMATIC secretory granules (stain purple with toluidine blue).\n     * Granule Contents: HISTAMINE (increases vascular permeability, vasodilation), HEPARIN (anticoagulant), Eosinophil Chemotactic Factor (ECF-A), Leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4).\n     * Mechanism: Express high-affinity Fc receptors for IgE; cross-linking of surface IgE by allergen triggers degranulation -> Type I Hypersensitivity (Anaphylaxis, allergic rhinitis, asthma).\n- Wandering (Transient / Immune) Cells:\n  1. Macrophages (Histiocytes):\n     * Derived from circulating blood monocytes; irregular borders with pseudopodia, kidney-shaped eccentric nucleus; abundant lysosomes.\n     * Function: Phagocytosis of debris, foreign bacteria, and apoptotic cells; Antigen-Presenting Cell (APC) expressing MHC Class II; secretes cytokines (IL-1, TNF-alpha).\n     * Mononuclear Phagocyte System: Kupffer cells (liver), Microglia (CNS), Osteoclasts (bone), Alveolar macrophages / dust cells (lungs), Langerhans cells (epidermis).\n  2. Plasma Cells:\n     * Differentiated from activated B lymphocytes; oval cell with eccentric spherical nucleus displaying 'CLOCK-FACE' or 'CARTWHEEL' chromatin pattern; intense basophilic cytoplasm with pale perinuclear halo (Golgi complex).\n     * Function: Synthesis and secretion of specific Immunoglobulins (Antibodies).\n     * Russell Bodies: Eosinophilic inclusions of accumulated immunoglobulins in overactive or degenerating plasma cells.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nPlasma cells are cellular protein factories. Their intense basophilia reflects immense concentrations of rough endoplasmic reticulum needed to produce thousands of antibody molecules per second. The pale perinuclear Golgi halo is where immunoglobulins are sorted and packaged. When overstressed, immunoglobulin aggregates crystallize inside cisternae as eosinophilic Russell bodies (characteristic of chronic periodontitis and multiple myeloma).",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nPlasma cells = CLOCK-FACE / CARTWHEEL chromatin, eccentric nucleus, perinuclear Golgi halo, and Russell bodies! Mast cells = METACHROMATIC granules containing Histamine & Heparin (Type I Hypersensitivity via IgE). Macrophages in bone = Osteoclasts; in liver = Kupffer cells; in CNS = Microglia.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Plasma Cells = CLOCK-FACE / CARTWHEEL nucleus! Produces antibodies! Russell bodies!\n* Mast Cells = IgE receptors + HISTAMINE & HEPARIN (Allergic reaction / Anaphylaxis)!\n* Macrophage names:\n  - Bone = Osteoclast\n  - Liver = Kupffer cell\n  - Brain = Microglia\n  - Skin = Langerhans cell!",
    "summary": "Cells of the Connective Tissue:\n- Resident (Fixed) Cells:\n  1. Fibroblasts:\n     * Most abundant cell type in connective tissue; spindle-shaped with tapering cytoplasmic processes and elongated, oval nucleus.\n     * Function: Synthesizes almost all ECM components (tropocollagen, elastic fibers, reticular fibers, and ground substance GAGs).\n     * Active Fibroblasts (plump, basophilic cytoplasm, active protein synthesis) vs Inactive Fibrocytes (smaller, spindle-shaped, dark condensed nucleus).\n     * Myofibroblasts: Contain actin and non-muscle myosin; mediate wound contraction in granulation tissue.\n  2. Adipocytes (Fat Cells):\n     * White (Unilocular) Adipocytes: Single large lipid droplet displacing nucleus and cytoplasm to periphery ('signet-ring cell'); energy storage, thermal insulation, endocrine secretion (leptin).\n     * Brown (Multilocular) Adipocytes: Multiple small lipid droplets, abundant mitochondria, central nucleus; specialized for non-shivering thermogenesis via Thermogenin (UCP-1); abundant in newborns and hibernating mammals.\n  3. Mast Cells:\n     * Bone marrow origin; densely packed with METACHROMATIC secretory granules (stain purple with toluidine blue).\n     * Granule Contents: HISTAMINE (increases vascular permeability, vasodilation), HEPARIN (anticoagulant), Eosinophil Chemotactic Factor (ECF-A), Leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4).\n     * Mechanism: Express high-affinity Fc receptors for IgE; cross-linking of surface IgE by allergen triggers degranulation -> Type I Hypersensitivity (Anaphylaxis, allergic rhinitis, asthma).\n- Wandering (Transient / Immune) Cells:\n  1. Macrophages (Histiocytes):\n     * Derived from circulating blood monocytes; irregular borders with pseudopodia, kidney-shaped eccentric nucleus; abundant lysosomes.\n     * Function: Phagocytosis of debris, foreign bacteria, and apoptotic cells; Antigen-Presenting Cell (APC) expressing MHC Class II; secretes cytokines (IL-1, TNF-alpha).\n     * Mononuclear Phagocyte System: Kupffer cells (liver), Microglia (CNS), Osteoclasts (bone), Alveolar macrophages / dust cells (lungs), Langerhans cells (epidermis).\n  2. Plasma Cells:\n     * Differentiated from activated B lymphocytes; oval cell with eccentric spherical nucleus displaying 'CLOCK-FACE' or 'CARTWHEEL' chromatin pattern; intense basophilic cytoplasm with pale perinuclear halo (Golgi complex).\n     * Function: Synthesis and secretion of specific Immunoglobulins (Antibodies).\n     * Russell Bodies: Eosinophilic inclusions of accumulated immunoglobulins in overactive or degenerating plasma cells.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nPlasma cells are cellular protein factories. Their intense basophilia reflects immense concentrations of rough endoplasmic reticulum needed to produce thousands of antibody molecules per second. The pale perinuclear Golgi halo is where immunoglobulins are sorted and packaged. When overstressed, immunoglobulin aggregates crystallize inside cisternae as eosinophilic Russell bodies (characteristic of chronic periodontitis and multiple myeloma).\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nPlasma cells = CLOCK-FACE / CARTWHEEL chromatin, eccentric nucleus, perinuclear Golgi halo, and Russell bodies! Mast cells = METACHROMATIC granules containing Histamine & Heparin (Type I Hypersensitivity via IgE). Macrophages in bone = Osteoclasts; in liver = Kupffer cells; in CNS = Microglia.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Plasma Cells = CLOCK-FACE / CARTWHEEL nucleus! Produces antibodies! Russell bodies!\n* Mast Cells = IgE receptors + HISTAMINE & HEPARIN (Allergic reaction / Anaphylaxis)!\n* Macrophage names:\n  - Bone = Osteoclast\n  - Liver = Kupffer cell\n  - Brain = Microglia\n  - Skin = Langerhans cell!",
