    "originalPrint": "Cartilage Histology: Types, Cells & Structure:\n- General Characteristics of Cartilage:\n  * Firm, resilient, highly specialized skeletal connective tissue.\n  * AVASCULAR, ANEURAL, and ALYMPHATIC: Receives all nutrition, oxygen, and metabolic exchange by diffusion through matrix from surrounding perichondrial blood vessels or synovial fluid.\n  * Chondrocytes reside within isolated cavities called LACUNAE; synthesize and maintain ECM.\n  * Perichondrium: Dense irregular connective tissue capsule covering cartilage; contains outer fibrous vascular layer and inner chondrogenic layer with chondroblasts.\n  * ABSENT PERICHONDRIUM in: 1) Articular cartilage of synovial joints, 2) Fibrocartilage, 3) Epiphyseal growth plates.\n- The Three Types of Cartilage:\n  1. HYALINE CARTILAGE (Most Abundant Cartilage):\n     * Glassy, semitransparent matrix rich in Type II collagen fibrils and Aggrecan proteoglycans.\n     * Chondrocytes often arranged in clusters of 2-8 cells derived from a single parent cell called ISOGENOUS GROUPS.\n     * Basophilic Territorial Matrix (surrounds lacunae, high GAG content) vs Interterritorial Matrix.\n     * Locations: Embryonic cartilaginous skeleton, Epiphyseal plates, Costal cartilages, Articular cartilage of synovial joints, Respiratory tract (nasal septum, larynx: thyroid, cricoid, arytenoid cartilages; trachea and bronchi).\n  2. ELASTIC CARTILAGE:\n     * Contains abundant network of branched ELASTIC FIBERS in addition to Type II collagen fibrils.\n     * Greater flexibility and elasticity; resilient recoil; does not calcify with age.\n     * Always covered by perichondrium.\n     * Locations: External ear (auricle/pinna), External auditory meatus, Eustachian (auditory) tube, Epiglottis, Cuneiform and Corniculate cartilages of larynx.\n  3. FIBROCARTILAGE:\n     * Combination of dense regular connective tissue and hyaline cartilage; possesses thick, parallel bundles of TYPE I COLLAGEN and alternating rows of chondrocytes in lacunae.\n     * LACKS A PERICHONDRIUM; exceptional tensile strength and shock absorption.\n     * Locations: Intervertebral discs (annulus fibrosus), Pubic symphysis, Glenoid and acetabular labrum, Articular disc of the Temporomandibular Joint (TMJ).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe TMJ articular disc is unique: unlike typical synovial joints covered by hyaline cartilage, the articulating surfaces of the mandibular condyle and the temporal glenoid fossa, as well as the TMJ articular disc itself, are lined by dense AVASCULAR FIBROCARTILAGE (Type I collagen). Fibrocartilage withstands heavy compression and shear stress far better than hyaline cartilage and possesses superior repair capacity.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nTMJ Articular Disc & Intervertebral Discs = FIBROCARTILAGE (Type I collagen, NO perichondrium)! Epiglottis & Ear auricle = ELASTIC cartilage. Costal & Tracheal rings = HYALINE cartilage (Type II collagen). Articular cartilage and fibrocartilage lack a perichondrium.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* TMJ Articular Disc = FIBROCARTILAGE (Type I collagen)! NO perichondrium!\n* Ear pinna & Epiglottis = ELASTIC cartilage (bends and springs back)!\n* Trachea & Ribs (Costal) = HYALINE cartilage (glassy, Type II collagen)!\n* Cartilage has NO blood vessels (avascular) -> heals very slowly!",
    "summary": "Cartilage Histology: Types, Cells & Structure:\n- General Characteristics of Cartilage:\n  * Firm, resilient, highly specialized skeletal connective tissue.\n  * AVASCULAR, ANEURAL, and ALYMPHATIC: Receives all nutrition, oxygen, and metabolic exchange by diffusion through matrix from surrounding perichondrial blood vessels or synovial fluid.\n  * Chondrocytes reside within isolated cavities called LACUNAE; synthesize and maintain ECM.\n  * Perichondrium: Dense irregular connective tissue capsule covering cartilage; contains outer fibrous vascular layer and inner chondrogenic layer with chondroblasts.\n  * ABSENT PERICHONDRIUM in: 1) Articular cartilage of synovial joints, 2) Fibrocartilage, 3) Epiphyseal growth plates.\n- The Three Types of Cartilage:\n  1. HYALINE CARTILAGE (Most Abundant Cartilage):\n     * Glassy, semitransparent matrix rich in Type II collagen fibrils and Aggrecan proteoglycans.\n     * Chondrocytes often arranged in clusters of 2-8 cells derived from a single parent cell called ISOGENOUS GROUPS.\n     * Basophilic Territorial Matrix (surrounds lacunae, high GAG content) vs Interterritorial Matrix.\n     * Locations: Embryonic cartilaginous skeleton, Epiphyseal plates, Costal cartilages, Articular cartilage of synovial joints, Respiratory tract (nasal septum, larynx: thyroid, cricoid, arytenoid cartilages; trachea and bronchi).\n  2. ELASTIC CARTILAGE:\n     * Contains abundant network of branched ELASTIC FIBERS in addition to Type II collagen fibrils.\n     * Greater flexibility and elasticity; resilient recoil; does not calcify with age.\n     * Always covered by perichondrium.\n     * Locations: External ear (auricle/pinna), External auditory meatus, Eustachian (auditory) tube, Epiglottis, Cuneiform and Corniculate cartilages of larynx.\n  3. FIBROCARTILAGE:\n     * Combination of dense regular connective tissue and hyaline cartilage; possesses thick, parallel bundles of TYPE I COLLAGEN and alternating rows of chondrocytes in lacunae.\n     * LACKS A PERICHONDRIUM; exceptional tensile strength and shock absorption.\n     * Locations: Intervertebral discs (annulus fibrosus), Pubic symphysis, Glenoid and acetabular labrum, Articular disc of the Temporomandibular Joint (TMJ).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe TMJ articular disc is unique: unlike typical synovial joints covered by hyaline cartilage, the articulating surfaces of the mandibular condyle and the temporal glenoid fossa, as well as the TMJ articular disc itself, are lined by dense AVASCULAR FIBROCARTILAGE (Type I collagen). Fibrocartilage withstands heavy compression and shear stress far better than hyaline cartilage and possesses superior repair capacity.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nTMJ Articular Disc & Intervertebral Discs = FIBROCARTILAGE (Type I collagen, NO perichondrium)! Epiglottis & Ear auricle = ELASTIC cartilage. Costal & Tracheal rings = HYALINE cartilage (Type II collagen). Articular cartilage and fibrocartilage lack a perichondrium.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* TMJ Articular Disc = FIBROCARTILAGE (Type I collagen)! NO perichondrium!\n* Ear pinna & Epiglottis = ELASTIC cartilage (bends and springs back)!\n* Trachea & Ribs (Costal) = HYALINE cartilage (glassy, Type II collagen)!\n* Cartilage has NO blood vessels (avascular) -> heals very slowly!",
