    "originalPrint": "Bone Histology: Matrix & Bone Cells:\n- Composition of Bone Matrix:\n  * Inorganic Matrix (~65% dry weight): Hydroxyapatite crystals [Ca10(PO4)6(OH)2]; stores 99% of total body calcium; provides compressive hardness.\n  * Organic Matrix (Osteoid - ~35% dry weight): Dominated by TYPE I COLLAGEN (90%); ground substance proteoglycans, osteocalcin, osteonectin, osteopontin; provides tensile flexibility.\n- The Bone Cells:\n  1. Osteoprogenitor (Osteogenic) Cells:\n     * Pluripotent mesenchymal stem cells located in periosteum and endosteum; differentiate into osteoblasts.\n  2. Osteoblasts ('Bone Builders'):\n     * Cuboidal or columnar cells lining bone-forming surfaces; prominent RER and Golgi apparatus.\n     * Function: Synthesize and secrete unmineralized organic bone matrix (OSTEOID) and initiate mineralization by releasing matrix vesicles.\n     * High alkaline phosphatase (ALP) activity; express receptors for Parathyroid Hormone (PTH), causing release of RANKL to stimulate osteoclasts.\n  3. Osteocytes ('Bone Maintainers'):\n     * Mature, differentiated bone cells entrapped within mineralized matrix inside LACUNAE.\n     * Possess extensive dendritic cell processes extending through narrow radiating tunnels called CANALICULI; interconnected via GAP JUNCTIONS.\n     * Function: Maintain bone matrix; act as mechanosensors detecting mechanical strain and fluid flow; secrete Sclerostin to regulate bone remodeling.\n  4. Osteoclasts ('Bone Resorbers'):\n     * Large, MULTINUCLEATED GIANT CELLS (up to 50 nuclei); derived from hematopoietic MONOCYTE/MACROPHAGE lineage (NOT from osteoprogenitor cells!).\n     * Reside inside shallow enzymatic resorption depressions called HOWSHIP'S LACUNAE (resorption bays).\n     * Specialized Structural Zones:\n       - Ruffled Border: Deeply folded plasma membrane forming active resorption microenvironment.\n       - Sealing (Clear) Zone: Actin ring sealing resorption compartment from extracellular space.\n     * Resorption Mechanism: Carbonic anhydrase generates H+ ions; H+ ATPase pump acidifies compartment (dissolves inorganic hydroxyapatite); Cathepsin K and matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) digest organic collagen matrix.\n     * Marker enzyme: Tartrate-Resistant Acid Phosphatase (TRAP).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nBone remodeling is an orchestrated equilibrium between osteoblastic matrix deposition and osteoclastic resorption. Parathyroid hormone (PTH) does not bind osteoclasts directly; instead, PTH binds osteoblasts, stimulating them to express RANKL (Receptor Activator of Nuclear Factor Kappa-B Ligand). RANKL binds RANK on osteoclast precursors, inducing fusion into active multinucleated osteoclasts. Calcitonin directly binds osteoclast receptors, rapidly inhibiting their ruffled border motility.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nOsteoclasts: Derived from MONOCYTE/MACROPHAGE lineage, MULTINUCLEATED giant cells, reside in HOWSHIP'S LACUNAE, possess RUFFLED BORDER, and stain for TRAP! Osteoblasts = Bone formation, secretes osteoid, high Alkaline Phosphatase. Osteocytes reside in lacunae connected by canaliculi.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* OsteoBLASTS = BUILD bone! (High ALP, cuboidal cells)\n* OsteoCLASTS = CRUSH / RESORB bone! (Multinucleated, macrophage origin, Howship's lacunae, Ruffled border)!\n* OsteoCYTES = Mature bone cells trapped in LACUNAE with CANALICULI (gap junctions)!\n* Inorganic bone = HYDROXYAPATITE (calcium phosphate)!",
    "summary": "Bone Histology: Matrix & Bone Cells:\n- Composition of Bone Matrix:\n  * Inorganic Matrix (~65% dry weight): Hydroxyapatite crystals [Ca10(PO4)6(OH)2]; stores 99% of total body calcium; provides compressive hardness.\n  * Organic Matrix (Osteoid - ~35% dry weight): Dominated by TYPE I COLLAGEN (90%); ground substance proteoglycans, osteocalcin, osteonectin, osteopontin; provides tensile flexibility.\n- The Bone Cells:\n  1. Osteoprogenitor (Osteogenic) Cells:\n     * Pluripotent mesenchymal stem cells located in periosteum and endosteum; differentiate into osteoblasts.\n  2. Osteoblasts ('Bone Builders'):\n     * Cuboidal or columnar cells lining bone-forming surfaces; prominent RER and Golgi apparatus.\n     * Function: Synthesize and secrete unmineralized organic bone matrix (OSTEOID) and initiate mineralization by releasing matrix vesicles.\n     * High alkaline phosphatase (ALP) activity; express receptors for Parathyroid Hormone (PTH), causing release of RANKL to stimulate osteoclasts.\n  3. Osteocytes ('Bone Maintainers'):\n     * Mature, differentiated bone cells entrapped within mineralized matrix inside LACUNAE.\n     * Possess extensive dendritic cell processes extending through narrow radiating tunnels called CANALICULI; interconnected via GAP JUNCTIONS.\n     * Function: Maintain bone matrix; act as mechanosensors detecting mechanical strain and fluid flow; secrete Sclerostin to regulate bone remodeling.\n  4. Osteoclasts ('Bone Resorbers'):\n     * Large, MULTINUCLEATED GIANT CELLS (up to 50 nuclei); derived from hematopoietic MONOCYTE/MACROPHAGE lineage (NOT from osteoprogenitor cells!).\n     * Reside inside shallow enzymatic resorption depressions called HOWSHIP'S LACUNAE (resorption bays).\n     * Specialized Structural Zones:\n       - Ruffled Border: Deeply folded plasma membrane forming active resorption microenvironment.\n       - Sealing (Clear) Zone: Actin ring sealing resorption compartment from extracellular space.\n     * Resorption Mechanism: Carbonic anhydrase generates H+ ions; H+ ATPase pump acidifies compartment (dissolves inorganic hydroxyapatite); Cathepsin K and matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) digest organic collagen matrix.\n     * Marker enzyme: Tartrate-Resistant Acid Phosphatase (TRAP).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nBone remodeling is an orchestrated equilibrium between osteoblastic matrix deposition and osteoclastic resorption. Parathyroid hormone (PTH) does not bind osteoclasts directly; instead, PTH binds osteoblasts, stimulating them to express RANKL (Receptor Activator of Nuclear Factor Kappa-B Ligand). RANKL binds RANK on osteoclast precursors, inducing fusion into active multinucleated osteoclasts. Calcitonin directly binds osteoclast receptors, rapidly inhibiting their ruffled border motility.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nOsteoclasts: Derived from MONOCYTE/MACROPHAGE lineage, MULTINUCLEATED giant cells, reside in HOWSHIP'S LACUNAE, possess RUFFLED BORDER, and stain for TRAP! Osteoblasts = Bone formation, secretes osteoid, high Alkaline Phosphatase. Osteocytes reside in lacunae connected by canaliculi.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* OsteoBLASTS = BUILD bone! (High ALP, cuboidal cells)\n* OsteoCLASTS = CRUSH / RESORB bone! (Multinucleated, macrophage origin, Howship's lacunae, Ruffled border)!\n* OsteoCYTES = Mature bone cells trapped in LACUNAE with CANALICULI (gap junctions)!\n* Inorganic bone = HYDROXYAPATITE (calcium phosphate)!",
