    "originalPrint": "Bone Development: Intramembranous vs Endochondral Ossification:\n- Modes of Osteogenesis:\n  1. INTRAMEMBRANOUS (DESMAL) OSSIFICATION:\n     * Direct differentiation of embryonic mesenchymal cells into osteoblasts without a cartilaginous precursor.\n     * Sequence: Mesenchymal condensation -> differentiation into osteoblasts -> osteoid secretion -> calcification -> spicule formation -> trabecular/woven bone -> remodeling into lamellar bone and periosteum.\n     * Bones Formed:\n       - Flat bones of the skull (frontal, parietal, occipital, temporal squama).\n       - Maxilla and Zygomatic bones.\n       - Body of the MANDIBLE (except condyle and mental ossicles).\n       - Clavicle (medial part endochondral, shaft intramembranous).\n  2. ENDOCHONDRAL (INTRACARTILAGINOUS) OSSIFICATION:\n     * Bone formation utilizing a pre-existing HYALINE CARTILAGE template that is progressively eroded, calcified, and replaced by bone tissue.\n     * Primary Ossification Center: In diaphysis (shaft) of long bones.\n     * Secondary Ossification Centers: In epiphyses (ends) of long bones.\n     * Bones Formed:\n       - Long bones of extremities (femur, humerus, tibia, fibula).\n       - Vertebrae, ribs, sternum, pelvis, base of skull (sphenoid, ethmoid, petrous temporal).\n       - Mandibular CONDYLE (specialized secondary cartilage).\n- Zones of the Epiphyseal Growth Plate (From Epiphysis to Diaphysis):\n  1. Zone of Resting (Reserve) Cartilage: Typical inactive hyaline cartilage.\n  2. Zone of Proliferation: Chondrocytes divide rapidly by mitosis and organize into distinctive longitudinal columns ('stacks of coins').\n  3. Zone of Hypertrophy & Maturation: Chondrocytes swell markedly, accumulate glycogen, and compress surrounding matrix.\n  4. Zone of Calcification: Chondrocytes undergo apoptosis; extracellular cartilage matrix becomes calcified.\n  5. Zone of Ossification: Osteoprogenitor cells and capillaries invade calcified cartilage; osteoblasts deposit osteoid on calcified cartilage spicules.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe developmental origins of the facial skeleton are critical in craniofacial biology. The maxilla and mandibular body develop via intramembranous ossification directly within neural-crest-derived ectomesenchyme. However, the mandibular condyle develops through secondary endochondral cartilage, allowing multidirectional adaptive growth in response to functional occlusal and orthodontic forces during childhood and adolescence.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nIntramembranous Ossification = Direct bone formation from mesenchyme with NO cartilage precursor (Maxilla, Mandibular body, Flat bones of skull). Endochondral Ossification = Cartilage template replaced by bone (Long bones, Base of skull, Mandibular CONDYLE). Zone of proliferation = Stacks of coins.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* INTRAMEMBRANOUS = DIRECT from mesenchyme, NO cartilage! (Maxilla, Mandible body, Cranial vault).\n* ENDOCHONDRAL = Uses a HYALINE CARTILAGE MODEL! (Long bones, Mandibular CONDYLE).\n* Mandibular condyle is the exception in the mandible (has cartilage)!",
    "summary": "Bone Development: Intramembranous vs Endochondral Ossification:\n- Modes of Osteogenesis:\n  1. INTRAMEMBRANOUS (DESMAL) OSSIFICATION:\n     * Direct differentiation of embryonic mesenchymal cells into osteoblasts without a cartilaginous precursor.\n     * Sequence: Mesenchymal condensation -> differentiation into osteoblasts -> osteoid secretion -> calcification -> spicule formation -> trabecular/woven bone -> remodeling into lamellar bone and periosteum.\n     * Bones Formed:\n       - Flat bones of the skull (frontal, parietal, occipital, temporal squama).\n       - Maxilla and Zygomatic bones.\n       - Body of the MANDIBLE (except condyle and mental ossicles).\n       - Clavicle (medial part endochondral, shaft intramembranous).\n  2. ENDOCHONDRAL (INTRACARTILAGINOUS) OSSIFICATION:\n     * Bone formation utilizing a pre-existing HYALINE CARTILAGE template that is progressively eroded, calcified, and replaced by bone tissue.\n     * Primary Ossification Center: In diaphysis (shaft) of long bones.\n     * Secondary Ossification Centers: In epiphyses (ends) of long bones.\n     * Bones Formed:\n       - Long bones of extremities (femur, humerus, tibia, fibula).\n       - Vertebrae, ribs, sternum, pelvis, base of skull (sphenoid, ethmoid, petrous temporal).\n       - Mandibular CONDYLE (specialized secondary cartilage).\n- Zones of the Epiphyseal Growth Plate (From Epiphysis to Diaphysis):\n  1. Zone of Resting (Reserve) Cartilage: Typical inactive hyaline cartilage.\n  2. Zone of Proliferation: Chondrocytes divide rapidly by mitosis and organize into distinctive longitudinal columns ('stacks of coins').\n  3. Zone of Hypertrophy & Maturation: Chondrocytes swell markedly, accumulate glycogen, and compress surrounding matrix.\n  4. Zone of Calcification: Chondrocytes undergo apoptosis; extracellular cartilage matrix becomes calcified.\n  5. Zone of Ossification: Osteoprogenitor cells and capillaries invade calcified cartilage; osteoblasts deposit osteoid on calcified cartilage spicules.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe developmental origins of the facial skeleton are critical in craniofacial biology. The maxilla and mandibular body develop via intramembranous ossification directly within neural-crest-derived ectomesenchyme. However, the mandibular condyle develops through secondary endochondral cartilage, allowing multidirectional adaptive growth in response to functional occlusal and orthodontic forces during childhood and adolescence.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nIntramembranous Ossification = Direct bone formation from mesenchyme with NO cartilage precursor (Maxilla, Mandibular body, Flat bones of skull). Endochondral Ossification = Cartilage template replaced by bone (Long bones, Base of skull, Mandibular CONDYLE). Zone of proliferation = Stacks of coins.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* INTRAMEMBRANOUS = DIRECT from mesenchyme, NO cartilage! (Maxilla, Mandible body, Cranial vault).\n* ENDOCHONDRAL = Uses a HYALINE CARTILAGE MODEL! (Long bones, Mandibular CONDYLE).\n* Mandibular condyle is the exception in the mandible (has cartilage)!",
