    "originalPrint": "Histology of Muscle Tissues: Comparative Analysis:\n- Classification of Muscle Types:\n  1. SKELETAL MUSCLE:\n     * Morphology: Long, cylindrical, unbranched fibers; STRIATED (cross-striations of A and I bands).\n     * Nuclei: MULTINUCLEATED; nuclei located strictly in the PERIPHERY / subsarcolemmal position.\n     * Control: Voluntary; innervated by somatic motor nervous system.\n     * Sarcoplasmic System: TRIAD at the A-I junction (composed of 1 central T-tubule + 2 terminal cisternae of sarcoplasmic reticulum).\n     * Regeneration: Satellite cells reside beneath basal lamina; limited regenerative capacity.\n  2. CARDIAC MUSCLE (Myocardium):\n     * Morphology: Short, cylindrical, BRANCHED fibers; STRIATED.\n     * Nuclei: Single (occasionally two) oval nucleus located CENTRALLY.\n     * Control: Involuntary; intrinsic rhythmicity modulated by autonomic nervous system.\n     * Diagnostic Feature: INTERCALATED DISCS (transverse junctions linking adjacent cardiomyocytes; contain Fascia Adherens, Macula Adherens/Desmosomes, and GAP JUNCTIONS for electrical syncytium).\n     * Sarcoplasmic System: DIAD located at the Z-line (composed of 1 T-tubule + 1 cisterna of sarcoplasmic reticulum).\n     * Regeneration: Non-regenerative; damaged heart tissue heals by fibrous scar formation.\n  3. SMOOTH MUSCLE:\n     * Morphology: Small, SPINDLE-SHAPED (fusiform) fibers; NON-STRIATED (lacks organized sarcomeres).\n     * Nuclei: Single elongated, cigar-shaped nucleus located CENTRALLY; assumes corkscrew appearance during contraction.\n     * Control: Involuntary; innervated by autonomic nervous system.\n     * Contractile Apparatus: Actin and myosin filaments criss-cross cell, anchored to DENSE BODIES (alpha-actinin; functional equivalents of Z-lines).\n     * Calcium Regulation: Lacks Troponin; Ca2+ binds CALMODULIN to activate Myosin Light Chain Kinase (MLCK).\n     * Locations: Blood vessel walls, gastrointestinal tract, respiratory bronchioles, urinary bladder, myometrium, arrector pili.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe physiological differences between muscle types govern their pharmacology. Skeletal muscle relies on motor end-plate acetylcholine receptors (nicotinic) and rapid triad intracellular Ca2+ cycling. Cardiac muscle depends heavily on extracellular Ca2+ influx via L-type calcium channels during its action potential plateau to trigger calcium-induced calcium release (CICR). Smooth muscle contraction utilizes Ca2+-calmodulin-MLCK phosphorylation rather than troponin displacement.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nSkeletal Muscle = Multinucleated, PERIPHERAL nuclei, Triad at A-I junction. Cardiac Muscle = Branching fibers, CENTRAL nucleus, INTERCALATED DISCS (gap junctions + desmosomes), Diad at Z-line. Smooth Muscle = Fusiform/spindle-shaped, Non-striated, Central cigar nucleus, uses CALMODULIN (No troponin!).",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Skeletal = Multinucleated, nuclei at the PERIPHERY / edge!\n* Cardiac = BRANCHED, CENTRAL nucleus, INTERCALATED DISCS (gap junctions)!\n* Smooth = Non-striated, cigar-shaped central nucleus, uses CALMODULIN (NO troponin)!\n* Triad = Skeletal muscle; Diad = Cardiac muscle!",
    "summary": "Histology of Muscle Tissues: Comparative Analysis:\n- Classification of Muscle Types:\n  1. SKELETAL MUSCLE:\n     * Morphology: Long, cylindrical, unbranched fibers; STRIATED (cross-striations of A and I bands).\n     * Nuclei: MULTINUCLEATED; nuclei located strictly in the PERIPHERY / subsarcolemmal position.\n     * Control: Voluntary; innervated by somatic motor nervous system.\n     * Sarcoplasmic System: TRIAD at the A-I junction (composed of 1 central T-tubule + 2 terminal cisternae of sarcoplasmic reticulum).\n     * Regeneration: Satellite cells reside beneath basal lamina; limited regenerative capacity.\n  2. CARDIAC MUSCLE (Myocardium):\n     * Morphology: Short, cylindrical, BRANCHED fibers; STRIATED.\n     * Nuclei: Single (occasionally two) oval nucleus located CENTRALLY.\n     * Control: Involuntary; intrinsic rhythmicity modulated by autonomic nervous system.\n     * Diagnostic Feature: INTERCALATED DISCS (transverse junctions linking adjacent cardiomyocytes; contain Fascia Adherens, Macula Adherens/Desmosomes, and GAP JUNCTIONS for electrical syncytium).\n     * Sarcoplasmic System: DIAD located at the Z-line (composed of 1 T-tubule + 1 cisterna of sarcoplasmic reticulum).\n     * Regeneration: Non-regenerative; damaged heart tissue heals by fibrous scar formation.\n  3. SMOOTH MUSCLE:\n     * Morphology: Small, SPINDLE-SHAPED (fusiform) fibers; NON-STRIATED (lacks organized sarcomeres).\n     * Nuclei: Single elongated, cigar-shaped nucleus located CENTRALLY; assumes corkscrew appearance during contraction.\n     * Control: Involuntary; innervated by autonomic nervous system.\n     * Contractile Apparatus: Actin and myosin filaments criss-cross cell, anchored to DENSE BODIES (alpha-actinin; functional equivalents of Z-lines).\n     * Calcium Regulation: Lacks Troponin; Ca2+ binds CALMODULIN to activate Myosin Light Chain Kinase (MLCK).\n     * Locations: Blood vessel walls, gastrointestinal tract, respiratory bronchioles, urinary bladder, myometrium, arrector pili.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe physiological differences between muscle types govern their pharmacology. Skeletal muscle relies on motor end-plate acetylcholine receptors (nicotinic) and rapid triad intracellular Ca2+ cycling. Cardiac muscle depends heavily on extracellular Ca2+ influx via L-type calcium channels during its action potential plateau to trigger calcium-induced calcium release (CICR). Smooth muscle contraction utilizes Ca2+-calmodulin-MLCK phosphorylation rather than troponin displacement.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nSkeletal Muscle = Multinucleated, PERIPHERAL nuclei, Triad at A-I junction. Cardiac Muscle = Branching fibers, CENTRAL nucleus, INTERCALATED DISCS (gap junctions + desmosomes), Diad at Z-line. Smooth Muscle = Fusiform/spindle-shaped, Non-striated, Central cigar nucleus, uses CALMODULIN (No troponin!).\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Skeletal = Multinucleated, nuclei at the PERIPHERY / edge!\n* Cardiac = BRANCHED, CENTRAL nucleus, INTERCALATED DISCS (gap junctions)!\n* Smooth = Non-striated, cigar-shaped central nucleus, uses CALMODULIN (NO troponin)!\n* Triad = Skeletal muscle; Diad = Cardiac muscle!",
