    "originalPrint": "The Sarcomere & Contractile Molecular Machinery:\n- The Sarcomere Architecture:\n  * The fundamental contractile repeating unit of striated muscle fibrils; extends from Z-DISC TO Z-DISC.\n- Bands and Zones of the Sarcomere:\n  1. Z-Line (Z-Disc): Dark transverse line anchoring thin actin filaments via alpha-actinin; defines sarcomere boundary.\n  2. A-Band (Anisotropic Band): Dark central band; spans the entire length of thick MYOSIN filaments; may overlap with actin; DOES NOT CHANGE LENGTH during muscle contraction!\n  3. I-Band (Isotropic Band): Light band composed exclusively of thin ACTIN filaments; bisected by the Z-disc; NARROWS and shortens during contraction.\n  4. H-Zone: Pale central region within A-band containing thick MYOSIN filaments ONLY (no actin overlap); NARROWS and disappears during maximal contraction.\n  5. M-Line: Central transverse protein meshwork in middle of H-zone; anchors thick myosin filaments.\n- Molecular Filaments:\n  * Thick Filaments: MYOSIN II molecules (2 heavy chains forming globular heads with ATPase activity + 4 light chains).\n  * Thin Filaments: Double-stranded F-ACTIN helix + TROPOMYOSIN (covers myosin-binding sites at rest) + TROPONIN Complex:\n    - Troponin T (TnT): Binds to tropomyosin.\n    - Troponin I (TnI): Inhibits actin-myosin binding.\n    - Troponin C (TnC): Binds CALCIUM ions.\n- Sliding Filament Mechanism of Contraction:\n  1. Somatic action potential releases Acetylcholine at neuromuscular junction -> depolarizes sarcolemma -> travels down T-tubules.\n  2. DHP receptors activate Ryanodine receptors (RyR1), releasing Ca2+ from sarcoplasmic reticulum into sarcoplasm.\n  3. Ca2+ binds Troponin C -> conformational change pulls Tropomyosin off actin active sites.\n  4. Myosin head binds actin forming CROSS-BRIDGE; release of Pi triggers POWER STROKE (myosin pulls actin toward M-line).\n  5. ATP binds myosin head, causing detachment of cross-bridge; ATP hydrolysis re-cocks myosin head.\n  * Rigor Mortis: Absence of ATP after death prevents myosin detachment from actin -> persistent muscle rigidity.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nDuring muscle shortening, neither actin nor myosin filaments actually change their individual physical lengths. Instead, thin actin filaments slide past thick myosin filaments toward the M-line. Consequently, the A-band remains completely constant, while both the I-band and the H-zone progressively narrow and obliterate as overlap becomes maximal. ATP is required for detachment, explaining post-mortem rigor mortis.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nDuring Muscle Contraction:\n- A-Band = REMAINS CONSTANT (never changes length)!\n- I-Band & H-Zone = SHORTEN / NARROW / Disappear!\n- Sarcomere extends from Z-line to Z-line.\n- Calcium binds to TROPONIN C to uncover binding sites.\n- Rigor mortis is caused by lack of ATP (cannot detach cross-bridges).",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Sarcomere = Z-line to Z-line!\n* During Contraction: A-BAND STAYS THE SAME! I-band and H-zone get smaller/disappear!\n* Calcium binds to TROPONIN C!\n* ATP needed to DETACH myosin head (no ATP = Rigor mortis)!",
    "summary": "The Sarcomere & Contractile Molecular Machinery:\n- The Sarcomere Architecture:\n  * The fundamental contractile repeating unit of striated muscle fibrils; extends from Z-DISC TO Z-DISC.\n- Bands and Zones of the Sarcomere:\n  1. Z-Line (Z-Disc): Dark transverse line anchoring thin actin filaments via alpha-actinin; defines sarcomere boundary.\n  2. A-Band (Anisotropic Band): Dark central band; spans the entire length of thick MYOSIN filaments; may overlap with actin; DOES NOT CHANGE LENGTH during muscle contraction!\n  3. I-Band (Isotropic Band): Light band composed exclusively of thin ACTIN filaments; bisected by the Z-disc; NARROWS and shortens during contraction.\n  4. H-Zone: Pale central region within A-band containing thick MYOSIN filaments ONLY (no actin overlap); NARROWS and disappears during maximal contraction.\n  5. M-Line: Central transverse protein meshwork in middle of H-zone; anchors thick myosin filaments.\n- Molecular Filaments:\n  * Thick Filaments: MYOSIN II molecules (2 heavy chains forming globular heads with ATPase activity + 4 light chains).\n  * Thin Filaments: Double-stranded F-ACTIN helix + TROPOMYOSIN (covers myosin-binding sites at rest) + TROPONIN Complex:\n    - Troponin T (TnT): Binds to tropomyosin.\n    - Troponin I (TnI): Inhibits actin-myosin binding.\n    - Troponin C (TnC): Binds CALCIUM ions.\n- Sliding Filament Mechanism of Contraction:\n  1. Somatic action potential releases Acetylcholine at neuromuscular junction -> depolarizes sarcolemma -> travels down T-tubules.\n  2. DHP receptors activate Ryanodine receptors (RyR1), releasing Ca2+ from sarcoplasmic reticulum into sarcoplasm.\n  3. Ca2+ binds Troponin C -> conformational change pulls Tropomyosin off actin active sites.\n  4. Myosin head binds actin forming CROSS-BRIDGE; release of Pi triggers POWER STROKE (myosin pulls actin toward M-line).\n  5. ATP binds myosin head, causing detachment of cross-bridge; ATP hydrolysis re-cocks myosin head.\n  * Rigor Mortis: Absence of ATP after death prevents myosin detachment from actin -> persistent muscle rigidity.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nDuring muscle shortening, neither actin nor myosin filaments actually change their individual physical lengths. Instead, thin actin filaments slide past thick myosin filaments toward the M-line. Consequently, the A-band remains completely constant, while both the I-band and the H-zone progressively narrow and obliterate as overlap becomes maximal. ATP is required for detachment, explaining post-mortem rigor mortis.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nDuring Muscle Contraction:\n- A-Band = REMAINS CONSTANT (never changes length)!\n- I-Band & H-Zone = SHORTEN / NARROW / Disappear!\n- Sarcomere extends from Z-line to Z-line.\n- Calcium binds to TROPONIN C to uncover binding sites.\n- Rigor mortis is caused by lack of ATP (cannot detach cross-bridges).\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Sarcomere = Z-line to Z-line!\n* During Contraction: A-BAND STAYS THE SAME! I-band and H-zone get smaller/disappear!\n* Calcium binds to TROPONIN C!\n* ATP needed to DETACH myosin head (no ATP = Rigor mortis)!",
