    "originalPrint": "Histology of Nervous Tissue: Neurons & Synaptic Structure:\n- General Organization:\n  * Central Nervous System (CNS: Brain & Spinal Cord) and Peripheral Nervous System (PNS: Cranial & Spinal nerves, Ganglia).\n  * Composed of: 1) Excitable Neurons and 2) Supporting Neuroglia.\n- Structural Components of a Neuron:\n  1. Cell Body (Soma / Perikaryon):\n     * Synthetic center of neuron; contains large spherical euchromatic nucleus with prominent nucleolus ('owl's eye' appearance).\n     * Nissl Bodies (Chromatophilic Substance): Dense aggregates of rough ER and polyribosomes; extensive protein synthesis; found in soma and proximal dendrites, but ABSENT from axon hillock and axon.\n  2. Dendrites: Highly branched, tapering receptor processes; receive input via dendritic spines.\n  3. Axon (Nerve Fiber):\n     * Single, uniform cylinder propagating action potentials AWAY from soma; lacks Nissl bodies and Golgi.\n     * Axon Hillock: Funnel-shaped junction between soma and axon; lacks Nissl substance.\n     * Initial Segment: Region between axon hillock and first myelin sheath; highest density of voltage-gated Na+ channels -> SITE OF ACTION POTENTIAL INITIATION.\n- Structural Classification of Neurons:\n  1. Multipolar Neurons: Most common; 1 axon + multiple dendrites (motor neurons, pyramidal cells of cerebral cortex, Purkinje cells of cerebellum).\n  2. Bipolar Neurons: 1 axon + 1 main dendrite on opposite poles (Retina of eye, Olfactory epithelium, Vestibulocochlear ganglion CN VIII).\n  3. Pseudounipolar (Unipolar) Neurons: Single axon emerges from soma and T-splits into peripheral and central branches (Primary sensory neurons in DORSAL ROOT GANGLIA and TRIGEMINAL GANGLION; Mesencephalic nucleus of CN V).\n- Synaptic Transmission:\n  * Presynaptic terminal with voltage-gated Ca2+ channels -> Ca2+ influx triggers exocytosis of neurotransmitter vesicles -> diffuse across 20-30 nm Synaptic Cleft -> bind post-synaptic receptors (ionotropic or metabotropic).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe axon hillock is the critical computational decision-center of the neuron. Because it possesses the lowest threshold for depolarization (densest population of Nav1.6 channels), summation of all excitatory (EPSP) and inhibitory (IPSP) postsynaptic potentials occurs here. The total absence of protein-synthesizing Nissl bodies in axons necessitates continuous bidirectional axoplasmic transport (kinesin/dynein) along microtubules.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nNissl bodies are ABSENT in the Axon and Axon Hillock! Action potentials initiate at the INITIAL SEGMENT of the axon. Sensory ganglia (Trigeminal ganglion, dorsal root ganglia) = PSEUDOUNIPOLAR neurons! Retina and Olfactory = BIPOLAR neurons. Motor neurons = MULTIPOLAR.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Nissl bodies = In cell body & dendrites, but NEVER IN AXON or AXON HILLOCK!\n* Sensory Ganglia (Gasserian / Trigeminal Ganglion) = PSEUDOUNIPOLAR neurons!\n* Retina & Olfactory = BIPOLAR neurons!\n* Motor neurons = MULTIPOLAR neurons!",
    "summary": "Histology of Nervous Tissue: Neurons & Synaptic Structure:\n- General Organization:\n  * Central Nervous System (CNS: Brain & Spinal Cord) and Peripheral Nervous System (PNS: Cranial & Spinal nerves, Ganglia).\n  * Composed of: 1) Excitable Neurons and 2) Supporting Neuroglia.\n- Structural Components of a Neuron:\n  1. Cell Body (Soma / Perikaryon):\n     * Synthetic center of neuron; contains large spherical euchromatic nucleus with prominent nucleolus ('owl's eye' appearance).\n     * Nissl Bodies (Chromatophilic Substance): Dense aggregates of rough ER and polyribosomes; extensive protein synthesis; found in soma and proximal dendrites, but ABSENT from axon hillock and axon.\n  2. Dendrites: Highly branched, tapering receptor processes; receive input via dendritic spines.\n  3. Axon (Nerve Fiber):\n     * Single, uniform cylinder propagating action potentials AWAY from soma; lacks Nissl bodies and Golgi.\n     * Axon Hillock: Funnel-shaped junction between soma and axon; lacks Nissl substance.\n     * Initial Segment: Region between axon hillock and first myelin sheath; highest density of voltage-gated Na+ channels -> SITE OF ACTION POTENTIAL INITIATION.\n- Structural Classification of Neurons:\n  1. Multipolar Neurons: Most common; 1 axon + multiple dendrites (motor neurons, pyramidal cells of cerebral cortex, Purkinje cells of cerebellum).\n  2. Bipolar Neurons: 1 axon + 1 main dendrite on opposite poles (Retina of eye, Olfactory epithelium, Vestibulocochlear ganglion CN VIII).\n  3. Pseudounipolar (Unipolar) Neurons: Single axon emerges from soma and T-splits into peripheral and central branches (Primary sensory neurons in DORSAL ROOT GANGLIA and TRIGEMINAL GANGLION; Mesencephalic nucleus of CN V).\n- Synaptic Transmission:\n  * Presynaptic terminal with voltage-gated Ca2+ channels -> Ca2+ influx triggers exocytosis of neurotransmitter vesicles -> diffuse across 20-30 nm Synaptic Cleft -> bind post-synaptic receptors (ionotropic or metabotropic).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe axon hillock is the critical computational decision-center of the neuron. Because it possesses the lowest threshold for depolarization (densest population of Nav1.6 channels), summation of all excitatory (EPSP) and inhibitory (IPSP) postsynaptic potentials occurs here. The total absence of protein-synthesizing Nissl bodies in axons necessitates continuous bidirectional axoplasmic transport (kinesin/dynein) along microtubules.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nNissl bodies are ABSENT in the Axon and Axon Hillock! Action potentials initiate at the INITIAL SEGMENT of the axon. Sensory ganglia (Trigeminal ganglion, dorsal root ganglia) = PSEUDOUNIPOLAR neurons! Retina and Olfactory = BIPOLAR neurons. Motor neurons = MULTIPOLAR.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Nissl bodies = In cell body & dendrites, but NEVER IN AXON or AXON HILLOCK!\n* Sensory Ganglia (Gasserian / Trigeminal Ganglion) = PSEUDOUNIPOLAR neurons!\n* Retina & Olfactory = BIPOLAR neurons!\n* Motor neurons = MULTIPOLAR neurons!",
