    "originalPrint": "Histology of the Circulatory System: Arteries, Veins & Capillaries:\n- The Three Concentric Tunics of Blood Vessels:\n  1. TUNICA INTIMA (Innermost Layer):\n     * Single layer of simple squamous ENDOTHELIUM resting on basal lamina + subendothelial loose connective tissue.\n     * Delimited from media by Internal Elastic Lamina (IEL - prominent in muscular arteries).\n     * Weibel-Palade Bodies: Endothelial storage granules containing von Willebrand Factor (vWF) and P-selectin.\n  2. TUNICA MEDIA (Middle Muscular Layer):\n     * Concentric layers of helically arranged SMOOTH MUSCLE cells interspersed with elastic fibers, reticular fibers, and proteoglycans.\n     * Delimited from adventitia by External Elastic Lamina (EEL).\n     * Thickest layer in ARTERIES.\n  3. TUNICA ADVENTITIA (Outermost Layer):\n     * Dense irregular connective tissue with longitudinally oriented collagen (Type I) and elastic fibers.\n     * Contains VASA VASORUM ('vessels of the vessels') supplying oxygen to thick outer walls, and Nervi Vascularis (autonomic vasomotor nerves).\n     * Thickest layer in VEINS.\n- Classification of Arteries:\n  * Elastic (Conducting) Arteries (Aorta, Pulmonary trunk, Common carotid): Tunica media packed with 40-70 concentric fenestrated elastic lamellae; expands during systole and recoils during diastole (Windkessel effect) to maintain constant diastolic pressure.\n  * Muscular (Distributing) Arteries: Dominated by smooth muscle in media; regulates regional blood flow.\n  * Arterioles: 1 to 3 layers of smooth muscle; PRIMARY SITE OF TOTAL PERIPHERAL RESISTANCE (TPR) and systemic arterial blood pressure regulation.\n- Classification of Capillaries:\n  1. Continuous Capillaries: Unbroken endothelial lining with tight junctions (zonula occludens) and continuous basal lamina (Muscle, Skin, Lungs, CNS [Blood-Brain Barrier]).\n  2. Fenestrated Capillaries: Endothelial cells perforated with 60-80 nm pores (fenestrae) closed by thin diaphragms (Endocrine glands, Kidney glomeruli [no diaphragms], Intestinal villi).\n  3. Sinusoidal (Discontinuous) Capillaries: Large irregular lumens, wide intercellular gaps, discontinuous basal lamina; permits passage of whole cells and large proteins (Liver, Spleen, Bone marrow).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nMicrovascular anatomy correlates directly with dental and systemic physiology. Arterioles are the primary resistance vessels; adrenergic vasoconstriction via epinephrine in dental local anesthetics acts primarily on alpha-1 receptors of arteriolar smooth muscle, reducing local blood flow and prolonging anesthesia. Sinusoidal capillaries in the spleen and liver slow circulation to allow macrophage inspection of passing erythrocytes.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nArterioles are the PRIMARY SITE of vascular resistance and blood pressure regulation! Tunica media is thickest in Arteries; Tunica adventitia is thickest in Veins. Vasa vasorum are located in the Tunica Adventitia. Weibel-Palade bodies store von Willebrand Factor. Sinusoids are found in Liver, Spleen, and Bone Marrow.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Arterioles = Main site of VASCULAR RESISTANCE & Blood Pressure control!\n* Media thickest in Arteries; Adventitia thickest in Veins!\n* Vasa vasorum = In the Adventitia (nourishes large blood vessel walls)!\n* Capillaries: Continuous (CNS), Fenestrated (Kidneys, Gut), Sinusoids (Liver, Spleen, Bone marrow)!",
    "summary": "Histology of the Circulatory System: Arteries, Veins & Capillaries:\n- The Three Concentric Tunics of Blood Vessels:\n  1. TUNICA INTIMA (Innermost Layer):\n     * Single layer of simple squamous ENDOTHELIUM resting on basal lamina + subendothelial loose connective tissue.\n     * Delimited from media by Internal Elastic Lamina (IEL - prominent in muscular arteries).\n     * Weibel-Palade Bodies: Endothelial storage granules containing von Willebrand Factor (vWF) and P-selectin.\n  2. TUNICA MEDIA (Middle Muscular Layer):\n     * Concentric layers of helically arranged SMOOTH MUSCLE cells interspersed with elastic fibers, reticular fibers, and proteoglycans.\n     * Delimited from adventitia by External Elastic Lamina (EEL).\n     * Thickest layer in ARTERIES.\n  3. TUNICA ADVENTITIA (Outermost Layer):\n     * Dense irregular connective tissue with longitudinally oriented collagen (Type I) and elastic fibers.\n     * Contains VASA VASORUM ('vessels of the vessels') supplying oxygen to thick outer walls, and Nervi Vascularis (autonomic vasomotor nerves).\n     * Thickest layer in VEINS.\n- Classification of Arteries:\n  * Elastic (Conducting) Arteries (Aorta, Pulmonary trunk, Common carotid): Tunica media packed with 40-70 concentric fenestrated elastic lamellae; expands during systole and recoils during diastole (Windkessel effect) to maintain constant diastolic pressure.\n  * Muscular (Distributing) Arteries: Dominated by smooth muscle in media; regulates regional blood flow.\n  * Arterioles: 1 to 3 layers of smooth muscle; PRIMARY SITE OF TOTAL PERIPHERAL RESISTANCE (TPR) and systemic arterial blood pressure regulation.\n- Classification of Capillaries:\n  1. Continuous Capillaries: Unbroken endothelial lining with tight junctions (zonula occludens) and continuous basal lamina (Muscle, Skin, Lungs, CNS [Blood-Brain Barrier]).\n  2. Fenestrated Capillaries: Endothelial cells perforated with 60-80 nm pores (fenestrae) closed by thin diaphragms (Endocrine glands, Kidney glomeruli [no diaphragms], Intestinal villi).\n  3. Sinusoidal (Discontinuous) Capillaries: Large irregular lumens, wide intercellular gaps, discontinuous basal lamina; permits passage of whole cells and large proteins (Liver, Spleen, Bone marrow).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nMicrovascular anatomy correlates directly with dental and systemic physiology. Arterioles are the primary resistance vessels; adrenergic vasoconstriction via epinephrine in dental local anesthetics acts primarily on alpha-1 receptors of arteriolar smooth muscle, reducing local blood flow and prolonging anesthesia. Sinusoidal capillaries in the spleen and liver slow circulation to allow macrophage inspection of passing erythrocytes.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nArterioles are the PRIMARY SITE of vascular resistance and blood pressure regulation! Tunica media is thickest in Arteries; Tunica adventitia is thickest in Veins. Vasa vasorum are located in the Tunica Adventitia. Weibel-Palade bodies store von Willebrand Factor. Sinusoids are found in Liver, Spleen, and Bone Marrow.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Arterioles = Main site of VASCULAR RESISTANCE & Blood Pressure control!\n* Media thickest in Arteries; Adventitia thickest in Veins!\n* Vasa vasorum = In the Adventitia (nourishes large blood vessel walls)!\n* Capillaries: Continuous (CNS), Fenestrated (Kidneys, Gut), Sinusoids (Liver, Spleen, Bone marrow)!",
