    "originalPrint": "Histology of Lymphoid Organs & Tissues:\n- Primary vs Secondary Lymphoid Organs:\n  * Primary (Site of lymphocyte maturation and antigen-independent differentiation): BONE MARROW (B-cell maturation) and THYMUS (T-cell maturation).\n  * Secondary (Site of antigen presentation and lymphocyte activation): Lymph Nodes, Spleen, Tonsils, MALT/Peyer's patches.\n- Histology of Primary Organs:\n  * Thymus:\n    - Bilobed organ in superior mediastinum; lobules divided into outer Cortex (dense immature T-cells undergoing positive selection) and inner Medulla (mature T-cells undergoing negative selection).\n    - HASSALL'S CORPUSCLES (Thymic Corpuscles): Concentrically arranged, keratinized epithelial reticular cells located exclusively in the MEDULLA of the thymus.\n    - Lacks lymphoid follicles and germinal centers; undergoes involution (fat replacement) after puberty.\n- Histology of Secondary Organs:\n  1. Lymph Node (Filters LYMPH):\n     * Capsule -> Subcapsular sinus -> Trabecular sinus -> Medullary sinus.\n     * Cortex: Outer Cortex contains lymphoid follicles with GERMINAL CENTERS (rich in proliferating B-LYMPHOCYTES, follicular dendritic cells, tangible body macrophages).\n     * Paracortex: T-CELL ZONE; houses High Endothelial Venules (HEVs) for lymphocyte homing from bloodstream; expands in viral cell-mediated reactions.\n     * Medulla: Medullary Cords (contain Plasma Cells producing antibodies) and Medullary Sinuses (macrophages).\n  2. Spleen (Filters BLOOD; Largest lymphoid organ):\n     * White Pulp (25%): Immune component; Periarteriolar Lymphoid Sheaths (PALS - T-cell zone surrounding central arterioles) and Splenic Nodules (B-cell follicles).\n     * Red Pulp (75%): Splenic Cords of Billroth (macrophages, plasma cells) and Splenic Sinusoids (stave endothelial cells); filters aged, damaged RBCs and stores platelets.\n  3. Waldeyer's Tonsillar Ring (Oral & Pharyngeal Gateway Protection):\n     * Palatine Tonsils: Lined by Stratified Squamous Non-Keratinized epithelium; 10-20 deep, branched TONSILLAR CRYPTS; prone to tonsilloliths.\n     * Pharyngeal Tonsil (Adenoids): Lined by Pseudostratified Ciliated Columnar epithelium; folds, no crypts.\n     * Lingual Tonsils: Base of tongue; stratified squamous, single crypt per nodule.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nIn oral infections and odontogenic abscesses, regional cervical lymph nodes enlarge rapidly (lymphadenopathy). This reflects massive B-cell blastogenesis inside cortical germinal centers and rapid recruitment of circulating lymphocytes through paracortical High Endothelial Venules (HEVs). The palatine tonsils guard the oropharynx, but their deep, blind-ending epithelial crypts accumulate cellular debris, desquamated keratin, and bacteria, predisposing to acute tonsillitis.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nHassall's corpuscles = THYMIC MEDULLA! Lymph node: Germinal Centers in cortex = B-CELLS; Paracortex = T-CELLS (contains High Endothelial Venules); Medullary cords = PLASMA CELLS. Spleen: PALS = T-cells around central arterioles; Red pulp = Cords of Billroth (filters old RBCs). Palatine tonsils have deep crypts.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Thymus = HASSALL'S CORPUSCLES (in medulla)! T-cell maturation! Shrinks with age.\n* Lymph Node Zones:\n  - Germinal center / Follicle = B-CELLS\n  - Paracortex = T-CELLS (with HEV blood vessels)\n  - Medullary cords = PLASMA CELLS (antibodies)\n* Spleen Red Pulp = Cords of Billroth (destroys old red blood cells)!\n* Palatine Tonsil = Deep crypts lined by stratified squamous epithelium!",
    "summary": "Histology of Lymphoid Organs & Tissues:\n- Primary vs Secondary Lymphoid Organs:\n  * Primary (Site of lymphocyte maturation and antigen-independent differentiation): BONE MARROW (B-cell maturation) and THYMUS (T-cell maturation).\n  * Secondary (Site of antigen presentation and lymphocyte activation): Lymph Nodes, Spleen, Tonsils, MALT/Peyer's patches.\n- Histology of Primary Organs:\n  * Thymus:\n    - Bilobed organ in superior mediastinum; lobules divided into outer Cortex (dense immature T-cells undergoing positive selection) and inner Medulla (mature T-cells undergoing negative selection).\n    - HASSALL'S CORPUSCLES (Thymic Corpuscles): Concentrically arranged, keratinized epithelial reticular cells located exclusively in the MEDULLA of the thymus.\n    - Lacks lymphoid follicles and germinal centers; undergoes involution (fat replacement) after puberty.\n- Histology of Secondary Organs:\n  1. Lymph Node (Filters LYMPH):\n     * Capsule -> Subcapsular sinus -> Trabecular sinus -> Medullary sinus.\n     * Cortex: Outer Cortex contains lymphoid follicles with GERMINAL CENTERS (rich in proliferating B-LYMPHOCYTES, follicular dendritic cells, tangible body macrophages).\n     * Paracortex: T-CELL ZONE; houses High Endothelial Venules (HEVs) for lymphocyte homing from bloodstream; expands in viral cell-mediated reactions.\n     * Medulla: Medullary Cords (contain Plasma Cells producing antibodies) and Medullary Sinuses (macrophages).\n  2. Spleen (Filters BLOOD; Largest lymphoid organ):\n     * White Pulp (25%): Immune component; Periarteriolar Lymphoid Sheaths (PALS - T-cell zone surrounding central arterioles) and Splenic Nodules (B-cell follicles).\n     * Red Pulp (75%): Splenic Cords of Billroth (macrophages, plasma cells) and Splenic Sinusoids (stave endothelial cells); filters aged, damaged RBCs and stores platelets.\n  3. Waldeyer's Tonsillar Ring (Oral & Pharyngeal Gateway Protection):\n     * Palatine Tonsils: Lined by Stratified Squamous Non-Keratinized epithelium; 10-20 deep, branched TONSILLAR CRYPTS; prone to tonsilloliths.\n     * Pharyngeal Tonsil (Adenoids): Lined by Pseudostratified Ciliated Columnar epithelium; folds, no crypts.\n     * Lingual Tonsils: Base of tongue; stratified squamous, single crypt per nodule.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nIn oral infections and odontogenic abscesses, regional cervical lymph nodes enlarge rapidly (lymphadenopathy). This reflects massive B-cell blastogenesis inside cortical germinal centers and rapid recruitment of circulating lymphocytes through paracortical High Endothelial Venules (HEVs). The palatine tonsils guard the oropharynx, but their deep, blind-ending epithelial crypts accumulate cellular debris, desquamated keratin, and bacteria, predisposing to acute tonsillitis.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nHassall's corpuscles = THYMIC MEDULLA! Lymph node: Germinal Centers in cortex = B-CELLS; Paracortex = T-CELLS (contains High Endothelial Venules); Medullary cords = PLASMA CELLS. Spleen: PALS = T-cells around central arterioles; Red pulp = Cords of Billroth (filters old RBCs). Palatine tonsils have deep crypts.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Thymus = HASSALL'S CORPUSCLES (in medulla)! T-cell maturation! Shrinks with age.\n* Lymph Node Zones:\n  - Germinal center / Follicle = B-CELLS\n  - Paracortex = T-CELLS (with HEV blood vessels)\n  - Medullary cords = PLASMA CELLS (antibodies)\n* Spleen Red Pulp = Cords of Billroth (destroys old red blood cells)!\n* Palatine Tonsil = Deep crypts lined by stratified squamous epithelium!",
