    "originalPrint": "Histology of the Skin (Integumentary System):\n- Layers of the Epidermis (Stratified Squamous Keratinized Epithelium - From Deep to Superficial):\n  1. STRATUM BASALE (Stratum Germinativum):\n     * Single layer of basophilic cuboidal/columnar stem cells resting on basement membrane; attached via hemidesmosomes.\n     * High mitotic activity; origin of all keratinocytes.\n     * Contains MELANOCYTES (synthesize melanin in melanosomes from tyrosine via tyrosinase; transfer pigment to keratinocytes via cytocrine secretion) and MERKEL CELLS (mechanoreceptors for light touch).\n  2. STRATUM SPINOSUM ('Prickle Cell Layer'):\n     * Several layers of polyhedral cells with spine-like cytoplasmic processes extending to neighboring cells.\n     * Held firmly together by abundant DESMOSOMES (spines represent tonofilaments converging on desmosomal plaques).\n     * Houses LANGERHANS CELLS (star-shaped dendritic APCs derived from bone marrow; contain tennis-racket shaped BIRBECK GRANULES; express CD1a and Langerin).\n  3. STRATUM GRANULOSUM:\n     * 3 to 5 layers of flattened cells containing conspicuous, coarse, non-membrane-bound KERATOHYALIN GRANULES (contain profilaggrin) and lamellar bodies (membrane-coating granules / Odland bodies secreting glycolipids for epidermal water barrier).\n     * Nuclei and organelles begin to degenerate.\n  4. STRATUM LUCIDUM:\n     * Thin, translucent, eosinophilic band of flattened dead cells containing Eleidin; nuclei and organelles completely absent.\n     * Found EXCLUSIVELY IN THICK SKIN (palms of hands and soles of feet).\n  5. STRATUM CORNEUM:\n     * Outermost layer of 15 to 30 layers of dead, flattened, enucleated squames packed entirely with mature KERATIN filaments; coated by extracellular hydrophobic lipid envelope.\n- Dermis & Appendages:\n  * Papillary Layer: Superficial loose connective tissue forming dermal papillae interdigitating with epidermal ridges (rete pegs); Meissner's corpuscles (light touch).\n  * Reticular Layer: Deeper, thick dense irregular connective tissue (Type I collagen & coarse elastic fibers); Pacinian corpuscles (deep pressure & vibration).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe epidermis achieves its barrier function through two complementary mechanisms: keratinization and lipid waterproofing. In the stratum granulosum, keratohyalin granules release filaggrin to bundle keratin filaments into dense cords, while lamellar bodies exocytose glycolipids into intercellular spaces. This creates an impenetrable hydrophobic lipid envelope that prevents transepidermal water loss and blocks microbial entry.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nEpidermal Layers Mnemonic (Deep to Superficial): Before Signing, Get Legal Counsel -> Basale, Spinosum, Granulosum, Lucidum, Corneum! Stratum Lucidum is found ONLY in THICK SKIN (palms and soles). Langerhans cells = BIRBECK GRANULES (tennis-racket shape). Melanocytes produce melanin from Tyrosine.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Layers of Skin (Deep -> Superficial):\n  - Basale (stem cells & melanocytes)\n  - Spinosum (desmosomes / prickles + Langerhans cells)\n  - Granulosum (keratohyalin granules)\n  - Lucidum (THICK SKIN ONLY - palms & soles!)\n  - Corneum (dead keratin squames)\n* Langerhans cells = BIRBECK GRANULES (look like tennis rackets)!",
    "summary": "Histology of the Skin (Integumentary System):\n- Layers of the Epidermis (Stratified Squamous Keratinized Epithelium - From Deep to Superficial):\n  1. STRATUM BASALE (Stratum Germinativum):\n     * Single layer of basophilic cuboidal/columnar stem cells resting on basement membrane; attached via hemidesmosomes.\n     * High mitotic activity; origin of all keratinocytes.\n     * Contains MELANOCYTES (synthesize melanin in melanosomes from tyrosine via tyrosinase; transfer pigment to keratinocytes via cytocrine secretion) and MERKEL CELLS (mechanoreceptors for light touch).\n  2. STRATUM SPINOSUM ('Prickle Cell Layer'):\n     * Several layers of polyhedral cells with spine-like cytoplasmic processes extending to neighboring cells.\n     * Held firmly together by abundant DESMOSOMES (spines represent tonofilaments converging on desmosomal plaques).\n     * Houses LANGERHANS CELLS (star-shaped dendritic APCs derived from bone marrow; contain tennis-racket shaped BIRBECK GRANULES; express CD1a and Langerin).\n  3. STRATUM GRANULOSUM:\n     * 3 to 5 layers of flattened cells containing conspicuous, coarse, non-membrane-bound KERATOHYALIN GRANULES (contain profilaggrin) and lamellar bodies (membrane-coating granules / Odland bodies secreting glycolipids for epidermal water barrier).\n     * Nuclei and organelles begin to degenerate.\n  4. STRATUM LUCIDUM:\n     * Thin, translucent, eosinophilic band of flattened dead cells containing Eleidin; nuclei and organelles completely absent.\n     * Found EXCLUSIVELY IN THICK SKIN (palms of hands and soles of feet).\n  5. STRATUM CORNEUM:\n     * Outermost layer of 15 to 30 layers of dead, flattened, enucleated squames packed entirely with mature KERATIN filaments; coated by extracellular hydrophobic lipid envelope.\n- Dermis & Appendages:\n  * Papillary Layer: Superficial loose connective tissue forming dermal papillae interdigitating with epidermal ridges (rete pegs); Meissner's corpuscles (light touch).\n  * Reticular Layer: Deeper, thick dense irregular connective tissue (Type I collagen & coarse elastic fibers); Pacinian corpuscles (deep pressure & vibration).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe epidermis achieves its barrier function through two complementary mechanisms: keratinization and lipid waterproofing. In the stratum granulosum, keratohyalin granules release filaggrin to bundle keratin filaments into dense cords, while lamellar bodies exocytose glycolipids into intercellular spaces. This creates an impenetrable hydrophobic lipid envelope that prevents transepidermal water loss and blocks microbial entry.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nEpidermal Layers Mnemonic (Deep to Superficial): Before Signing, Get Legal Counsel -> Basale, Spinosum, Granulosum, Lucidum, Corneum! Stratum Lucidum is found ONLY in THICK SKIN (palms and soles). Langerhans cells = BIRBECK GRANULES (tennis-racket shape). Melanocytes produce melanin from Tyrosine.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Layers of Skin (Deep -> Superficial):\n  - Basale (stem cells & melanocytes)\n  - Spinosum (desmosomes / prickles + Langerhans cells)\n  - Granulosum (keratohyalin granules)\n  - Lucidum (THICK SKIN ONLY - palms & soles!)\n  - Corneum (dead keratin squames)\n* Langerhans cells = BIRBECK GRANULES (look like tennis rackets)!",
