    "originalPrint": "Histology of Major Endocrine Glands:\n- Pituitary Gland (Hypophysis):\n  * Adenohypophysis (Anterior Pituitary - derived from Oral Ectoderm / Rathke's pouch):\n    - Pars Distalis contains Chromophils: Acidophils (Growth Hormone [GH], Prolactin [PRL]) and Basophils (TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH); and Chromophobes.\n  * Neurohypophysis (Posterior Pituitary - derived from Neuroectoderm / Diencephalon):\n    - Unmyelinated axons originating from hypothalamic supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei; Pituicytes (glial cells).\n    - HERRING BODIES: Dilated axonal terminals storing Oxytocin and Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH / Vasopressin).\n- Thyroid Gland:\n  * Composed of spherical THYROID FOLLICLES filled with gelatinous pink COLLOID (thyroglobulin).\n  * Follicular Cells (Simple cuboidal when active): Synthesize, iodinate, and secrete T3 (triiodothyronine) and T4 (thyroxine).\n  * Parafollicular Cells (C-Cells): Pale cells located between follicles; secrete CALCITONIN (lowers blood calcium by inhibiting osteoclasts).\n- Parathyroid Gland:\n  * Chief (Principal) Cells: Small polygonal cells with round central nucleus; secrete PARATHYROID HORMONE (PTH - raises blood calcium by activating osteoclasts via RANKL, increasing renal Ca2+ reabsorption and 1-alpha-hydroxylase vitamin D activation).\n  * Oxyphil Cells: Larger cells packed with mitochondria; appear at puberty, function unknown.\n- Adrenal (Suprarenal) Gland:\n  * Adrenal Cortex (Derived from Mesoderm - 3 distinct zones):\n    1. Zona Glomerulosa (Outer 15%): Cells in rounded clusters/arches; secretes Mineralocorticoids (ALDOSTERONE - regulates Na+/K+ balance via renin-angiotensin system).\n    2. Zona Fasciculata (Middle 75%): Cells arranged in straight vertical cords with abundant lipid droplets ('spongiocytes'); secretes Glucocorticoids (CORTISOL).\n    3. Zona Reticularis (Inner 10%): Branching anastomosing cords; secretes Androgens (DHEA).\n    * Mnemonic for Cortex layers and hormones: G-F-R -> Salt, Sugar, Sex!\n  * Adrenal Medulla (Derived from Neural Crest):\n    - CHROMAFFIN CELLS: Modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons; synthesize and release EPINEPHRINE (80%) and NOREPINEPHRINE (20%).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nCalcium homeostasis depends on the strict antagonism between Parathyroid Chief cells and Thyroid C-cells. A drop in ionized serum Ca2+ immediately triggers PTH release from chief cells, stimulating renal Ca2+ reabsorption, activating Vitamin D in PCT tubules, and upregulating osteoblastic RANKL to promote osteoclastic resorption. Calcitonin plays a rapid counter-regulatory role during hypercalcemic spikes.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nThyroid C-Cells = CALCITONIN (lowers Ca2+). Parathyroid Chief Cells = PTH (raises Ca2+). Adrenal Cortex Zones (GFR - Salt, Sugar, Sex):\n- Glomerulosa = Mineralocorticoids (Aldosterone)\n- Fasciculata = Glucocorticoids (Cortisol - spongiocytes)\n- Reticularis = Androgens (DHEA)\nAdrenal Medulla = Chromaffin cells (Epinephrine & Norepinephrine).",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Thyroid C-cells = Calcitonin (tone down calcium)!\n* Parathyroid Chief cells = PTH (Puts calcium into blood)!\n* Adrenal Cortex layers: G-F-R (Salt, Sugar, Sex):\n  - G = Glomerulosa = Aldosterone (Salt)\n  - F = Fasciculata = Cortisol (Sugar)\n  - R = Reticularis = Androgens (Sex)\n* Adrenal Medulla = Chromaffin cells = Epinephrine!",
    "summary": "Histology of Major Endocrine Glands:\n- Pituitary Gland (Hypophysis):\n  * Adenohypophysis (Anterior Pituitary - derived from Oral Ectoderm / Rathke's pouch):\n    - Pars Distalis contains Chromophils: Acidophils (Growth Hormone [GH], Prolactin [PRL]) and Basophils (TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH); and Chromophobes.\n  * Neurohypophysis (Posterior Pituitary - derived from Neuroectoderm / Diencephalon):\n    - Unmyelinated axons originating from hypothalamic supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei; Pituicytes (glial cells).\n    - HERRING BODIES: Dilated axonal terminals storing Oxytocin and Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH / Vasopressin).\n- Thyroid Gland:\n  * Composed of spherical THYROID FOLLICLES filled with gelatinous pink COLLOID (thyroglobulin).\n  * Follicular Cells (Simple cuboidal when active): Synthesize, iodinate, and secrete T3 (triiodothyronine) and T4 (thyroxine).\n  * Parafollicular Cells (C-Cells): Pale cells located between follicles; secrete CALCITONIN (lowers blood calcium by inhibiting osteoclasts).\n- Parathyroid Gland:\n  * Chief (Principal) Cells: Small polygonal cells with round central nucleus; secrete PARATHYROID HORMONE (PTH - raises blood calcium by activating osteoclasts via RANKL, increasing renal Ca2+ reabsorption and 1-alpha-hydroxylase vitamin D activation).\n  * Oxyphil Cells: Larger cells packed with mitochondria; appear at puberty, function unknown.\n- Adrenal (Suprarenal) Gland:\n  * Adrenal Cortex (Derived from Mesoderm - 3 distinct zones):\n    1. Zona Glomerulosa (Outer 15%): Cells in rounded clusters/arches; secretes Mineralocorticoids (ALDOSTERONE - regulates Na+/K+ balance via renin-angiotensin system).\n    2. Zona Fasciculata (Middle 75%): Cells arranged in straight vertical cords with abundant lipid droplets ('spongiocytes'); secretes Glucocorticoids (CORTISOL).\n    3. Zona Reticularis (Inner 10%): Branching anastomosing cords; secretes Androgens (DHEA).\n    * Mnemonic for Cortex layers and hormones: G-F-R -> Salt, Sugar, Sex!\n  * Adrenal Medulla (Derived from Neural Crest):\n    - CHROMAFFIN CELLS: Modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons; synthesize and release EPINEPHRINE (80%) and NOREPINEPHRINE (20%).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nCalcium homeostasis depends on the strict antagonism between Parathyroid Chief cells and Thyroid C-cells. A drop in ionized serum Ca2+ immediately triggers PTH release from chief cells, stimulating renal Ca2+ reabsorption, activating Vitamin D in PCT tubules, and upregulating osteoblastic RANKL to promote osteoclastic resorption. Calcitonin plays a rapid counter-regulatory role during hypercalcemic spikes.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nThyroid C-Cells = CALCITONIN (lowers Ca2+). Parathyroid Chief Cells = PTH (raises Ca2+). Adrenal Cortex Zones (GFR - Salt, Sugar, Sex):\n- Glomerulosa = Mineralocorticoids (Aldosterone)\n- Fasciculata = Glucocorticoids (Cortisol - spongiocytes)\n- Reticularis = Androgens (DHEA)\nAdrenal Medulla = Chromaffin cells (Epinephrine & Norepinephrine).\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* Thyroid C-cells = Calcitonin (tone down calcium)!\n* Parathyroid Chief cells = PTH (Puts calcium into blood)!\n* Adrenal Cortex layers: G-F-R (Salt, Sugar, Sex):\n  - G = Glomerulosa = Aldosterone (Salt)\n  - F = Fasciculata = Cortisol (Sugar)\n  - R = Reticularis = Androgens (Sex)\n* Adrenal Medulla = Chromaffin cells = Epinephrine!",
