    "originalPrint": "Renal Microscopic Anatomy & Comprehensive Histology Review:\n- The Nephron (Functional Filtration Unit of the Kidney):\n  1. Renal Corpuscle (Glomerulus + Bowman's Capsule):\n     * Glomerular Capillaries: Fenestrated endothelium without diaphragms.\n     * Glomerular Basement Membrane (GBM): Thick fused lamina densa (Type IV collagen, heparan sulfate proteoglycan giving strong negative charge barrier blocking albumin).\n     * Visceral Layer of Bowman's Capsule: Specialized PODOCYTES with primary and secondary interdigitating foot processes (pedicels) separated by FILTRATION SLITS bridged by nephrin.\n  2. Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT):\n     * Simple cuboidal epithelium with a dense, luxuriant LUMINAL BRUSH BORDER of microvilli; extensive basal invaginations with vertically packed mitochondria; indistinct lateral cell boundaries.\n     * Function: Reabsorbs ~65-70% of total glomerular filtrate, 100% of filtered glucose and amino acids (via SGLT2 cotransporters), and 85% of bicarbonate.\n  3. Loop of Henle:\n     * Thin descending limb (simple squamous, highly permeable to water); Thin ascending limb (impermeable to water, permeable to NaCl); Thick ascending limb (simple cuboidal, active Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter NKCC2; site of action of Loop Diuretics / Furosemide).\n  4. Distal Convoluted Tubule (DCT) & Juxtaglomerular Apparatus (JGA):\n     * DCT: Simple cuboidal epithelium; clean, sharp lumen WITHOUT brush border; reabsorbs Na+ and Cl- (Thiazide diuretics target NCCT cotransporter); regulated by aldosterone and parathyroid hormone.\n     * Juxtaglomerular Apparatus (JGA):\n       - Macula Densa: Densely clustered columnar cells in initial DCT monitoring tubular Na+ and Cl- concentration.\n       - Juxtaglomerular (JG) Cells: Modified smooth muscle cells in afferent arteriole; synthesize, store, and secrete RENIN.\n       - Lacis Cells (Extraglomerular Mesangial Cells).\n  5. Collecting Ducts:\n     * Principal Cells: Reabsorb Na+ and water, secrete K+ (regulated by Aldosterone and ADH / Aquaporin-2).\n     * Intercalated Cells: Alpha (secrete H+, reabsorb HCO3-) and Beta (secrete HCO3-).",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe renal filtration barrier relies on two sequential physical principles: size selectivity and charge selectivity. Pores in fenestrated capillaries (70-90 nm) and podocyte filtration slits (25-30 nm) block blood cells. Negatively charged polyanionic heparan sulfate in the glomerular basement membrane electrostatically repels circulating albumin. Loss of this negative charge barrier (as in Minimal Change Disease) causes massive selective proteinuria.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nPCT has a prominent BRUSH BORDER (microvilli) and reabsorbs 100% of glucose and amino acids! DCT has NO brush border. Podocytes have foot processes forming filtration slits. Juxtaglomerular cells in afferent arteriole produce RENIN. Macula densa monitors sodium/chloride in DCT. Loop diuretics inhibit NKCC2 in thick ascending limb.",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* PCT = Extensive BRUSH BORDER (microvilli)! Absorbs ALL glucose & amino acids!\n* DCT = NO brush border (clean lumen)!\n* Podocytes = Pedicels / foot processes forming filtration slits!\n* Juxtaglomerular cells = Make RENIN!\n* Macula densa = Sodium sensor in DCT!",
    "summary": "Renal Microscopic Anatomy & Comprehensive Histology Review:\n- The Nephron (Functional Filtration Unit of the Kidney):\n  1. Renal Corpuscle (Glomerulus + Bowman's Capsule):\n     * Glomerular Capillaries: Fenestrated endothelium without diaphragms.\n     * Glomerular Basement Membrane (GBM): Thick fused lamina densa (Type IV collagen, heparan sulfate proteoglycan giving strong negative charge barrier blocking albumin).\n     * Visceral Layer of Bowman's Capsule: Specialized PODOCYTES with primary and secondary interdigitating foot processes (pedicels) separated by FILTRATION SLITS bridged by nephrin.\n  2. Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT):\n     * Simple cuboidal epithelium with a dense, luxuriant LUMINAL BRUSH BORDER of microvilli; extensive basal invaginations with vertically packed mitochondria; indistinct lateral cell boundaries.\n     * Function: Reabsorbs ~65-70% of total glomerular filtrate, 100% of filtered glucose and amino acids (via SGLT2 cotransporters), and 85% of bicarbonate.\n  3. Loop of Henle:\n     * Thin descending limb (simple squamous, highly permeable to water); Thin ascending limb (impermeable to water, permeable to NaCl); Thick ascending limb (simple cuboidal, active Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter NKCC2; site of action of Loop Diuretics / Furosemide).\n  4. Distal Convoluted Tubule (DCT) & Juxtaglomerular Apparatus (JGA):\n     * DCT: Simple cuboidal epithelium; clean, sharp lumen WITHOUT brush border; reabsorbs Na+ and Cl- (Thiazide diuretics target NCCT cotransporter); regulated by aldosterone and parathyroid hormone.\n     * Juxtaglomerular Apparatus (JGA):\n       - Macula Densa: Densely clustered columnar cells in initial DCT monitoring tubular Na+ and Cl- concentration.\n       - Juxtaglomerular (JG) Cells: Modified smooth muscle cells in afferent arteriole; synthesize, store, and secrete RENIN.\n       - Lacis Cells (Extraglomerular Mesangial Cells).\n  5. Collecting Ducts:\n     * Principal Cells: Reabsorb Na+ and water, secrete K+ (regulated by Aldosterone and ADH / Aquaporin-2).\n     * Intercalated Cells: Alpha (secrete H+, reabsorb HCO3-) and Beta (secrete HCO3-).\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nThe renal filtration barrier relies on two sequential physical principles: size selectivity and charge selectivity. Pores in fenestrated capillaries (70-90 nm) and podocyte filtration slits (25-30 nm) block blood cells. Negatively charged polyanionic heparan sulfate in the glomerular basement membrane electrostatically repels circulating albumin. Loss of this negative charge barrier (as in Minimal Change Disease) causes massive selective proteinuria.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nPCT has a prominent BRUSH BORDER (microvilli) and reabsorbs 100% of glucose and amino acids! DCT has NO brush border. Podocytes have foot processes forming filtration slits. Juxtaglomerular cells in afferent arteriole produce RENIN. Macula densa monitors sodium/chloride in DCT. Loop diuretics inhibit NKCC2 in thick ascending limb.\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* PCT = Extensive BRUSH BORDER (microvilli)! Absorbs ALL glucose & amino acids!\n* DCT = NO brush border (clean lumen)!\n* Podocytes = Pedicels / foot processes forming filtration slits!\n* Juxtaglomerular cells = Make RENIN!\n* Macula densa = Sodium sensor in DCT!",
