    "originalPrint": "DMF High-Yield Visual Cheat Sheet 1:\nGeneral Embryology & Craniofacial Development\n- 3 Stages of Prenatal Development:\n  1. Pre-implantation Period (Weeks 0 - 2): Fertilization -> Zygote -> Morula (16 cells) -> Blastocyst -> Bilaminar embryonic disc (Epiblast & Hypoblast).\n  2. Embryonic Period (Weeks 3 - 8): Most critical period! GASTRULATION forms Trilaminar Disc: Ectoderm, Mesoderm, Endoderm; Organogenesis occurs; Highest susceptibility to TERATOGENS!\n  3. Fetal Period (Week 9 - Birth): Maturation, tissue refinement, and rapid physiological fetal growth.\n- Primary Germ Layer Derivatives:\n  * ECTODERM: Oral epithelium, Enamel of teeth, Epidermis of skin, Nervous system (CNS & PNS), Neural crest cells.\n  * NEURAL CREST CELLS ('The 4th Germ Layer'): Ectomesenchyme of head and neck -> Dentin, Pulp, Cementum, PDL, Alveolar bone, Craniofacial skeletal cartilages, Schwann cells, Melanocytes.\n  * MESODERM: Cardiovascular system, Skeletal muscles, Smooth/cardiac muscle, Bone, Cartilage, Kidneys, Gonads.\n  * ENDODERM: Lining of GI tract, Lining of respiratory tract, Liver, Pancreas, Thyroid follicular cells.\n- Pharyngeal (Branchial) Arches Summary Matrix:\n  * 1st ARCH (Mandibular Arch - Cranial Nerve V / Trigeminal):\n    - Skeletal / Cartilage: Meckel's cartilage -> Malleus, Incus, Sphenomandibular ligament; Mandible forms by intramembranous ossification around Meckel's.\n    - Muscles: Muscles of Mastication (Masseter, Temporalis, Medial & Lateral Pterygoid), Mylohyoid, Anterior belly of Digastric, Tensor tympani, Tensor veli palatini.\n  * 2nd ARCH (Hyoid Arch - Cranial Nerve VII / Facial):\n    - Skeletal / Cartilage: Reichert's cartilage -> Stapes, Styloid process, Stylohyoid ligament, Lesser horn and superior body of Hyoid bone.\n    - Muscles: Muscles of Facial Expression, Posterior belly of Digastric, Stylohyoid, Stapedius.\n  * 3rd ARCH (Cranial Nerve IX / Glossopharyngeal):\n    - Skeletal: Greater horn and inferior body of Hyoid bone.\n    - Muscle: Stylopharyngeus muscle.\n  * 4th & 6th ARCHES (Cranial Nerve X / Vagus & Superior/Recurrent Laryngeal):\n    - Skeletal: Thyroid, Cricoid, Arytenoid, Corniculate, Cuneiform laryngeal cartilages.\n    - Muscles: Pharyngeal constrictors, Cricothyroid (4th / Superior laryngeal), Intrinsic laryngeal muscles (6th / Recurrent laryngeal), Levator veli palatini.",
    "aiUnderstanding": "AI Conceptual Breakdown:\nCraniofacial development is dominated by the cranial neural crest. Unlike the trunk where skeletal elements originate from mesoderm, the facial bones, dentin, pulp, cementum, and periodontal ligament are derived from Neural Crest Ectomesenchyme. Understanding which pharyngeal arch gives rise to specific structures explains motor nerve innervation: every muscle derived from Arch 1 is innervated by CN V3; every muscle from Arch 2 is innervated by CN VII.",
    "boardPearls": "PRC Board Pearl:\nMeckel's cartilage (1st Arch) = Malleus and Incus (Mandible forms AROUND Meckel's, not from it!). Reichert's cartilage (2nd Arch) = Stapes, Styloid process, Lesser horn of hyoid. 1st Arch = CN V; 2nd Arch = CN VII; 3rd Arch = CN IX; 4th/6th Arch = CN X. Enamel is Ectodermal; Dentin/Pulp/Cementum/PDL are Neural Crest Ectomesenchymal!",
    "leaneNotes": "Dr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* 1st Arch = CN V (Trigeminal) -> Meckel's -> Malleus & Incus! Muscles of Mastication!\n* 2nd Arch = CN VII (Facial) -> Reichert's -> Stapes & Styloid! Muscles of Facial Expression!\n* 3rd Arch = CN IX -> Stylopharyngeus + Greater horn of hyoid!\n* 4th/6th Arch = CN X (Vagus) -> Laryngeal cartilages & muscles!\n* ENAMEL = ECTODERM only! Dentin, Pulp, Cementum, PDL = ECTOMESENCHYME (Neural Crest)!",
    "summary": "DMF High-Yield Visual Cheat Sheet 1:\nGeneral Embryology & Craniofacial Development\n- 3 Stages of Prenatal Development:\n  1. Pre-implantation Period (Weeks 0 - 2): Fertilization -> Zygote -> Morula (16 cells) -> Blastocyst -> Bilaminar embryonic disc (Epiblast & Hypoblast).\n  2. Embryonic Period (Weeks 3 - 8): Most critical period! GASTRULATION forms Trilaminar Disc: Ectoderm, Mesoderm, Endoderm; Organogenesis occurs; Highest susceptibility to TERATOGENS!\n  3. Fetal Period (Week 9 - Birth): Maturation, tissue refinement, and rapid physiological fetal growth.\n- Primary Germ Layer Derivatives:\n  * ECTODERM: Oral epithelium, Enamel of teeth, Epidermis of skin, Nervous system (CNS & PNS), Neural crest cells.\n  * NEURAL CREST CELLS ('The 4th Germ Layer'): Ectomesenchyme of head and neck -> Dentin, Pulp, Cementum, PDL, Alveolar bone, Craniofacial skeletal cartilages, Schwann cells, Melanocytes.\n  * MESODERM: Cardiovascular system, Skeletal muscles, Smooth/cardiac muscle, Bone, Cartilage, Kidneys, Gonads.\n  * ENDODERM: Lining of GI tract, Lining of respiratory tract, Liver, Pancreas, Thyroid follicular cells.\n- Pharyngeal (Branchial) Arches Summary Matrix:\n  * 1st ARCH (Mandibular Arch - Cranial Nerve V / Trigeminal):\n    - Skeletal / Cartilage: Meckel's cartilage -> Malleus, Incus, Sphenomandibular ligament; Mandible forms by intramembranous ossification around Meckel's.\n    - Muscles: Muscles of Mastication (Masseter, Temporalis, Medial & Lateral Pterygoid), Mylohyoid, Anterior belly of Digastric, Tensor tympani, Tensor veli palatini.\n  * 2nd ARCH (Hyoid Arch - Cranial Nerve VII / Facial):\n    - Skeletal / Cartilage: Reichert's cartilage -> Stapes, Styloid process, Stylohyoid ligament, Lesser horn and superior body of Hyoid bone.\n    - Muscles: Muscles of Facial Expression, Posterior belly of Digastric, Stylohyoid, Stapedius.\n  * 3rd ARCH (Cranial Nerve IX / Glossopharyngeal):\n    - Skeletal: Greater horn and inferior body of Hyoid bone.\n    - Muscle: Stylopharyngeus muscle.\n  * 4th & 6th ARCHES (Cranial Nerve X / Vagus & Superior/Recurrent Laryngeal):\n    - Skeletal: Thyroid, Cricoid, Arytenoid, Corniculate, Cuneiform laryngeal cartilages.\n    - Muscles: Pharyngeal constrictors, Cricothyroid (4th / Superior laryngeal), Intrinsic laryngeal muscles (6th / Recurrent laryngeal), Levator veli palatini.\n\nAI Conceptual Breakdown:\nCraniofacial development is dominated by the cranial neural crest. Unlike the trunk where skeletal elements originate from mesoderm, the facial bones, dentin, pulp, cementum, and periodontal ligament are derived from Neural Crest Ectomesenchyme. Understanding which pharyngeal arch gives rise to specific structures explains motor nerve innervation: every muscle derived from Arch 1 is innervated by CN V3; every muscle from Arch 2 is innervated by CN VII.\n\nPRC Board Pearl:\nMeckel's cartilage (1st Arch) = Malleus and Incus (Mandible forms AROUND Meckel's, not from it!). Reichert's cartilage (2nd Arch) = Stapes, Styloid process, Lesser horn of hyoid. 1st Arch = CN V; 2nd Arch = CN VII; 3rd Arch = CN IX; 4th/6th Arch = CN X. Enamel is Ectodermal; Dentin/Pulp/Cementum/PDL are Neural Crest Ectomesenchymal!\n\nDr. Leane's Handwritten Pearls:\n* 1st Arch = CN V (Trigeminal) -> Meckel's -> Malleus & Incus! Muscles of Mastication!\n* 2nd Arch = CN VII (Facial) -> Reichert's -> Stapes & Styloid! Muscles of Facial Expression!\n* 3rd Arch = CN IX -> Stylopharyngeus + Greater horn of hyoid!\n* 4th/6th Arch = CN X (Vagus) -> Laryngeal cartilages & muscles!\n* ENAMEL = ECTODERM only! Dentin, Pulp, Cementum, PDL = ECTOMESENCHYME (Neural Crest)!",
